// Generated by scripts/gen-l10n.mjs (gdetail n1, en) — 不要手改;改翻譯請改 scripts/out/l10n/en/ 再重 build。
window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N=window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N||{};window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]=Object.assign(window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]||{},{"n1-8":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ならでは」means <b>\"only ~ could have this\"</b> or <b>\"that's ~ for you\"</b>. Think \"scenery you only find in Kyoto\" or \"the warmth only handmade things have.\" You use it to stress that <b>this quality isn't found anywhere else, only here</b>, and it's usually praise.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Attach it directly to a <b>noun</b>, and most often follow it with 「の＋noun」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>職人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>職人ならではの技術</span></span> (skill that only a craftsman has)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>地元</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>地元ならではの料理</span></span> (food you can only get locally)<br>The noun in front is usually something with its own character: <b>a person, a place, a shop, or a particular way of doing things</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll see most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ならではの＋noun</span> is the most common; about 90% of uses look like this.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもならではの自由な発想に驚いた。</span> (I was amazed by the free thinking only children have.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>旅館ならではのおもてなしを楽しんだ。</span> (I enjoyed the hospitality you only get at a Japanese inn.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ならではだ</span> at the end of a sentence means \"this is exactly what only ~ can do.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この味は、老舗ならではだ。</span> (This flavor is something only a long-established shop can produce.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ならでは〜ない</span> is a more formal, old-fashioned pattern meaning \"you can't ~ unless it's ~.\" Just learn to recognize it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この味は、この店ならでは味わえない。</span> (You can't taste this flavor anywhere but this shop.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The 「なら」 here is <b>not \"if\"</b>. Don't translate it as \"if it's ~.\"<br>・The 「は」 in 「では」 is pronounced <b>わ</b>, so the whole thing is read ならでわ.<br>・Attach it directly to the noun, <b>with no extra の</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>日本ならではの</span>, not 日本のならではの.<br>・The point is that <b>only this has it</b>. You can't use it for ordinary traits everyone shares. For example, \"convenience stores you only find in Tokyo\" would sound odd, because convenience stores are everywhere.<br>・It's mostly used for praise. You'll occasionally see neutral uses like 「<span class='gd-jp'>都会ならではの悩み</span>」 (worries unique to city life), but using it for plain flaws or failures sounds strange.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>らしい</b> = feels very much like ~, typical of ~ (similar things may exist elsewhere):<br><span class='gd-jp'>京都らしい景色だ。</span> (It's a very Kyoto-like view.)<br><b>ならでは</b> = only there, can't be found elsewhere:<br><span class='gd-jp'>京都ならではの景色だ。</span> (It's a view you can only see in Kyoto.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>をおいて</b> = about <b>choosing a person</b>: there's no one else to pick besides them:<br><span class='gd-jp'>この役ができるのは、彼をおいてほかにいない。</span> (There's no one but him who can play this role.)<br><b>ならでは</b> = about <b>a distinctive quality</b>: only he can do this kind of thing:<br><span class='gd-jp'>この演技は彼ならではだ。</span> (This kind of acting is something only he can do.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use らしい for \"typical of ~\"; use ならでは for \"a quality only ~ has\"; use をおいてほかにない for \"if we're choosing, only him.\"</div>","n1-33":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とはいうものの」means <b>\"I know that's what they say, but...\"</b>. First you admit that something is true, then you add that reality didn't go that way. Think \"It's supposedly spring, but it's still so cold.\"<br>Breaking it down helps you remember: <span class='gd-jp'>と</span> (that) + <span class='gd-jp'>いう</span> (say) + <span class='gd-jp'>ものの</span> (although), which together mean \"although it's said that ~.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In the middle of a sentence</span> A とはいうものの、B. A is a fact, or something everyone says; B is \"you'd expect it to be this way, but it isn't.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>春とはいうものの、まだ寒い日が続いている。</span> (Although it's supposedly spring, the cold days keep going.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に十年住んでいるとはいうものの、敬語はまだ苦手だ。</span> (Even though I've lived in Japan for ten years, I'm still bad at honorifics.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>便利になったとはいうものの、失われたものも少なくない。</span> (Things have become more convenient, but we've lost quite a lot too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At the start of a sentence</span> After finishing one sentence, you can open the next with 「<span class='gd-jp'>とはいうものの</span>、」 to mean \"that said, ...\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今月から節約すると決めた。とはいうものの、コンビニに行くとつい買ってしまう。</span> (I decided to save money starting this month. That said, whenever I go into a convenience store I end up buying something.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Use the plain form before it, meaning the form without です・ます.<br>Verb: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>習った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>習ったとはいうものの</span></span><br>い-adjective: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安いとはいうものの</span></span><br>な-adjective: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利だとはいうものの</span></span>; you can also drop だ: <span class='gd-jp'>便利とはいうものの</span><br>Noun: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>春だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>春とはいうものの</span></span>; keeping だ is also fine: <span class='gd-jp'>春だとはいうものの</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What follows must be something that <b>goes against expectations</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>春とはいうものの、暖かい。</span> sounds odd: spring is naturally warm, so there's no contrast.<br>・The tone is formal and written, common in essays, news and speeches. With friends, 「<span class='gd-jp'>けど</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>とはいっても</span>」 sounds more natural; 「とはいうものの」 would sound like you're reading a script.<br>・What follows usually describes the situation or your own feelings. It rarely takes requests or invitations like \"please ~\" or \"let's ~.\"<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>いう</span> is actually pronounced 「ゆう」, but in writing it's still spelled いう, not ゆう.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation, \"It's supposedly spring, but it's still cold\":<br><b>とはいえ</b> (N2): <span class='gd-jp'>春とはいえ、まだ寒い。</span> Almost the same meaning, shorter, equally formal, and it can also open a sentence.<br><b>とはいうものの</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>春とはいうものの、まだ寒い。</span> Same meaning as とはいえ, just longer.<br><b>とはいっても</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>春とはいっても、まだ寒いね。</span> The version used most in speech.<br><b>ものの</b> (N2): <span class='gd-jp'>春になったものの、まだ寒い。</span> It attaches straight to a fact, without the \"it's said that ~\" layer.<br><b>とは言い条</b> (N1): <span class='gd-jp'>春とは言い条、まだ寒い。</span> Same meaning, but very archaic, and now you'll almost only see it in writing.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: in speech, use 「とはいっても」; in writing, use 「とはいえ」 or 「とはいうものの」; 「とは言い条」 you only need to recognize, not use yourself.</div>","n1-15":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜が早いか」 means <b>“the instant ~, immediately…”</b>, and it's so fast that <b>you can't tell which thing happened first</b>. Literally it asks, “Was (the first thing) faster?” The first action has barely started and the second one has already happened. Think of a school bell that has only just rung, and the students are already out in the hallway.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing a scene you see</span>Use it for things that have <b>already happened</b>, so the sentence usually ends in the past tense. It's common in novels, articles and news writing, and you'll almost never hear it in casual conversation.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>チャイムが鳴るが早いか、子供たちは教室を飛び出した。</span>(The moment the bell rang, the children rushed out of the classroom.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は家に帰るが早いか、ベッドに倒れ込んだ。</span>(The second he got home, he collapsed onto the bed.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新商品は発売されるが早いか、売り切れてしまった。</span>(The new product sold out the instant it went on sale.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「が早いか」 directly to the dictionary form of the verb (the base form you find in a dictionary). No conjugation needed.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>鳴る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>鳴るが早いか</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰るが早いか</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>発売される</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>発売されるが早いか</span></span><br>Even when the whole sentence is about the past, the verb before it stays in the dictionary form, and the tense is left to the end of the sentence. You'll occasionally see the た form, as in <span class='gd-jp'>言ったが早いか</span>, but just remember the dictionary form for now. That's enough.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It <b>can't be followed by a command, a request, or your own intention</b>: ×<span class='gd-jp'>家に着くが早いか、電話してください。</span> To say “Call me as soon as you get home,” use <span class='gd-jp'>家に着いたら、すぐ電話してください。</span><br>・English “as soon as” and “whenever” cover a lot of ground. A <b>general rule or condition</b> like “When spring comes, the flowers bloom” can't use が早いか. That's <span class='gd-jp'>春になると、花が咲く。</span><br>・The tone is stiff. To a friend you'd just say <span class='gd-jp'>家に帰って、すぐ寝ちゃった。</span> (“I got home and went straight to sleep”). Using が早いか would sound like you're reading from a novel.<br>・Don't put ます before it: ×<span class='gd-jp'>鳴りますが早いか</span>.<br>・「早い」 is read <b>はやい</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>In the same scene, all three versions are correct:<br><span class='gd-jp'>息子は帰ってくるが早いか、かばんを置いて遊びに行った。</span><br><span class='gd-jp'>息子は帰ってくるなり、かばんを置いて遊びに行った。</span><br><span class='gd-jp'>息子は帰ってくるや否や、かばんを置いて遊びに行った。</span><br>(The moment my son got back, he dropped his bag and went out to play.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>なり</b>: The actions before and after must be done by <b>the same person</b>, and the second one is often a slightly surprising move. So “The bell rang and the students rushed out” can't use なり: ×<span class='gd-jp'>チャイムが鳴るなり、生徒が飛び出した。</span><br><b>や否や</b>: The two parts can involve different people or things. The tone is the <b>most written and most formal</b>, and news reports love it: <span class='gd-jp'>ドアが開くや否や、客が店内になだれ込んだ。</span> (The moment the door opened, customers flooded into the store.)<br><b>が早いか</b>: The subjects can also differ, and the focus is on <b>being so fast you can't tell the order</b>. It often paints a picture of someone eager, rushing to do something.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: same person and a somewhat unexpected follow-up action, use なり. To stress “almost simultaneous,” use が早いか. For formal written reporting, use や否や. None of the three can be followed by a command, a request, or your own intention.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜そばから」 also looks like “as soon as ~,” but it describes something <b>repeated again and again</b>, “no sooner have I done ~ than…”: <span class='gd-jp'>覚えるそばから忘れてしまう。</span> (No sooner do I memorize it than I forget it.) It means “this happens every time,” not the speed of a single event.</div>","n1-16":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜や否や」 means <b>“as soon as ~, …”</b>, with the speed of <b>one thing finishing and the next happening right away</b>. It's a very formal <b>written expression</b>, common in novels, news and essays. You can think of it as the written version of <span class='gd-jp'>〜たとたん</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Understand this word first</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's read <b>やいなや</b>. 「否」 means “no / not.” The original sense of the whole phrase is “it's not even clear yet whether it was done or not,” in other words <b>the first action has only just happened and the next one follows right on its heels</b>. Keep that picture in mind and you'll remember that it stresses speed.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb dictionary form + や否や<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>目を覚ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>目を覚ますや否や</span></span>(as soon as someone opens their eyes, ~)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ドアが開く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ドアが開くや否や</span></span>(as soon as the door opens, ~)<br>In writing you'll sometimes see just 「や」, with the same meaning: <span class='gd-jp'>家に着くや</span> (as soon as someone gets home, ~).</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing something that already happened</span>Use it when telling a story or reporting that “A happened, and B happened immediately.” The end of the sentence is usually in the past tense.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ドアが開くや否や、子どもたちが飛び出してきた。</span>(The moment the door opened, the children came rushing out.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新商品は発売されるや否や、売り切れた。</span>(The new product sold out as soon as it went on sale.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長が現れるや否や、会場は静まり返った。</span>(The moment the president appeared, the hall fell silent.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The two parts can involve <b>different people or things</b>. In the examples above, “the door opened” and “the children rushed out” have different subjects, and that's perfectly fine.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use the <b>dictionary form</b> before it, not the た form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>帰るや否や</span>, not 「帰ったや否や」. In English we say “as soon as he came back,” so many learners instinctively reach for the past tense.<br>・「否」 is read <b>いな</b>, not ひ.<br>・It can't be followed by a request or an intention like “I'll ~,” “Please ~,” or “Let's ~.”<br>　× 駅に着くや否や、電話してください。<br>　○ <span class='gd-jp'>駅に着いたら、すぐ電話してください。</span>(Please call me right away when you get to the station.)<br>・It's stiff and bookish. When chatting with a friend and saying “I fell asleep as soon as I got home,” just say <span class='gd-jp'>家に着いたとたん、寝ちゃった</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>帰ってすぐ寝た</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same situation, “As soon as he entered the room, he burst into tears”:<br><span class='gd-tag'>や否や</span><span class='gd-jp'>彼は部屋に入るや否や、泣き出した。</span><br>The most formal and written. The two parts can have different subjects.<br><span class='gd-tag'>なり</span><span class='gd-jp'>彼は部屋に入るなり、泣き出した。</span><br>Both parts must have <b>the same subject</b>, and it often carries a feeling of “why did he suddenly do that?”<br><span class='gd-tag'>が早いか</span><span class='gd-jp'>彼は部屋に入るが早いか、泣き出した。</span><br>Stresses “so fast it's almost simultaneous,” with a strong novel-like flavor. The た form can also appear before it.<br><span class='gd-tag'>たとたん</span><span class='gd-jp'>彼は部屋に入ったとたん、泣き出した。</span><br>The everyday version, usable in both speech and writing, and it follows the た form.</div><div class='gd-sec'>Two more are often confused with these:<br><b>次第</b> can be followed by a request or an intention: <span class='gd-jp'>着き次第、連絡します。</span> (I'll contact you as soon as I arrive.) This sentence doesn't work with や否や.<br><b>そばから</b> means “as soon as you do it, it's undone again, and this keeps repeating”: <span class='gd-jp'>覚えるそばから忘れてしまう。</span> (No sooner do I memorize it than I forget it, every time.) This isn't a one-time “as soon as ~.”</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to ask the other person to do something, use 「次第」. For wasted effort that keeps repeating, use 「そばから」. To describe a single event that already happened, use 「たとたん」 in conversation and 「や否や」 in writing.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜なり</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜が早いか</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜そばから</span> together. This “as soon as ~” group often shows up side by side in N1 exam questions.</div>","n1-40":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にひきかえ」 works like English <b>\"in contrast to ~\"</b> or <b>\"whereas ~\"</b>. You describe one side, then the completely opposite side. Usually one side is good and the other is not, and the speaker has feelings about it. For example: \"Look how hardworking his older brother is, <b>whereas</b> the younger one...\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b> attach directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>去年</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>去年にひきかえ</span></span><br>A <b>full clause</b> first needs 「の」 to turn it into a noun, then add にひきかえ:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>兄はよく勉強する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>兄がよく勉強するのにひきかえ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>姉は明るい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>姉が明るいのにひきかえ</span></span><br>Words like 「社交的」 need な before の: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>社交的</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>社交的なのにひきかえ</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A big gap between two sides</span>You set two people, two periods or two places side by side to stress how different they are.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>去年の猛暑にひきかえ、今年の夏は過ごしやすい。</span>(Compared with last year's scorching heat, this summer is much more comfortable.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>前の部長にひきかえ、今度の部長はとても話しやすい。</span>(Compared with the previous department head, the new one is very easy to talk to.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>兄が社交的なのにひきかえ、弟は人見知りだ。</span>(My older brother is outgoing, whereas my younger brother is shy around new people.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>それにひきかえ</span>Placed at the start of a sentence, it means \"in contrast...\" or \"by comparison...\". First you talk about how good someone else is, then you turn to yourself or your own family, often with a complaint or a self-deprecating tone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>隣の子は毎日勉強している。それにひきかえ、うちの子はゲームばかりだ。</span>(The kid next door studies every day. By contrast, my kid does nothing but play video games.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't drop the 「の」 when attaching a clause: say <span class='gd-jp'>社交的なのにひきかえ</span>, not 社交的にひきかえ. The 「のに」 here is 「の＋に」, not the \"even though...\" のに.<br>・It carries the speaker's judgment, so it is <b>not suited to comparing plain data</b>. For a sentence that just states numbers, like \"men are 30%, women are 50%\", にひきかえ would sound odd.<br>・It sounds written and formal. In everyday conversation, you're more likely to hear the sentence-initial <span class='gd-jp'>それにひきかえ</span>.<br>・Don't confuse it with <span class='gd-jp'>〜と引き換えに</span>, which means \"in exchange for ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>代金と引き換えに商品を渡す。</span> (Goods are handed over in exchange for payment.) The meaning is completely different.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same idea, an outgoing older brother and an introverted younger one:<br><b>に対して</b> = objectively lists how the two sides differ, with no emotion: <span class='gd-jp'>兄は社交的なのに対して、弟は内向的だ。</span> (My older brother is outgoing, while my younger brother is introverted.)<br><b>にひきかえ</b> = carries a feeling of \"what a difference!\" or dissatisfaction: <span class='gd-jp'>兄が社交的なのにひきかえ、弟は内向的だ。</span> (My older brother is so outgoing, yet my younger brother is so introverted.)<br><b>に比べて</b> = simply compares degree: <span class='gd-jp'>去年に比べて、今年は暑い。</span> (This year is hotter than last year.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if you feel \"look at him, and then look at you...\", use にひきかえ. If you're just neutrally saying two sides differ, use に対して. If you're just comparing who has more or less, use に比べて.</div>","n1-2":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜をもって」 is like the formal English <b>\"by ~\" / \"with ~\"</b> you see in official documents. One meaning is <b>\"do something by means of ~\"</b> (notify in writing, handle with sincerity). The other is <b>\"with ~ as the cutoff point\"</b> (as of today, up to today). You'll mostly see it in announcements, notices and ceremony speeches. It almost never shows up in casual conversation.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a noun in front, then add をもって: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書面</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書面をもって</span></span><br>The noun in front is usually formal or abstract, such as 書面, 誠意 or 拍手, or a time expression like 本日 or 3月.<br>To sound more polite when speaking, you say <span class='gd-jp'>をもちまして</span>, which means the same thing.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>By what means</span>\"Do something by means of ~\", like a formal version of 「で」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>合否は書面をもってお知らせします。</span>(We will notify you of the result in writing.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誠意をもって対応いたします。</span>(We will handle this with sincerity.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>盛大な拍手をもってお迎えください。</span>(Please welcome them with a big round of applause.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A time boundary</span>A certain point in time acts as the dividing line. Most often it means \"up to this day, then it ends\", but it can also mean \"takes effect from this day\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本日をもって、閉店いたします。</span>(This store will close as of today.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>以上をもちまして、本日の会議を終了いたします。</span>(With that, today's meeting is adjourned.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>4月1日をもって、社名を変更いたします。</span>(The company name will change effective April 1.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本日をもって閉店</span> means <b>the shop is still open today, and today is the last day</b>. It doesn't mean it's already closed today. Translating it as \"closed from today\" easily causes confusion.<br>・This is a very stiff, written expression, so don't use it in daily life. To tell a friend \"I write with a pen\", say <span class='gd-jp'>ペンで書く</span>. Saying ペンをもって書く would sound odd.<br>・It is a different word from <span class='gd-jp'>持って</span>, meaning \"holding\". <span class='gd-jp'>傘を持って出かける</span> really means going out with an umbrella in your hand. <span class='gd-jp'>書面をもって</span> means \"by way of a written document\", and nobody is holding anything. That's why the grammar form is usually written in hiragana.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>以上をもちまして</span> is the standard line for wrapping up an event or ceremony. When you hear it, you know it's about to end.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>で</b> = the everyday \"by/with ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>結果はメールで知らせるね。</span> (I'll let you know the result by email.)<br><b>をもって</b> = the formal \"by ~\" of official notices: <span class='gd-jp'>結果は書面をもってお知らせします。</span> (The result will be communicated in writing.)<br>The two sentences mean about the same. The difference is the setting: one is casual talk between people, the other is a formal notice from a company or school.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>を限りに</b> can also mean \"up to ~\", but it stresses <b>\"this is the last time, and I won't do it anymore\"</b>, often with a sense of personal resolve: <span class='gd-jp'>今日を限りにタバコをやめる。</span> (I'm quitting smoking, starting today.)<br><b>をもって</b> is more like a <b>formal announcement</b> from an organization or company. It can also mean \"takes effect from this day\", a use that を限りに doesn't have: <span class='gd-jp'>本日をもって受付を締め切ります。</span> (Applications close as of today.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for everyday \"by/with ~\", use で. For the \"by ~\" of announcements, notices and ceremonies, use をもって. For your own resolution, \"as of today I won't ~ anymore\", use を限りに.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>をもってしても</span> = \"even with ~...\", followed by something that can't be done: <span class='gd-jp'>最新の技術をもってしても、完全には防げない。</span> (Even with the latest technology, it can't be completely prevented.)</div>","n1-3":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜をものともせず」 means <b>\"undaunted by ~\" or \"in defiance of ~\"</b>. Put a difficulty, danger or obstacle before it, and what follows is \"and yet they did it anyway.\" You use it to praise someone as brave and determined. It has the tone of a news story like \"braving wind and rain, he stayed at his post.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + <span class='gd-jp'>をものともせず</span> (you can also add に: <span class='gd-jp'>をものともせずに</span>, same meaning)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>病気</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>病気をものともせず</span></span> (undaunted by illness)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>周囲の反対</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>周囲の反対をものともせず</span></span> (undaunted by opposition from those around them)<br>Breaking it down, <span class='gd-jp'>もの</span> is \"thing\" and <span class='gd-jp'>せず</span> is \"not doing,\" so literally it means \"not even treating it as a thing worth caring about.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>What goes before it: a difficulty</span>Things that stand in the way: illness, injury, bad weather, danger, failure, opposition, pressure.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は度重なる失敗をものともせず、挑戦し続けた。</span> (Undaunted by repeated failures, he kept taking on challenges.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>救助隊は激しい雨をものともせず、山へ向かった。</span> (Undaunted by the heavy rain, the rescue team headed up the mountain.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>What goes after it: what got done</span>This is usually something that actually happened, said with admiration or praise.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は周囲の反対をものともせず、自分の店を開いた。</span> (Ignoring the opposition around her, she opened her own shop.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その選手は怪我をものともせず、最後まで走り抜いた。</span> (Undaunted by his injury, that athlete ran all the way to the finish.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It <b>praises someone else</b> and is generally not used about yourself. <span class='gd-jp'>私は寒さをものともせず</span> sounds like bragging, which is odd.<br>・Don't follow it with \"please do ~\" or \"let's ~.\" Something like \"Don't be afraid of difficulties, let's do our best!\" doesn't work here. It is for <b>describing something already accomplished</b>.<br>・What comes before it must be an <b>obstacle</b>. You can't put in something good like \"support\" or \"an opportunity,\" because there's nothing to be undaunted by.<br>・The particle is <b>を</b>, not が.<br>・It's stiff, formal language for writing, news and speeches. You'll almost never hear it in chat with friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>をよそに</b> = <b>disregarding other people's worry or expectations</b> and doing your own thing. <span class='gd-jp'>心配</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>期待</span> often come before it, with a hint of \"not listening to advice.\"<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>親の心配をよそに、彼は一人で海外へ行った。</span> (Paying no heed to his parents' worry, he went abroad alone.)<br><b>をものともせず</b> = <b>not afraid of difficulty</b>; bravely pushing through. It is praise.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>言葉の壁をものともせず、彼は海外で成功した。</span> (Undaunted by the language barrier, he succeeded abroad.)<br>Your parents' worry isn't a hurdle to overcome, so <span class='gd-jp'>親の心配をものともせず</span> sounds unnatural.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にもかかわらず</b> = a plain \"even though ~, still ~.\" It carries no praise or blame and works for good and bad things alike.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨にもかかわらず、たくさんの人が来た。</span> (Despite the rain, a lot of people came.)<br>Switch to <span class='gd-jp'>雨をものともせず</span> only when you want to stress how impressive those people are.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if what comes before is a \"hurdle\" and you want to praise the person's courage, use をものともせず. If it's \"other people's feelings\" that the person ignores, use をよそに. If you simply mean \"despite,\" use にもかかわらず.</div>","n1-9":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AはおろかB」 means <b>\"let alone A, not even B\"</b> (\"never mind A, even B ~\"). A is the thing that goes without saying, so it's not worth mentioning. B is the more surprising thing, and it stresses how extreme the situation is. Watch the order: English usually says \"B, let alone A,\" while Japanese puts A first and B second. So the order is flipped compared with the English \"let alone.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Basically <b>noun + はおろか</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>漢字</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>漢字はおろか</span></span><br>If A is an action, first add 「こと」 after the verb to turn it into a noun: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>走る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>走ることはおろか</span></span><br>The second half usually pairs with <b>も、さえ、すら、まで</b>, which work like the English \"even.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to arrange A and B</div><div class='gd-sec'>How to decide: put in A the thing that is already taken for granted, and in B the more extreme, more surprising thing.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Negative sentences (most common)</span>A is the harder thing and B is the more basic thing. It only has force because \"not being able to do the hard thing is normal, but not even the easy thing\" is striking.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>漢字はおろか、ひらがなさえまだ読めない。</span> (I can't even read hiragana yet, let alone kanji.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しくて、休みはおろか、昼ご飯を食べる時間もない。</span> (I'm so busy I don't even have time to eat lunch, let alone take a day off.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>足をけがして、走ることはおろか、歩くこともできない。</span> (I hurt my leg, so I can't even walk, let alone run.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Affirmative sentences</span>The other way around: A is the more ordinary thing, and B is wider in scope or more impressive.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この歌は国内はおろか、海外でも人気がある。</span> (This song is popular even overseas, never mind at home.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Don't reverse the order: <span class='gd-jp'>ひらがなはおろか、漢字も読めない。</span> is illogical. If someone can't even read hiragana, of course they can't read kanji, so there's no point stressing it.<br>It works the same in negative and affirmative sentences: A is \"naturally so,\" and B is \"even this is so.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The second half <b>can't contain commands, requests or your own intentions</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>漢字はおろか、ひらがなも覚えなさい。</span> is wrong. Use <span class='gd-jp'>はもちろん</span> there instead.<br>・は is already built in, so don't add を or が before it. And it can't attach directly to a verb, so remember to add 「こと」 first.<br>・The おろか here has nothing to do with 「愚か」 (foolish). Don't read it as \"stupid.\"<br>・The tone is stiff and often carries a note of complaint or surprise, like \"how outrageous\" or \"how awful.\" It's mostly used in writing or fairly formal situations. In casual talk with friends, Japanese people more often say <span class='gd-jp'>どころか</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Compare them in the same situation:<br><b>はおろか</b> = stresses \"not even B\" with a tone of amazement or dissatisfaction; the second half can only be a statement: <span class='gd-jp'>漢字はおろか、ひらがなも書けない。</span> (I can't even write hiragana, let alone kanji.)<br><b>はもちろん</b> = a plain \"A of course, and B too\"; neutral in tone, and a request can follow: <span class='gd-jp'>漢字はもちろん、ひらがなもちゃんと書いてください。</span> (Kanji, of course, but please write hiragana properly too.)<br><b>どころか</b> = close in meaning to はおろか but more colloquial. It can also mean \"not only not A, but rather B\": <span class='gd-jp'>安いどころか、すごく高かった。</span> (Far from cheap, it was incredibly expensive.) はおろか doesn't have this \"on the contrary\" use.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to tell someone to do something, use はもちろん. When chatting with friends, use どころか. For writing, or when you want the \"it's so extreme that even this…\" feeling, use はおろか.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at <span class='gd-jp'>すら</span> (\"even ~\"), which often appears in the second half.</div>","n1-10":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ないまでも」 means <b>\"if not X, then at least Y\"</b>. First you set aside a high standard (a perfect score, every day, sounding like a native speaker). Then you state a <b>fallback, minimum</b> goal. It's the tone of telling yourself, \"I won't exercise every day, but at least once a week.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb ない-form + までも. The first half holds the \"high standard\" and the second half holds \"the least I'll accept\".<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>取れない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>取れないまでも</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ネイティブのようには話せないまでも、日常会話はこなしたい。</span>(Even if I can't speak like a native, I at least want to handle everyday conversation.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>In practice, the two fixed combinations below are more common. A noun goes directly before them, so I suggest memorizing each whole phrase:</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>とは言わないまでも</span> \"I'm not asking for X.\" It's often used to express hopes or demands.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日とは言わないまでも、週に一度は運動したい。</span>(I'm not saying every day, but I'd like to exercise at least once a week.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>とはいかないまでも</span> \"Although it didn't reach X.\" It's often used for results that already exist.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>満点とはいかないまでも、合格点は取れた。</span>(I didn't get a perfect score, but I at least got a passing mark.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What the second half looks like</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half is usually \"I want to…\", \"I should…\", \"I want the other person to…\", or a result you've already achieved. It's often paired with <b>「は」「くらいは」「せめて」</b> to stress \"at least this much\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>謝罪とは言わないまでも、せめて一言説明してほしい。</span>(I'm not asking for an apology, but I'd at least like a word of explanation.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The two halves must be <b>high and low on the same scale</b>: perfect score vs. passing, every day vs. once a week. The first is high and the second is low. If the second half is higher than the first, or the two things are unrelated, you can't use this pattern.<br>・The second half is <b>affirmative</b>: \"at least I did it / at least I want to do it.\" To say \"I didn't even reach the minimum,\" you need a different pattern (see はおろか below).<br>・Don't confuse it with 「〜までもない」: <span class='gd-jp'>言うまでもない</span> means \"it goes without saying.\" It attaches to the dictionary form and means \"there's no need to.\" <span class='gd-jp'>言わないまでも</span> means \"even if I don't go as far as saying it.\" One ない makes the meaning completely different.<br>・It feels fairly written and formal overall, though 「とは言わないまでも」 is also quite common in everyday conversation.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>The one most easily confused with it is <b>〜はおろか</b>. Both say \"the high standard wasn't met,\" but what follows points in opposite directions. Using the same exam example:<br><span class='gd-jp'>満点とはいかないまでも、合格点は取れた。</span>(I didn't get a perfect score, but I at least passed.) → The minimum <b>was met</b>, so it ends on a positive note.<br><span class='gd-jp'>満点はおろか、合格点も取れなかった。</span>(Never mind a perfect score, I didn't even pass.) → Even the minimum was <b>not met</b>, so it stresses the negative.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: \"Not the high one, <b>but the low one yes</b>\" → ないまでも. \"Not the high one, <b>and not even the low one</b>\" → はおろか.</div>","n1-22":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜べく」means <b>\"in order to…\"</b>, but it's written, formal language, much like \"with a view to\" or \"in an effort to\" in news reports or official documents. Its meaning is close to 「ために」, only stiffer and more formal, and you'll almost never use it in casual chat.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>News and reports</span> Explaining what goal someone acted for.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>真相を確かめるべく、記者は現地へ向かった。</span>(In order to find out the truth, the reporter headed to the site.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試験に合格すべく、毎日10時間勉強した。</span>(To pass the exam, I studied 10 hours every day.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Expressing resolve in formal settings</span> Speeches, press conferences and company announcements saying \"to do X, we will do our best.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皆様のご期待に応えるべく、全力を尽くします。</span>(To live up to your expectations, we will give it our all.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「べく」directly to the verb's <b>dictionary form</b> (the base form you'd look up):<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>確かめる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>確かめるべく</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>なる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なるべく</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>応える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>応えるべく</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>する</b> is special: both forms work, but <b>すべく</b> is more common:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>達成する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>達成すべく</span></span> (you can also say <span class='gd-jp'>達成するべく</span>)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It <b>can't follow a negative</b>: there's no such thing as 「遅刻しないべく」. To say \"in order not to…\", use <span class='gd-jp'>遅刻しないように</span>.<br>・What follows <b>can't be a request to someone else</b> (a request, command or invitation): <span class='gd-jp'>合格すべく、毎日勉強してください</span> is wrong. Change it to <span class='gd-jp'>合格するために、毎日勉強してください</span>.<br>・It <b>can't attach directly to a noun</b>: you can say <span class='gd-jp'>健康のために</span>, but there's no 「健康のべく」. To use べく you need a verb: <span class='gd-jp'>健康を維持すべく</span>.<br>・The subject of both halves is usually <b>the same person</b>, and the action is <b>something done on purpose</b>. Things people can't control, like rain, can't go before べく.<br>・Watch out for the adverb <span class='gd-jp'>なるべく</span> (as much as possible), which looks the same: <span class='gd-jp'>なるべく早く来てください</span> means \"Please come as early as you can,\" while <span class='gd-jp'>医者になるべく</span> is the one meaning \"in order to become a doctor.\" Check whether something comes before it and you can tell them apart.<br>・Saying 「〜べく」to a friend in chat sounds like reading an official notice aloud. In speech, use ために.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of these say \"I'm working hard in order to pass\":<br><b>ために</b> = the general form, fine in both speech and writing: <span class='gd-jp'>合格するために、毎日勉強している。</span> (I study every day in order to pass.)<br><b>べく</b> = written and formal: <span class='gd-jp'>合格すべく、毎日勉強した。</span> (I studied hard every day in an effort to pass.)<br><b>んがため（に）</b> = even older and stiffer, with a strong \"at all costs\" feel, and it attaches differently (<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>せんがため</span></span>): <span class='gd-jp'>合格せんがため、寝る間も惜しんで勉強した。</span> (To pass, I begrudged even the time for sleeping and studied like mad.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: use ために for everyday language, べく for news, speeches and formal writing, and んがため only when you want to stress \"I'll achieve this no matter what.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>・<span class='gd-jp'>べくして</span>: \"was bound to happen\", a different meaning from \"in order to\". <span class='gd-jp'>起こるべくして起こった事故だ。</span> (It was an accident waiting to happen.)<br>・The related forms <span class='gd-jp'>べき</span> (should) and <span class='gd-jp'>べからず</span> (must not) don't mean \"in order to\" either, so don't mix them up.</div>","n1-30":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜と思いきや」 means <b>\"I thought ~, but it turned out…\"</b>. First you state what you expected, then what actually happened, and the result is <b>the complete opposite</b> of the expectation. It carries a sense of surprise, like being proven wrong.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>晴れると思いきや、午後から急に雨が降り出した。</span>（I thought it would clear up, but in the afternoon it suddenly started raining）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the <b>plain form</b> (the form without ます or です) before it, then add 「と思いきや」 directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>合格した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>合格したと思いきや</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>簡単だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>簡単だと思いきや</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>かと思いきや</span>Very often a 「か」 is added in the middle, giving a feel of \"I wondered whether ~\". It's extremely common. Watch how you add 「か」:<br><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: add か directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>終わった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>終わったかと思いきや</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いかと思いきや</span></span><br><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: drop 「だ」 first, then add か.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>簡単だ</span> → drop だ → <span class='gd-jp'>簡単かと思いきや</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だ</span> → drop だ → <span class='gd-jp'>雨かと思いきや</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう帰ったかと思いきや、まだ会社にいた。</span>（I figured he had already gone home, but he was still at the office）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>高いかと思いきや、意外と安かった。</span>（I thought it would be expensive, but it was surprisingly cheap）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は怒るかと思いきや、笑って許してくれた。</span>（I thought he would get angry, but he smiled and forgave me）</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It mostly shows up in storytelling contexts like articles, news, TV narration and social media posts, with a touch of surprise or humor. Online, people sometimes write just 「〜と思いきや…」 and stop there, deliberately teasing the reader until the next line reveals the twist.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When adding か to nouns and な-adjectives, <b>don't keep だ</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>簡単かと思いきや</span>, not 「簡単だかと思いきや」.<br>・「思いきや」 is a fixed chunk and <b>can't be conjugated</b>. There's no such thing as 「思いましたきや」.<br>・Read it as <b>おもいきや</b>, and don't mix it up with <span class='gd-jp'>思い切り</span> (with full force, to one's heart's content) or <span class='gd-jp'>思い切って</span> (making up your mind and going for it). They look alike but are unrelated in meaning.<br>・The second half is a <b>fact that has already happened</b>, usually in the past tense. You can't follow it with an intention, command or invitation: 「雨だと思いきや、傘を持っていこう」 is wrong.<br>・The result must be the <b>opposite</b> of the expectation. If things turn out as you expected, you can't use it.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>In everyday conversation, <b>と思ったら</b> is more common. It can also mean \"I thought ~, but…\", and in that sense it's interchangeable with と思いきや, just more conversational:<br><span class='gd-jp'>簡単かと思ったら、意外と難しかった。</span>（I thought it would be easy, but it was surprisingly hard）<br>But と思ったら has another meaning: <b>\"no sooner had ~ than…\"</b>, describing things following each other quickly. と思いきや doesn't have this meaning:<br><span class='gd-jp'>子どもは泣いたと思ったら、もう笑っている。</span>（The kid was crying just a moment ago, and now he's already laughing）<br>Swapping in と思いきや here would be wrong, because the point is how fast things change, not an expectation being broken.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if it's \"expectation proven wrong\", both work. Use と思いきや in writing and と思ったら in conversation. If it's \"no sooner ~ than…\", only と思ったら works.</div>","n1-31":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ものを」 has the feel of <b>\"If only you had just said so!\"</b> Doing one simple thing would have been enough, but you didn't, and now things have gone wrong. It carries <b>regret, resentment and blame</b>, sometimes aimed at someone else, sometimes at yourself.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put an ordinary plain form (the dictionary-style form without ます) before it. It most often follows 「〜ばいい」 or 「〜ばよかった」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言えばいい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言えばいいものを</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞けばよかった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞けばよかったものを</span></span><br>な-adjectives connect with 「な」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利なものを</span></span>. You don't say 「便利だものを」.</div><div class='gd-h'>The three most common forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜ばいいものを</span>You could have just done that, but you didn't. Most often used to scold someone else.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>謝ればすむものを、意地を張るから話がややこしくなる。</span>（A simple apology would have settled it, but he's being stubborn, so it's getting complicated）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>黙っていればいいものを、余計な一言で彼を怒らせてしまった。</span>（All you had to do was keep quiet, but one needless remark made him angry）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜ば〜たものを</span>\"If you had ~, I would have ~\", but it didn't happen, and that's a pity.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>言ってくれれば手伝ったものを。</span>（If you had told me, I would have helped!）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう少し早く家を出ていれば間に合ったものを。</span>（If you had left home just a little earlier, you'd have made it）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Left hanging at the end</span>The follow-up \"but you didn't\" is swallowed and left unsaid, leaving only a sigh. The two examples just above end this way, and it's very common in TV dramas and novels.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't confuse it with もの (\"thing\") + を: in <span class='gd-jp'>おいしいものを食べた</span>, もの means \"thing\" and a verb follows right after. With 「〜ものを」, there is usually a comma after it, followed by \"but in the end…\", or nothing at all.<br>・It talks about something that has <b>already happened and can't be changed</b>, so you can't follow it with a request or command. You wouldn't say 「早く言えばいいものを、言ってください」.<br>・The tone is written-style and somewhat literary, with blame in it. In everyday conversation people mostly say 「〜ばよかったのに」. Scolding your boss or a customer with 「ものを」 would sound very rude.<br>・The second half is a sigh or complaint, not a plain statement of fact, so what comes before it is almost always a hypothetical like \"it would have been fine if…\".</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>のに</b>: The closest in meaning and the most common in everyday speech.<br><span class='gd-jp'>早く言えばよかったのに。</span>（You should have just said so）<br><b>ものを</b>: Also a complaint, but more formal and with stronger feeling.<br><span class='gd-jp'>早く言えばよかったものを。</span>（If only you had said so earlier, sigh）<br>Also, のに can simply state a fact meaning \"even though ~, …\" (<span class='gd-jp'>雨なのに出かけた。</span> He went out even though it was raining), whereas ものを is rarely used that way.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ものの</b> (N2): Just one letter different, it means \"although ~, …\". It calmly reports facts, with no intent to blame anyone.<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生に相談しようと思ったものの、言い出せなかった。</span>（I thought about talking to my teacher, but I couldn't bring myself to say it）<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生に相談すればよかったものを、一人で悩んでいた。</span>（Talking to the teacher would have been enough, but he agonized over it alone）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if 「〜ばいい／〜ばよかった」 comes before it and there's a \"I wish I had…\" sigh, it's ものを. If you're just saying \"although… but…\" in a flat way, it's ものの.</div>","n1-43":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とは」 is like English <b>\"I can't believe…\"</b> or <b>\"to think that…\"</b>. When something happens that you completely didn't expect, and you're shocked, moved, or even a bit stunned or let down, you put that thing right before とは. It's the same feeling as saying, \"Wait, he was the culprit?!\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Surprise・the unexpected</span>Things turned out completely differently from what you expected.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こんな所で君に会うとは。</span>(To think I'd run into you in a place like this)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>まさか一回で合格するとは思わなかった。</span>(I never thought I'd actually pass on the first try)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Stunned・disappointed</span>It's more than surprise. There's also a sense of \"how could this happen?\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大人がこんな簡単なルールも守れないとは、情けない。</span>(Adults who can't even follow a rule this simple. How embarrassing)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Trailing off</span>The 「驚いた」 or 「思わなかった」 that would follow is often dropped, leaving just the lingering \"to think that…\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼が犯人だったとは……。</span>(To think he was the culprit…)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Use the <b>plain form</b> (the form without です・ます) and add とは.<br>Verbs: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会うとは</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>負けた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>負けたとは</span></span><br>い-adjectives: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>こんなに高いとは</span></span><br>な-adjectives: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>簡単</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>こんなに簡単だとは</span></span><br>Nouns: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>犯人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>犯人だとは</span></span>. The だ is sometimes dropped, giving <span class='gd-jp'>犯人とは</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most often</div><div class='gd-sec'>It often pairs with <span class='gd-jp'>まさか</span> (\"never in a million years\") before it, and is often followed by <span class='gd-jp'>思わなかった</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>思いもよらなかった</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>夢にも思わなかった</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>信じられない</span>, or <span class='gd-jp'>驚きだ</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>自分が日本で働くことになるとは、夢にも思わなかった。</span>(I never even dreamed I'd end up working in Japan)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The は in とは is pronounced 「わ」, so the whole thing is read とわ.<br>・Use the plain form before it. The ます form doesn't work: <span class='gd-jp'>会いますとは</span> is wrong.<br>・You can't use とは for a mere hypothetical or something still uncertain (for example, 「もし合格するとは」 is wrong). But you can use it for news that hasn't happened yet but is already settled: <span class='gd-jp'>彼女が来月結婚するとは、驚いた。</span> (I'm surprised she's getting married next month)<br>・とは sounds fairly formal and bookish. In casual chat with friends, Japanese people more often say なんて. You can still use とは, but it sounds a bit exaggerated and dramatic.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>なんて</b>: It means the same \"to think that…\", just more colloquial.<br><span class='gd-jp'>こんな所で会うなんて！</span> (Can't believe we met here! Among friends)<br><span class='gd-jp'>こんな所でお会いするとは。</span> (What a surprise to meet you here. More formal)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>The とは meaning \"what is ~\"</b>: It's written the same way, but here it defines a word, followed by an explanation or a question. There's no surprise at all.<br><span class='gd-jp'>幸せとは何だろう。</span> (What is happiness, anyway?)<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼が幸せだったとは。</span> (To think he was happy)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>と思いきや</b>: It means \"I thought ~, but then…\", and what follows has to state <b>the actual outcome</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>晴れると思いきや、雨が降ってきた。</span> (I thought it would clear up, but it started raining)<br><span class='gd-jp'>こんなに雨が降るとは。</span> (To think it would rain this hard)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before とは is a whole event that surprised you and a reaction follows, it's \"to think that…\". If it's just one noun and what follows explains it, it's \"what is ~\". If you first state what you expected and then the real result, it's と思いきや.</div>","n1-47":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ともなると」 means <b>\"once you reach the level of ~, naturally…\"</b>. Once your status rises, you reach a certain age, or a special period arrives, things naturally differ from usual. In English you'd say \"Once you're a department head, of course the responsibility gets heavier,\" and in Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>部長ともなると、責任も重くなる。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Status・position</span>When you reach a higher position, certain situations come with it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長ともなると、毎日会議ばかりだ。</span>(Once you're a department head, it's nothing but meetings every day)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Age・life stage</span>When a person reaches a certain stage, they naturally change.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>高校生ともなると、親とあまり話さなくなる。</span>(Once they're in high school, they stop talking much with their parents)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Time of year</span>Whenever a special period arrives, it happens every time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年末ともなると、どこの店も人でいっぱいだ。</span>(Once the end of the year comes, every shop is packed with people)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Scale of an undertaking</span>When you take on something big and weighty, what follows grows in scale too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家を建てるともなると、決めることが山ほどある。</span>(When it comes to actually building a house, there are mountains of things to decide)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + ともなると: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>社長</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>社長ともなると</span></span><br>Verb dictionary form (the basic form you'd look up) + ともなると: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>留学する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>留学するともなると</span></span><br>What goes before it is usually a status, age, period, or undertaking that is <b>a step above the ordinary and carries weight</b>. What follows is a situation or judgment that <b>naturally</b> arises at that stage.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It carries the nuance of \"once you've reached that point.\" For a plain, general change, like \"flowers bloom when spring comes,\" <span class='gd-jp'>春になると</span> sounds more natural.<br>・What follows is usually a general situation or judgment, such as heavy responsibility, crowds, or lots to prepare. It rarely takes your own intentions (\"I'll ~\") or requests to others (\"please ~\").<br>・The とも here doesn't mean \"also\" or \"even.\" It's also completely unrelated to <span class='gd-jp'>ともなく</span> (\"without really ~ing\"), so don't mix them up just because both contain とも.<br>・It's formal and bookish. When chatting with friends, <span class='gd-jp'>部長になると</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>部長にもなると</span> sounds more relaxed.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ともなれば</b>: The meaning is almost the same, and in most cases you can swap them directly.<br><span class='gd-jp'>部長ともなれば、責任も重くなる。</span> (Once you're a department head, of course the responsibility gets heavier)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>とあって</b>: It says \"because of this special situation, ~ is happening right now,\" describing something you can actually see happening. <b>ともなると</b> states a general tendency: \"once you reach this point, it always happens.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>連休とあって、高速道路はどこも渋滞している。</span>(Because it's a long holiday weekend, the expressways are jammed everywhere right now)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>連休ともなると、高速道路はどこも渋滞する。</span>(Whenever a long holiday weekend comes, the expressways get jammed everywhere)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for \"this time, because of ~, so ~,\" use とあって. For \"once you reach ~, it naturally always ~,\" use ともなると.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>〜たる</span> also often appears with status, but it means \"since you hold this status, you should act the part\": <span class='gd-jp'>教師たる者、生徒の手本でなければならない。</span> (Anyone who is a teacher must be a role model for their students). ともなると describes the <b>natural result</b> that comes with a status, while たる describes the <b>expected conduct</b> of that status. Remember them side by side.</div>","n1-28":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようが／〜ようと」means <b>\"no matter what… I'll do it anyway.\"</b> Whatever happens in the first part, and whatever other people do, the conclusion in the second part doesn't change. It sounds <b>stiffer and stronger</b> than the ordinary「〜ても」, with an attitude of \"my mind is made up, and I'm not afraid.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>With question words</span>Put 「何・どこ・誰・どんなに・いくら」 before it to mean \"no matter what / where / who / how much…\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何を言われようが、気にしない。</span>(No matter what people say to me, I don't care.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どこへ行こうと、あなたの自由です。</span>(Wherever you go, that's your choice.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰が反対しようと、私はやる。</span>(No matter who objects, I'm going to do it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>On its own</span>With no question word, it means \"even if…, I'll still…\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ろうが行く。</span>(Even if it rains, I'm going anyway.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Two side by side</span>「AようがBようが」 = whether A or B; 「Aようが Aまいが」 = whether or not you do it. Here 「まい」 means \"not do\". Group 1 (godan) verbs take the dictionary form: <span class='gd-jp'>行くまい</span>; <span class='gd-jp'>する</span> is often said as <span class='gd-jp'>しまい</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ろうが風が吹こうが、試合は行われる。</span>(Rain or wind, the match will go ahead.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>参加しようがしまいが、あなたの自由だ。</span>(Whether you take part or not is up to you.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half very often has 「<span class='gd-jp'>自由だ</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>関係ない</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>構わない</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>気にしない</span>」, or a statement of determination like \"I'm doing it regardless.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: first turn the verb into the volitional form (the 「行こう」「食べよう」 shape), then add が or と.<br>Group 1 (godan): <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → swap く for こ → <span class='gd-jp'>行こうが</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言おうが</span></span><br>Group 2 (ichidan): drop る and add よう　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べようが</span></span>. Passive forms change the same way: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言われる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言われようが</span></span><br>Group 3 (する・来る): <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しようが</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ようが</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: drop the final 「い」 and add 「かろう」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暑い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暑かろうが</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高かろうと</span></span><br>「いい」 conjugates from 「よい」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よかろうが</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どんなに暑かろうと、毎朝のランニングは欠かさない。</span>(No matter how hot it is, I never skip my morning run.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: add 「だろう」 or the more formal 「であろう」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>不便だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>不便だろうが</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子ども</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子どもであろうと</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>相手が子どもだろうが、ルールはルールだ。</span>(Even if the other person is a child, rules are rules.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The volitional form here does <b>not</b> mean \"let's…\" or \"I'll…\". It just borrows the shape to mean \"no matter what.\"<br>・Group 1 verbs can't attach directly: it's <span class='gd-jp'>行こうが</span>, not 「行くようが」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来ようが</span> is read 「こようが」, not 「きようが」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>降ろうが</span> alone only means \"even if it rains\"; to say \"whether it rains or not,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>降ろうが降るまいが</span>.<br>・「ようが」 and 「ようと」 mean basically the same thing and are interchangeable.<br>・The tone is hard and forceful. In everyday chat, 「<span class='gd-jp'>暑くても</span>」 sounds more natural; the かろう form like 「<span class='gd-jp'>暑かろうが</span>」 is for writing or emphasis.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: no matter how much you hurry.<br><b>ても</b> = the plain \"even if…\", the most neutral: <span class='gd-jp'>いくら急いでも、間に合わない。</span>(No matter how much I hurry, I won't make it.)<br><b>ようが</b> = what follows is \"I'll do it anyway / it makes no difference,\" with a strong attitude: <span class='gd-jp'>いくら急がされようが、手は抜かない。</span>(No matter how much they rush me, I won't cut corners.)<br><b>たところで</b> = what follows is \"it's pointless / it won't turn out well\": <span class='gd-jp'>いくら急いだところで、もう間に合わない。</span>(No matter how much we hurry now, it's already too late.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, with nouns you can use 「<b>であれ</b>」 to say the same \"no matter what it is\": <span class='gd-jp'>理由が何であれ、遅刻はだめだ。</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>理由が何であろうと、遅刻はだめだ。</span> mean almost the same thing (whatever the reason, being late is not okay).</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if what follows is \"I'll do it anyway / do as you like / it doesn't matter,\" use ようが. If it's \"it's pointless even if you do,\" use たところで. For casual speech, or when you don't want to sound so blunt, use ても.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>For another \"whether A or B\" pattern, look at 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜にせよ〜にせよ</span>」: <span class='gd-jp'>行くにせよ行かないにせよ、連絡してください。</span>(Whether you go or not, please let me know.)</div>","n1-29":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AにせよBにせよ」 means <b>\"whether A or B, …\"</b> in English. You throw out two possibilities, then say: either way, it's the same, and the conclusion doesn't change. The tone is fairly formal and often appears in articles, news and meeting remarks.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the form without ます or です).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くにせよ行かないにせよ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いにせよ安いにせよ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: attach directly, and in the present tense <b>don't add だ</b>. For a more formal feel, you can add 「である」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大人にせよ子どもにせよ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>好きにせよ嫌いにせよ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生であるにせよ</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A pair of opposites</span>Go / don't go, agree / disagree: it's the same either way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>行くにせよ行かないにせよ、早めに連絡してください。</span>(Whether you go or not, please let me know early.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>好きにせよ嫌いにせよ、この仕事は誰かがやらなければならない。</span>(Whether you like it or not, someone has to do this job.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Two items of the same kind</span>You give two examples that stand for \"this whole category is the same.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大人にせよ子どもにせよ、ルールは守らなければならない。</span>(Whether adults or children, everyone has to follow the rules.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half is usually a judgment like \"should…\", \"must…\" or \"no difference\" that is <b>true for both sides</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Set expressions</span>No examples listed; one word covers every possibility. Very common in both speech and writing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いずれにせよ、結果が出たら連絡します。</span>(Either way, I'll contact you once the results are in.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何にせよ、無事でよかった。</span>(Anyway, I'm just glad you're safe.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Used only once</span>A single 「にせよ」 = \"even if…\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冗談にせよ、そんなことを言ってはいけない。</span>(Even as a joke, you shouldn't say something like that.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns and な-adjectives take no だ in the present tense: not 学生だにせよ, but <span class='gd-jp'>学生にせよ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>学生であるにせよ</span>.<br>・「せよ」 looks like a command (\"do it!\"), but here it's just a fixed expression with <b>no command meaning</b>.<br>・The second half must be true for both cases. If the conclusion fits only one side, you can't use this pattern.<br>・It's very bookish. Chatting with a friend, <span class='gd-jp'>行っても行かなくても</span> sounds more natural; にせよ can sound like you're reading an official document.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にしろ…にしろ</b>: exactly the same meaning, a little softer, and still usable in speech.<br><span class='gd-jp'>行くにしろ行かないにしろ、連絡してください。</span>(Whether you go or not, please contact me.)<br><b>であれ…であれ</b>: mainly used with nouns, and stiffer and more formal than にせよ.<br><span class='gd-jp'>大人であれ子どもであれ、ルールは守るべきだ。</span>(Adults and children alike should follow the rules.)<br><b>ようが…まいが</b>: carries a strong \"do what you like, it's all the same to me\" feeling.<br><span class='gd-jp'>行こうが行くまいが、私には関係ない。</span>(Whether you go or not has nothing to do with me.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for a calm \"whether A or B,\" use にせよ／にしろ. For nouns only and a more formal tone, use であれ. For a \"whatever, it won't change anyway\" attitude, use ようが.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>For \"regardless of the content, …\", learn <span class='gd-jp'>いかんにかかわらず</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>理由のいかんにかかわらず、遅刻は認めません。</span>(Whatever the reason, we do not accept lateness.)</div>","n1-67":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜をよそに」means <b>\"disregarding ~\"</b> or <b>\"paying no attention to ~\"</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>よそ</span> means \"someone else's home, somewhere else,\" so <span class='gd-jp'>〜をよそに</span> treats other people's worries or objections, or the surrounding situation, as <b>somebody else's business</b> while the subject carries on in their own way.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Ignoring other people's feelings</span>This is the most common use. Put other people's worry, opposition, expectations or criticism first, then say that the subject goes on doing their own thing anyway. It often carries a feeling of \"how could they?\", but it isn't necessarily a scolding. It can also be a neutral description.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親の心配をよそに、息子は毎晩遅くまで遊んでいる。</span>(Despite his parents' worry, their son plays out until late every night.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>周囲の反対をよそに、彼女は会社を辞めて留学した。</span>(Ignoring the objections of those around her, she quit her job and went to study abroad.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Unaffected by the surroundings</span>Put a situation that you would expect to have an effect, such as the economy or a public uproar, first. Then say that the subject wasn't affected at all.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>不景気をよそに、この店はいつも行列ができている。</span>(Despite the bad economy, this shop always has a line out the door.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>世間の騒ぎをよそに、本人はのんびり旅行を楽しんでいた。</span>(While the public was in an uproar, the person involved was calmly enjoying a trip.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It attaches directly to a <b>noun</b>: noun + をよそに<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>家族の心配</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>家族の心配をよそに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ファンの期待</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ファンの期待をよそに</span></span><br>Nouns that most often go with it: <span class='gd-jp'>心配</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>反対</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>期待</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>批判</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>不安</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>騒ぎ</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It needs a noun before it. Don't say 「心配するをよそに」. Change the verb to the noun <span class='gd-jp'>心配</span> first.<br>・What comes before it is <b>other people's reactions or the surrounding situation</b>, not something you yourself are supposed to do. For \"skipping homework to go play,\" don't say 「宿題をよそに」. <span class='gd-jp'>宿題そっちのけで遊ぶ</span> sounds more natural.<br>・It mostly describes <b>other people's</b> behavior. It's a written-style expression common in news and articles, and you'll rarely hear it in casual conversation with friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>をものともせず</b> = not letting <b>difficulties or danger</b> bother you, with a tone of praise:<br><span class='gd-jp'>けがをものともせず、彼は最後まで走った。</span>(Undeterred by his injury, he ran all the way to the end: admirable.)<br><b>をよそに</b> = ignoring <b>other people's feelings or the surrounding situation</b> and doing what you want:<br><span class='gd-jp'>家族の心配をよそに、彼は最後まで走った。</span>(Despite his family's worry, he ran all the way to the end: the point is that he ignored his family.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にもかかわらず</b> = a plain \"although ~, still ~\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨にもかかわらず、たくさんの人が来た。</span>(Although it was raining, a lot of people came.)<br>This sentence simply states \"it rained, yet many people came,\" so にもかかわらず is the most natural choice. The point of をよそに is that the subject shrugs off something they should have cared about or been affected by, and carries on in their own way. That's why it's mostly used with other people's worry or opposition, or with surrounding conditions like the economy that should have had an effect.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: overcoming difficulty and deserving admiration → をものともせず; ignoring what others think and being unaffected by the surroundings → をよそに; a plain \"although ~, still ~\" → にもかかわらず.</div>","n1-70":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ずにはおかない」means <b>\"is bound to make someone ~\"</b> or <b>\"won't rest until ~\"</b>. It has two uses. In one, something is so powerful that it <b>inevitably</b> produces a certain feeling in people. In the other, a person is determined and <b>won't stop until they've done it</b>. It's a hard-edged N1 expression, mostly found in film reviews, book reviews, news and novels.</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's easier to remember if you break it down: <span class='gd-jp'>おく</span> means \"to leave something be.\" So \"won't leave it alone without doing ~\" means \"will definitely do ~.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Something inevitably triggers a reaction</span>This is the most common use. The subject is a film, a work, a phrase or an event, and you say it is <b>bound to</b> move, surprise or make people think.<br>The person affected is marked with <b>を</b> or <b>に</b> (<span class='gd-jp'>人を～させる</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>人に～させる</span>), so the verb is usually in the <b>causative form</b>, the \"make someone ~\" form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>感動する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>感動させる</span></span> (to move someone emotionally).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この映画は、観客を感動させずにはおかない。</span>(This film is bound to move the audience.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この小説の結末は、読者を驚かせずにはおかないだろう。</span>(The ending of this novel is sure to astonish readers.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の言葉は、聞く人の心を動かさずにはおかない。</span>(His words are bound to touch the hearts of those who hear them.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Won't stop until it's done</span>The subject is a person, and it expresses strong determination, with a fierce \"I will make this happen\" feel.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今度こそ、真相を明らかにせずにはおかない。</span>(This time I won't rest until I've uncovered the truth.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あんな失礼なことを言われたんだ。必ず謝らせずにはおかない。</span>(He said something that rude to me. I'll make him apologize, no matter what.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First turn the verb into its ない form, then <b>drop the ない</b> and add <b>ずにはおかない</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>感動させる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>感動させない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>感動させずにはおかない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>動かす</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>動かさない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>動かさずにはおかない</span></span><br>Remember the irregular verbs specially: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>せずにはおかない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ずにはおかない</span>（こず）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Another form attaches <b>ではおかない</b> <b>directly</b> to the ない form. You don't drop the ない, and the meaning is the same:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>感動させない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>感動させないではおかない</span></span><br>「ずには」 is the more common one, and it sounds more formal and written.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・する becomes <b>せず</b>, not しず: <span class='gd-jp'>明らかにせずにはおかない</span>.<br>・In the first use, the person affected is the object marked with を／に, not the subject. <span class='gd-jp'>観客は感動せずにはおかない</span> is wrong. To say \"the film moves the audience,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>映画は観客を感動させずにはおかない</span>.<br>・The tone is heavy and very written. In everyday conversation, to say \"it'll definitely make you cry,\" just say <span class='gd-jp'>絶対泣けるよ</span>. Using ずにはおかない would sound like you're reading out a review.<br>・The second use expresses a \"do it at all costs\" determination, so don't use it for small things like \"finishing this bowl of ramen.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ずにはいられない</b> (N2) = <b>a person can't help themselves</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>この映画を見て、泣かずにはいられなかった。</span>(Watching this film, I couldn't help crying.)<br><b>ずにはおかない</b> = <b>something is bound to make people</b> react that way: <span class='gd-jp'>この映画は、見る人を泣かせずにはおかない。</span>(This film is bound to make viewers cry.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ずにはすまない</b> (n1-71) = given the reason or situation, <b>you can't get away without doing it</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>失礼なことを言ってしまったから、謝らずにはすまない。</span>(I said something rude, so I can't get away without apologizing.)<br><b>ずにはおかない</b> = I <b>insist on making it happen</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>失礼なことを言われたから、謝らせずにはおかない。</span>(He said something rude to me, so I'll make him apologize.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: \"can't help oneself\" is ずにはいられない; \"something is bound to make people ~\" or \"I must make it happen\" is ずにはおかない; \"can't get away without doing it\" is ずにはすまない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to study next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜ずにはすまない・ないではすまない</span> (n1-71). The two are often tested together, and comparing them side by side makes the difference clearest.</div>","n1-72":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜を余儀なくされる」 is the phrase the news uses for <b>“be forced to ~.”</b> Think “the event was forced to be canceled because of the typhoon.” It isn't something you chose. <b>The situation or the wider circumstances left you no other way out.</b> <span class='gd-jp'>余儀</span> means “another option,” so <span class='gd-jp'>余儀ない</span> means “there's no other option.”</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>News and reports</span>Big events such as natural disasters, pandemics or economic shocks that leave a person or organization with no choice but to do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大雨で、試合は中止を余儀なくされた。</span>(Because of the heavy rain, the match was forced to be canceled.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>地震で、多くの住民が避難生活を余儀なくされている。</span>(Because of the earthquake, many residents are forced to live in shelters.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing your own situation in formal settings</span>You'll also use it in reports, or when explaining your background in an interview.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会社の倒産で、退職を余儀なくされました。</span>(Because the company went bankrupt, I had no choice but to leave my job.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a <b>noun</b> before it, specifically a noun that expresses an action. The most common are <span class='gd-jp'>中止</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>延期</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>変更</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>撤退</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入院</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>移転</span>, words that can take する after them.<br>If you start from a verb, drop the する and keep the noun: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>延期する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>延期を余儀なくされた</span></span><br>The subject of the sentence (the part before は／が) is <b>the side that is affected and forced</b>: the match, the old shop, the residents, me.</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>余儀なくされた</span>Something that already happened. This is the most common form.<br><span class='gd-tag'>余儀なくされている</span>Being forced to do it right now, still ongoing, like the shelter life above.<br><span class='gd-tag'>余儀なくさせる</span>The reverse, from the side of the cause. The subject is often the event itself:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>円安が、計画の見直しを余儀なくさせた。</span>(The weak yen forced a rethink of the plan.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Read it <b>よぎなく</b>, not よぎむなく.<br>・You can't put a verb directly before it. Say <span class='gd-jp'>中止を余儀なくされた</span>, not 「中止するを余儀なくされた」.<br>・Don't use it for things you chose or wanted to do. If you changed jobs because you wanted a new challenge, you weren't “forced.”<br>・It's heavy and very formal, so using it for trivial matters sounds exaggerated. 「宿題を余儀なくされた」 sounds like a joke.<br>・English “be forced to” maps onto both させられる and を余儀なくされる, so many learners reach for させられる by instinct. The two mean different things, so see below.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take “leaving a job” as the example:<br><b>を余儀なくされる</b> = forced by <b>circumstances</b>. Nobody in particular is pushing you. Formal and mostly written:<br><span class='gd-jp'>会社の倒産で、退職を余儀なくされた。</span>(Because the company went bankrupt, I was forced to leave.)<br><b>させられる</b> = <b>a specific person</b> makes you do it, often with some resentment. Common in speech too:<br><span class='gd-jp'>上司に無理やり退職させられた。</span>(My boss forced me to quit.)<br><b>ざるを得ない</b> = <b>you yourself</b> weigh it up and conclude you have no choice. The focus is on your own judgment:<br><span class='gd-jp'>体調が悪くて、退職せざるを得なかった。</span>(I was in poor health, so I had no choice but to quit.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if a <i>person</i> is pushing you, use させられる. If you thought it over and had no choice, use ざるを得ない. If big circumstances like a disaster, the economy or the times left you no way out, and the setting is formal, use を余儀なくされる.</div>","n1-77":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜んばかりに」 means <b>“as if about to ~”</b> or <b>“looking like one is about to ~.”</b> It's an exaggerated way to describe how intense something is: <b>it's close to</b> doing that action, but usually <b>it hasn't actually done it</b>. Compare English “she looked like she was about to cry.” The point is how upset she was, not that she actually cried.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Turn the verb into its ない form, remove 「ない」, then add 「んばかり」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泣く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泣かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泣かんばかり</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言わんばかり</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>あふれる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>あふれない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>あふれんばかり</span></span><br><b>する is an exception</b>: it's not しんばかり, but the classical form <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>せんばかり</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>んばかりに + action</span>Describes <i>how</i> someone does an action.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は泣かんばかりに頼んできた。</span>(She begged me, almost in tears.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>犬が飛びつかんばかりに喜んで走ってきた。</span>(The dog came running, so happy it seemed about to jump on me.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>んばかりの + noun</span>Modifies the noun that follows.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会場は割れんばかりの拍手に包まれた。</span>(The hall was filled with deafening applause.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は「お前のせいだ」と言わんばかりの顔で私を見た。</span>(He looked at me as if to say “it's your fault.”)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>んばかりだ</span>Placed at the end of the sentence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の喜びようは、今にも踊り出さんばかりだった。</span>(He was so happy he looked ready to burst into dance.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Four combinations come up especially often: <span class='gd-jp'>言わんばかり</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>割れんばかり</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>あふれんばかり</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>泣かんばかり</span>. Memorize them as set phrases. The overall tone is written and literary, though 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と言わんばかりの顔</span>」 is also common in everyday speech.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The 「ん」 here is <b>not a negative</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>泣かん</span> looks like the Kansai-style “won't cry,” but in this grammar it's the classical ん meaning “about to ~.” <span class='gd-jp'>泣かんばかり</span> means “on the verge of tears,” not “not crying.”<br>・する becomes <b>せんばかり</b>. Writing しんばかり is wrong.<br>・It's an exaggerated description of <b>appearance or degree</b>. If you just want to say “looks like they're about to cry,” everyday conversation is fine with <span class='gd-jp'>泣きそうな顔</span>. Using <span class='gd-jp'>泣かんばかり</span> sounds very dramatic.<br>・It usually means “close to doing it,” not that it actually happened. <span class='gd-jp'>あふれんばかり</span> means “full to the brim,” and the water hasn't actually spilled over.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>とばかりに</b> follows a line of “dialogue,” and means “as if saying ~.” It means almost the same as <span class='gd-jp'>と言わんばかりに</span>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>店長は「早く帰れ」と言わんばかりに時計を見た。</span>(The manager looked at the clock as if to say “go home already.”)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>店長は「早く帰れ」とばかりに時計を見た。</span>(Same as above.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The difference is what can go before it. Before <b>んばかりに</b> goes <b>an action that was nearly carried out</b>, such as crying, overflowing or jumping up. Before <b>とばかりに</b> goes <b>words in someone's mind</b>. So “almost in tears” can only be <span class='gd-jp'>泣かんばかりに</span>, never 泣くとばかりに.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before is a “line of speech,” either とばかりに or と言わんばかりに works. If it's an “action about to happen,” you can only use the ない form + んばかりに.</div>","n1-89":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This pair works like English <b>\"without ~\"</b> and <b>\"not ... without ~\"</b>. There are two uses:<br><span class='gd-jp'>なしに</span>: \"He took it <b>without</b> saying anything.\" Someone <b>skipped the step they should have taken and just did it</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>なしには</span>: \"<b>Without</b> you, we <b>couldn't</b> have succeeded.\" <b>Without A, B is simply impossible</b>.<br>The two differ by just one は, but they are used very differently.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>なしに</span>Something that should have been there was missing, and the person went ahead anyway. It often carries a hint of disapproval, like \"that's not right, is it?\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は連絡なしに会社を休んだ。</span>(He took the day off without telling anyone.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>断りなしに、人の物を使ってはいけない。</span>(You must not use other people's things without asking.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は一言の断りもなしに帰ってしまった。</span>(He went home without so much as a word.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add も in the middle to get <span class='gd-jp'>もなしに</span>. It is stronger and feels like \"not even ~\".</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>なしには</span>A is an indispensable condition. Without A, B can't be done.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この映画は、涙なしには見られない。</span>(You can't watch this movie without crying.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皆さんの協力なしには、成功できなかった。</span>(Without everyone's help, I couldn't have succeeded.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皆さんの協力なしには、成功しなかったでしょう。</span>(Without everyone's help, it probably wouldn't have succeeded.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b> attach directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>許可</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>許可なしに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>努力</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>努力なしには</span></span><br><b>Verbs</b> need こと first, giving 「dictionary form + ことなしに(は)」. This is the more formal, written form:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は休むことなしに働き続けた。</span>(He kept working without a moment's rest.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>努力することなしには、夢はかなわない。</span>(Without effort, dreams don't come true.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>The second half after なしには must be negative.</b> Common endings are <span class='gd-jp'>〜できない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜られない</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜ありえない</span>, but <span class='gd-jp'>〜ない</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜なかった（でしょう）</span> also work.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>What follows なしに doesn't have to be negative</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>連絡なしに休んだ</span> is an affirmative sentence. Only <b>なしには</b> must be followed by a negative.<br>・なしには can't be followed by an affirmative: ×<span class='gd-jp'>協力なしには成功した</span> doesn't make sense.<br>・Verbs can't attach directly: ×<span class='gd-jp'>連絡しなしに</span>. Write <span class='gd-jp'>連絡なしに</span> (as a noun) or <span class='gd-jp'>連絡することなしに</span>.<br>・This is a formal, written style. In everyday speech people mostly say <b>なしで</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>予約なしで入れますか。</span>(Can I go in without a reservation?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both of these talk about \"no reservation\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>予約なしに店に入った。</span>(He went into the shop without a reservation: this describes <b>how he did it</b>.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>予約なしには入れない。</span>(You can't get in without a reservation: this states a <b>necessary condition</b>.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>なくして（は）</b>: nearly the same meaning as なしには, but stiffer, and mostly used in speeches and writing.<br><span class='gd-jp'>皆さんの協力なくしては、成功はありえなかった。</span>(Without your cooperation, success would have been impossible.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>あっての</b>: the same idea from the opposite direction: \"B exists only because of A.\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>お客様あっての店だ。</span>(A shop exists only because of its customers.)<br>＝<span class='gd-jp'>お客様なしには、店は成り立たない。</span>(Without customers, a shop can't survive.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for \"did it without ~\", use なしに. For \"impossible without ~\", use なしには (always followed by a negative). For a more formal tone, switch to なくしては. To say it from the \"B exists only because of A\" angle, use あっての.</div>","n1-91":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に足る」 means <b>\"worthy of ~\"</b> or <b>\"enough to ~\"</b>: a person or thing is <b>good enough</b> to be trusted, respected or found satisfying. The reverse, 「〜に足りない」, is like <b>\"nothing to fear\"</b> or <b>\"not worth mentioning\"</b>: not worth it, no need to bother. 「足る」 is read <b>たる</b>. It is a stiff, written expression that often appears in news, commentary and novels.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verb dictionary form + に足る</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>信頼する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>信頼するに足る</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>満足する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>満足するに足る</span></span><br><b>For 「〜する」 verbs, drop する and attach the noun directly</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>信頼</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>信頼に足る</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>尊敬</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>尊敬に足る</span></span><br>It is mostly placed before a noun as a modifier, as in 「<span class='gd-jp'>信頼に足る人物</span>」, but it can also end a sentence as a conclusion.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>に足る</span>A formal way to judge a person, document, reason or result as \"good enough, up to standard.\" It often goes with verbs of evaluation such as <span class='gd-jp'>信頼する・尊敬する・満足する・納得する・信じる</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は尊敬に足る先輩だ。</span>(She is a senior colleague worthy of respect.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の説明は、納得するに足るものだった。</span>(His explanation was convincing enough.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この報告書には、信頼に足るデータがない。</span>(This report contains no data worth trusting.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>に足りない</span>Says something is not important at all, nothing to be afraid of. It is mostly used in a few fixed expressions. <span class='gd-jp'>に足らない</span> is also common and means the same.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんなのは取るに足りない問題だ。</span>(That's just a trivial problem.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>相手は新人ばかりだ。恐れるに足りない。</span>(The opponents are all rookies. There's nothing to fear.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>取るに足りない</span> doesn't mean \"not enough to pick up.\" It is a set phrase to memorize as a whole: \"trivial, insignificant.\" It has nothing to do with the \"not enough\" of <span class='gd-jp'>お金が足りない</span> (not enough money).<br>・Only the <b>dictionary form</b> or the noun of a する verb can come before it, not the た form or ます form: <span class='gd-jp'>信頼したに足る</span> is wrong. You also can't attach ordinary nouns freely: <span class='gd-jp'>彼に足る</span> makes no sense.<br>・The negative <span class='gd-jp'>に足りない</span> is mainly used in fixed phrases like <span class='gd-jp'>取るに足りない</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>恐れるに足りない</span>. To say \"not trustworthy\" in general, <span class='gd-jp'>信頼できない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>信頼に値しない</span> sounds more natural.<br>・This is written language. When chatting with a friend and saying \"that person is reliable,\" <span class='gd-jp'>あの人は信頼できる</span> is enough. Using に足る would sound like reading out an official document.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to judge a book:<br><b>に足る</b> = up to standard, good enough: <span class='gd-jp'>これは読むに足る本だ。</span>(This is a book worth reading.)<br><b>に値する</b> = has that kind of value. The meaning is very close, and in many cases they are interchangeable. The difference is that its negative <span class='gd-jp'>に値しない</span> can be used freely: <span class='gd-jp'>この本は一読に値しない。</span>(This book isn't worth reading even once.)<br><b>に堪えない</b> (n1-36) = so bad that it's <b>unbearable</b>, with strong emotion: <span class='gd-jp'>読むに堪えない文章だ。</span>(It's writing too awful to read.)<br>So 「<span class='gd-jp'>取るに足りない</span>」 means \"unimportant, not worth minding,\" while 「<span class='gd-jp'>見るに堪えない</span>」 means \"so bad I can't stand to look at it.\" The two point in completely different directions.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for \"enough to ~ / not worth mentioning,\" use に足る・に足りない. To freely say \"not worth ~,\" use に値しない. For \"so bad it's unbearable,\" use に堪えない.</div>","n1-19":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に至るまで」 is like English <b>\"from A all the way down to B\"</b> or <b>\"right down to B\"</b>. Take \"from the boss to the part-timers, everyone joined.\" The point isn't where the range stops. It stresses that <b>even something that far out, or that small, is included</b>, so the range is very wide.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + に至るまで. It most often pairs with 「から」:<br><b>A から B に至るまで</b> = from A all the way to (even) B<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子供から高齢者</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子供から高齢者に至るまで</span></span> (from children to the elderly)<br>B usually sits at the <b>other end</b> of the range, and it's often the end you wouldn't expect to be counted. It's often followed by \"all\"-type words such as <span class='gd-jp'>全員</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>すべて</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>誰もが</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>細かく</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Where you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Range of people</span>From top to bottom, from old to young: everyone is included.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長から新入社員に至るまで、全員が会議に参加した。</span> (From the company president to the newest hires, everyone took part in the meeting.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供から高齢者に至るまで、誰もが楽しめるイベントだ。</span> (It's an event that everyone, from children to the elderly, can enjoy.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Range of details</span>From the big picture down to the smallest detail, everything is covered.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>服装から髪型に至るまで、細かく決められている。</span> (From clothing down to hairstyle, even the details are specified.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部屋の隅々に至るまで、きれいに掃除した。</span> (I cleaned the room spotless, right down to every corner.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Range of time</span>From long ago all the way to the present, stressing a long stretch of time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>創業から現在に至るまで、この味を守り続けている。</span> (From the founding to the present day, we have kept protecting this flavor.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・English \"up to\" or \"until\" can make it sound like <b>drawing a line or setting a limit</b>. It's actually the opposite: it doesn't mean \"only as far as here\" but \"<b>even this is included</b>\". The point is the wide range.<br>・A and B must be the two ends of the <b>same kind of thing</b>: people with people, things with things, time with time.<br>・For an ordinary endpoint, like \"wait until what time\" or \"walk to the station\", plain <span class='gd-jp'>まで</span> is enough. Using に至るまで there sounds odd.<br>・This is <b>formal, written</b> language, common in news, announcements and company profiles. With friends, <span class='gd-jp'>おじいちゃんまでみんなスマホ持ってるよ</span> (even grandpa has a smartphone!) is all you need.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>N1 has three easily confused 「至る」 patterns. Let's compare them in one situation, \"a family and their smartphones\":<br><b>に至るまで</b> = a very wide range, <b>everything included</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>祖父母から孫に至るまで、家族全員がスマホを持っている。</span> (From the grandparents to the grandchildren, the whole family has a smartphone.)<br><b>に至っては</b> = <b>picks out the most extreme case</b> from a group, usually with a hint of criticism:<br><span class='gd-jp'>うちの家族は機械に弱い。祖母に至っては、電話のかけ方も知らない。</span> (My family is bad with machines. As for my grandmother, she doesn't even know how to make a phone call.)<br><b>に至る</b> = things develop step by step until they <b>finally reach a result</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>何度も話し合って、ようやく祖母もスマホを買うに至った。</span> (After talking it over many times, my grandmother finally ended up buying a smartphone too.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for \"from ~ to ~, all of it\", use に至るまで. For \"the most extreme one is ~\", use に至っては. For \"it ended up as ~\", use に至る.</div>","n1-20":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜を経て」 is like English <b>\"after going through ~\"</b>, <b>\"through ~\"</b> or <b>\"via ~\"</b>. The point is that you <b>pass through a process, a stage, or a place</b> along the way, and only then reach the result that follows. It's formal and common in news, reports, profiles and speeches, but less so in chats with friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + を経て　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>長い議論</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>長い議論を経て</span> (after a long discussion)</span><br>Only a <b>noun</b> goes before it, never a verb directly. To talk about \"preparing\", write <span class='gd-jp'>準備を経て</span>, not 「準備するを経て」.<br>「経て」 is read <b>へて</b>. Its base form is <span class='gd-jp'>経る</span> (へる), meaning \"to pass through\".</div><div class='gd-sec'>When it needs to modify a noun, change it to <b>を経た + noun</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>厳しい訓練</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>厳しい訓練を経た選手</span> (an athlete who has gone through strict training)</span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Going through a process</span>Things that take time and effort, like discussion, review, negotiation or preparation. The result comes only after they're done.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>長い議論を経て、ようやく結論が出た。</span> (After a long discussion, a conclusion was finally reached.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>厳しい審査を経て、新しい薬が発売された。</span> (After a strict review, the new drug went on sale.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Time passing</span>Often used with 「歳月」 or 「年月」, giving a feeling of \"after so many years\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>10年の歳月を経て、橋が完成した。</span> (After ten years, the bridge was finally completed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Going through a role</span>Very common in profiles, describing the stages a person has been through.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は会社員を経て、作家になった。</span> (After working as an office worker, he became a writer.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Passing through a place</span>A place you pass through along the way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>香港を経て、日本に着いた。</span> (Via Hong Kong, I arrived in Japan.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台北から台中を経て、高雄へ向かう。</span> (From Taipei, via Taichung, we head to Kaohsiung.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・「経て」 is read <b>へて</b>, not けいて or たって.<br>・The same 「経」 also appears in <span class='gd-jp'>経つ</span> (たつ), meaning \"time passes\". <span class='gd-jp'>3年経って</span> is read さんねんたって and is the everyday spoken \"three years went by\". The two look alike, but the reading and the tone are different.<br>・This is formal. If you tell a friend \"I changed planes via Hong Kong\", <span class='gd-jp'>香港経由で</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>香港で乗り換えて</span> sounds more natural. Using 「を経て」 for small everyday things sounds like reading a press release.<br>・What goes before it should be a <b>process worth mentioning</b>. For small things like eating a meal or taking a bath, just use 「〜てから」, not 「を経て」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>を経て vs 経って</b>: both can express \"a period of time passed\".<br><span class='gd-jp'>3年経って、やっと仕事に慣れた。</span> (After three years, I finally got used to the job.): Casual. 「経って」 follows the time expression directly, with no を.<br><span class='gd-jp'>3年の歳月を経て、作品が完成した。</span> (After three years, the work was completed.): Formal, and it usually carries the feeling that \"a lot of effort went into the result\".</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>を経て vs に至る</b>: they often appear together, but they play different roles.<br><span class='gd-jp'>何度もの交渉を経て、合意に至った。</span> (After many rounds of negotiation, they finally reached an agreement.)<br>「を経て」 describes <b>the road passed along the way</b>, while 「に至る」 describes <b>the final destination reached</b>.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to say what you went through in the middle, use 「を経て」. To say where you ended up, use 「に至る」. For a casual \"a few years passed\", use 「経って」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same-level <span class='gd-jp'>〜に至る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜に至るまで</span> are worth memorizing together with 「を経て」. They're very useful when writing about a process leading to a result.</div>","n1-38":"<div class='gd-h'>The gist</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に難くない」 means <b>\"it's not hard to imagine\", \"it's easy to understand\", \"it goes without saying\"</b>. You use a small clue to work out the situation or feeling behind it. It's read <b>かたくない</b>. It's formal and written in tone, and very common in news, commentary, and essays.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>What comes before it is almost always a word for \"working something out in your head\". The set is small, so it's fastest to memorize each chunk whole:<br><span class='gd-jp'>想像に難くない</span> (not hard to imagine)<br><span class='gd-jp'>理解に難くない</span> (not hard to understand)<br><span class='gd-jp'>予想に難くない</span> (not hard to predict)<br><span class='gd-jp'>推測に難くない</span> (not hard to infer)<br><span class='gd-jp'>察するに難くない</span> (not hard to sense or guess)<br>Nouns like 想像 and 理解 attach directly with 「に」. <span class='gd-jp'>察する</span> is a verb itself, so it takes the dictionary form.</div><div class='gd-sec'>The content you're inferring goes in front. It usually takes one of three shapes: 「〜は」, 「〜ことは」, or 「〜のは」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親を亡くした彼の悲しみは想像に難くない。</span> (It's not hard to imagine how sad he is after losing his parents.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この計画に反対の声が上がることは予想に難くない。</span> (It's easy to predict that opposition to this plan will arise.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>物価がこれだけ上がれば、生活が苦しくなるのは想像に難くない。</span> (With prices rising this much, it's easy to imagine that life will get harder.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女の表情から、何があったかは察するに難くない。</span> (From her expression, it's not hard to guess what happened.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・「難くない」 is read <b>かたくない</b>, not むずかしくない.<br>・It means \"not hard to <b>imagine</b>\", not \"easy to <b>do</b>\". To say \"this problem is easy to solve\", say <span class='gd-jp'>この問題は簡単だ</span>. You can't use に難くない there.<br>・Don't put just any verb in front. Stick to 想像, 理解, 予想, 推測, and 察する.<br>・It's stiff, and you won't hear it in everyday chat. With a friend, for \"you can imagine\", <span class='gd-jp'>想像できる</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>想像がつく</span> sounds natural.<br>・Don't read the 「ない」 as a negative meaning. 「難くない」 = not hard = <b>easy</b>, so the whole sentence is affirmative in tone.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>N1 has several 「〜に＋negative」 patterns that look alike but mean very different things. Let's compare them in one situation: a friend was laid off.<br><b>に難くない</b> = not hard to imagine: <span class='gd-jp'>彼のショックは想像に難くない。</span> (It's not hard to imagine how shocked he is.)<br><b>には当たらない</b> = no need to react that way: <span class='gd-jp'>この不景気だから、リストラされても驚くには当たらない。</span> (With the economy this bad, there's no need to be surprised at being laid off.)<br><b>に堪えない</b> = so bad you can't bear to watch or listen: <span class='gd-jp'>会社の説明は聞くに堪えない言い訳ばかりだった。</span> (The company's explanation was nothing but excuses too awful to listen to.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: \"you can well imagine\" is に難くない; \"no need to bother with ~\" is には当たらない; \"too awful to bear\" is に堪えない.</div>","n1-37":"<div class='gd-h'>The gist</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜を禁じ得ない」 means <b>\"can't help feeling ~\", \"can't help but ~\"</b>. When you see or hear something, an emotion like sympathy, surprise, or anger wells up on its own, and you can't suppress it. It's very formal and mostly appears in news commentary, essays, and speeches. You'll almost never say it in casual conversation.</div><div class='gd-h'>Break it down</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>禁じる</span> means \"to forbid\", and <span class='gd-jp'>得ない</span> means \"can't ~\". Put together, it's \"can't hold it back even if you try\": the emotion can't be stopped and just surfaces.<br>It's read <b>きんじえない</b>. Here 「得」 is read 「え」.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a <b>noun expressing an emotion</b> in front and add 「を禁じ得ない」. For past events, it becomes 「を禁じ得なかった」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>同情</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>同情を禁じ得ない</span></span> (can't help feeling sympathy)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>驚き</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>驚きを禁じ得なかった</span></span> (couldn't help being astonished)<br>Common nouns: <span class='gd-jp'>同情</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>驚き</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>怒り</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>憤り</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>涙</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>戸惑い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>疑問</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Being moved by something</span> First you usually say what you saw, heard, or thought about, then say that the emotion couldn't be held back.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その知らせを聞いて、驚きを禁じ得なかった。</span> (Hearing that news, I couldn't help being astonished.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>被害者の話を聞き、涙を禁じ得なかった。</span> (Listening to the victim's account, I couldn't hold back my tears.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Formal statements of opinion</span> Very common in commentary and editorials for expressing strong views.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>政府の対応には、怒りを禁じ得ない。</span> (I can't help feeling anger at the government's response.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この計画には疑問を禁じ得ない。</span> (I can't help having doubts about this plan.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・A <b>noun</b> must come before it, not a verb directly. Say <span class='gd-jp'>涙を禁じ得ない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>驚きを禁じ得ない</span>, not 「泣くを禁じ得ない」 or 「驚くを禁じ得ない」.<br>・It describes an emotion welling up in <b>yourself</b>. You don't use it to describe other people's feelings.<br>・The tone is very stiff. Saying 「感動を禁じ得なかった」 to a friend sounds like reading from a script. In speech, <span class='gd-jp'>思わず泣いちゃった</span> (I couldn't help crying) is enough.<br>・「得ない」 is read <b>えない</b>, not うない.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: you heard what happened to him and feel sorry for him.<br><b>を禁じ得ない</b> = the emotion <b>wells up and can't be held back</b>. Very formal, and it takes a noun:<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼の話を聞いて、同情を禁じ得なかった。</span> (Hearing his story, I couldn't help feeling sympathy for him.)<br><b>ずにはいられない</b> (N2) = \"can't help doing ~\". It takes a verb, can describe actions, and is more common and less stiff:<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼の話を聞いて、同情せずにはいられなかった。</span> (Hearing his story, I couldn't help feeling sorry for him.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>あまりにおかしくて、笑わずにはいられなかった。</span> (It was so funny that I couldn't help laughing.)<br>When <b>に堪えない</b> follows an emotion noun, it means \"extremely ~\" or \"~ beyond measure\". Common ones are <span class='gd-jp'>感謝に堪えない</span> (deeply grateful), <span class='gd-jp'>遺憾に堪えない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>同情に堪えない</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>憤慨に堪えない</span>. They're mostly used in formal speeches and writing. The feel is \"the emotion is so intense you can hardly bear it\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>被災された方々のことを思うと、同情に堪えない。</span> (When I think of those affected by the disaster, I feel deep sympathy.)<br>Also, when に堪えない follows a verb, it means something different: <span class='gd-jp'>見るに堪えない</span> means \"too awful to watch\". That has nothing to do with を禁じ得ない, so don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: an emotion that \"wells up and can't be held back\" takes を禁じ得ない; an emotion \"so intense you can hardly bear it, or ~ to the extreme\" takes に堪えない; the general \"can't help doing it\" takes ずにはいられない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another N1 pattern from the same group of strong emotions is <span class='gd-jp'>〜て止まない</span> (keep ~ from the bottom of one's heart). For example: <span class='gd-jp'>皆様のご健康を願って止みません。</span> (I sincerely wish everyone good health.) It often appears at the end of speeches and letters.</div>","n1-93":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たが最後」 means <b>\"once you do that, it's all over\"</b> or <b>\"there's no going back once you start\"</b>. As soon as the first thing happens, things inevitably turn bad, become irreversible, or can't be stopped. Think of \"once you open a bag of chips, that's it, you'll finish the whole bag.\" That's the feeling.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in the た-form (the past form ending in 「〜た」), then add 「が最後」 directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言ったが最後</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝た</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝たが最後</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見つかる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見つかった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見つかったが最後</span></span><br>「最後」 is read <b>さいご</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing someone's habits</span>Once a certain person does a certain thing, the same result always follows.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は一度寝たが最後、朝まで何をしても起きない。</span>(Once he falls asleep, that's it. Nothing you do will wake him until morning.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't stop</span>Once you start, you can't pull back.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このドラマは見始めたが最後、止まらなくなる。</span>(Once you start watching this drama, you can't stop.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Warning about consequences</span>A reminder: \"Whatever you do, don't do this, or you'll be in big trouble.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長に見つかったが最後、一時間は説教される。</span>(If the department head catches you, you're done. You'll get lectured for at least an hour.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>In speech it's often replaced by <b>「〜たら最後」</b>. The meaning is the same, but the tone is more casual:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんなこと言ったら最後、絶交されるよ。</span>(Once you say something like that, it's over. They'll cut you off, you know.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What follows <b>can only be a bad result</b>. You can't use が最後 for good things like \"once I win the lottery, I'll live well.\"<br>・The verb before it is always in the た-form, even when you're talking about something that hasn't happened yet. You can't say 「食べるが最後」.<br>・Here 「最後」 doesn't mean \"last\" or \"finally\" in the English sense. It means \"that's the end, it's over.\" Don't translate it as \"after eating, finally...\".<br>・What follows is usually a description or judgment (\"this will definitely happen\"). It rarely takes a request (\"please...\") or a statement of resolve (\"I will...\").<br>・It often pairs with \"no way back\" expressions like <span class='gd-jp'>二度と〜ない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>止まらない</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>終わりだ</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜が早いか</b>: also often translated \"as soon as ~\", but it only says that <b>something happens very quickly</b>, whether good or bad.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は布団に入るが早いか、眠ってしまった。</span>(As soon as he got under the covers, he fell asleep.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は布団に入ったが最後、昼まで出てこない。</span>(Once he gets under the covers, that's it. He won't come out until noon.)<br>The first sentence is about speed. The second is about \"no way back afterward.\"</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜たところで</b>: it also follows the た-form, but it means <b>\"even if you do it, it's no use.\"</b><br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼に話したが最後、みんなに知られてしまう。</span>(Once you tell him, that's it. Everyone will find out.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>今さら彼に話したところで、何も変わらない。</span>(Telling him now won't change anything.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: \"once it's done there's no going back, and the result is bad\" is が最後. \"As soon as X, immediately Y\" is が早いか. \"Even if you do it, it's pointless\" is たところで.</div>","n1-18":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に至る」 is the phrase you often hear in the news for <b>\"it developed into ~\"</b> or <b>\"it ended up reaching the point of ~\"</b>. Over time, through a series of developments, things arrive at a certain outcome. That outcome is usually something weighty: a lawsuit, bankruptcy, a settlement, a conclusion. It's very written and formal, and appears most in news, reports, press conferences, and company announcements.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: add に至る directly, with no の in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>訴訟</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>訴訟に至る</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>和解</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>和解に至る</span></span><br><b>Verbs</b>: use the dictionary form (the base form you find in the dictionary) plus に至る.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>中止する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>中止するに至る</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>考える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>考えるに至る</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>至る</span> itself is a <b>Group 1 (godan) verb</b> and conjugates like <span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>至る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>至った</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>至る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>至っていない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>至る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>至らなかった</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll most often hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Ending up at a result</span>It's most common in the past tense 「〜に至った」. It's often preceded by <span class='gd-jp'>ついに</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜の末</span>, or <span class='gd-jp'>〜結果</span>, signaling \"after a process.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>長い話し合いの末、両社は合併に至った。</span>(After lengthy negotiations, the two companies ended up merging.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>留学を通して、日本で働きたいと考えるに至った。</span>(Through studying abroad, I gradually came to want to work in Japan.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Not there yet</span>The negative is usually written 「〜には至っていない」, meaning things haven't developed to that stage yet.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この技術は、まだ実用化には至っていない。</span>(This technology hasn't reached the stage of practical use yet.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Set phrases</span>These three are very common. Memorize them as whole sentences.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>幸い、大事には至らなかった。</span>(Fortunately, it didn't turn into anything serious.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>中止に至った経緯をご説明します。</span>(I will explain how we came to the decision to cancel.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>1990年に創業し、現在に至る。</span>(Founded in 1990 and operating up to the present.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Verbs take the dictionary form, not the た-form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>中止するに至った</span>, not 「中止したに至った」. The past meaning goes on <span class='gd-jp'>至った</span> alone.<br>・It's very written. If you're telling a friend \"we ended up breaking up,\" <span class='gd-jp'>結局別れることになった</span> is enough. Saying 「別れるに至った」 would sound like you're reading out a press release.<br>・The outcome has to be weighty, usually after a process. For small things like buying bread because you're hungry, you can't say 「パンを買うに至った」.<br>・Although it literally means \"reach,\" for everyday \"arriving at a place\" you need <span class='gd-jp'>着く</span>.<br>・On its own, <span class='gd-jp'>至らない</span> often has a different meaning, \"lacking / falling short\": <span class='gd-jp'>至らない点もありますが、よろしくお願いします。</span>(There may be areas where I fall short, but I look forward to working with you.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>N1 has three look-alike 「至る」 patterns. Let's compare them in one scenario, \"a company in bad shape\":<br><b>に至る</b> = things <b>end up becoming</b> a certain result:<br><span class='gd-jp'>赤字が続き、ついに倒産に至った。</span>(Losses continued, and it finally ended in bankruptcy.)<br><b>に至るまで</b> = a range <b>from A all the way to B, even B</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>社長から新入社員に至るまで、全員が毎日残業している。</span>(From the president down to the newest employees, everyone works overtime every day.)<br><b>に至っては</b> = <b>as for B, it's even more extreme</b>, singling out the most extreme example:<br><span class='gd-jp'>どの部署も業績が悪いが、営業部に至っては目標の半分にも届いていない。</span>(Every department is performing badly, but the sales department hasn't even reached half its target.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: at the end of a sentence, saying \"it finally became ~\", it's に至る. With \"from A to B\" before it, describing a range, it's に至るまで. In the middle of a sentence as a topic, introducing \"the even worse one,\" it's に至っては.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, study <span class='gd-jp'>〜に至るまで</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜に至っては</span>. Memorizing all three together is the best way to avoid mixing them up. You can also learn <span class='gd-jp'>〜を経て</span> (\"after going through ~\"), which often appears together with に至る: <span class='gd-jp'>何度もの議論を経て、合意に至った。</span>(After many rounds of discussion, they finally reached an agreement.)</div>","n1-34":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜極まりない」 means <b>\"utterly ~\"</b> or <b>\"~ in the extreme\"</b>, as in \"utterly rude\" or \"dangerous in the extreme.\" You use it to <b>criticize something harshly</b>. The tone is as stiff as a news report, an editorial or a formal letter of protest. 「〜極まる」 means the same thing but sounds a bit more literary.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Harsh criticism</span>You pass a very strong verdict on someone's attitude or behavior.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの店員の対応は失礼極まりない。</span>(That clerk's service was utterly rude.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夜中に大声で騒ぐなんて、迷惑極まりない。</span>(Making loud noise in the middle of the night is a nuisance in the extreme.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing a very bad situation</span>News and reports use it for dangerous or troublesome things.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨の夜に無灯火で走るのは危険極まりない。</span>(Riding without a light on a rainy night is extremely dangerous.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Before a noun</span>You can use it directly to describe the noun that follows.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>退屈極まりない会議が三時間も続いた。</span>(The meeting was unbelievably boring and went on for a full three hours.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>With な-adjectives, drop 「な」 and attach it directly:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>失礼な</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>失礼極まりない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>危険な</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>危険極まる</span></span><br>The most common partners: <span class='gd-jp'>失礼、無礼、危険、迷惑、不快、不愉快、退屈、非常識、無責任</span>. Almost all are words of two or more kanji that are already negative.<br><br>Since 「極まりない」 ends in 「ない」, it can go into the past tense like an い-adjective: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>失礼極まりない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>失礼極まりなかった</span></span><br>い-adjectives can't attach directly. Add 「こと」 first: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>情けない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>情けないこと極まりない</span></span> (pathetic in the extreme)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The 「ない」 in 「極まりない」 is <b>not a negation</b>. 「極まり」 means \"limit\" or \"end,\" so 「極まりない」 means \"there is no limit,\" that is, \"extremely ~.\" <span class='gd-jp'>失礼極まりない</span> means \"utterly rude,\" not \"not rude.\"<br>・Don't add な in the middle. It's <span class='gd-jp'>失礼極まりない</span>, not 「失礼な極まりない」.<br>・It's used almost only for bad things. To say food is delicious or something is handy, use another expression. Don't force this one.<br>・The tone is heavy and stiff. When complaining to a friend, <span class='gd-jp'>本当に失礼だよね</span> is enough. Saying 「失礼極まりない」 to someone's face is basically a formal break in the relationship.<br>・Readings: 極まりない is read きわまりない, and 極まる is read きわまる.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same case of being treated rudely by a clerk:<br><b>極まりない</b> = a calm, very heavy <b>verdict</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>あの店員の態度は失礼極まりない。</span> (That clerk's attitude is utterly rude.)<br><b>といったらない</b> = an exclamation where your <b>emotions overflow</b>. It's more colloquial, and い-adjectives attach directly: <span class='gd-jp'>あの店員の態度、腹が立つといったらない。</span> (That clerk's attitude made me so angry, you can't imagine.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>の至り</b> also means \"the utmost ~,\" but it points the other way. It talks about <b>your own</b> feelings, mostly good ones like honor or gratitude, and it's used in speeches and polite settings.<br><span class='gd-jp'>このような賞をいただき、光栄の至りです。</span> (Receiving an award like this is the utmost honor.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to criticize something bad, use 「極まりない」. To politely say you feel very honored or grateful, use 「の至り」. For an outburst of anger, embarrassment or joy, use 「といったらない」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same kanji appears in two other common expressions:<br>Noun + の極み: <span class='gd-jp'>こんな料理を毎日食べるなんて、贅沢の極みだ。</span> (Eating food like this every day is the height of luxury.)<br>The set phrase 「感極まる」: <span class='gd-jp'>優勝が決まった瞬間、感極まって泣き出した。</span> (The moment the win was decided, I was overcome with emotion and burst into tears.)</div>","n1-52":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜まじき」 means <b>\"How could you do that, as a ~!\"</b> You use it to scold someone for doing something <b>unbecoming of their position</b>, like \"conduct unbecoming of…\" in the news. The tone is very heavy and formal, and you mostly see it in news, editorials and formal statements.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb dictionary form (the base form you'd look up) + まじき + noun<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>あるまじき行為</span></span> (conduct that should not exist)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>許す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>許すまじき犯罪</span></span> (an unforgivable crime)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言うまじきこと</span></span> (something that should never be said)<br>Besides <span class='gd-jp'>するまじき</span>, you'll also see the older form <span class='gd-jp'>すまじき</span> for 「する」.</div><div class='gd-h'>The form you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Position + にあるまじき</span>Over 90% of uses are this fixed combination: 「person or position + に (or として) + あるまじき + noun」, meaning \"a ~ that should never appear, given that position.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>医者にあるまじき行為だ。</span>(That's conduct unbecoming of a doctor.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>プロにあるまじきミスだ。</span>(That's a mistake a professional should never make.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社会人としてあるまじき態度だと叱られた。</span>(I was scolded for an attitude unbecoming of a working adult.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>許すまじき</span>Without mentioning any position, it says directly, \"This must never be forgiven.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもを狙った許すまじき犯罪だ。</span>(It's an unforgivable crime that targeted children.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・まじき <b>must be followed by a noun</b>. It can't end the sentence:<br>　×<span class='gd-jp'>教師にあるまじきだ。</span><br>　○<span class='gd-jp'>教師にあるまじき発言だ。</span><br>・It means \"<b>should not</b>,\" not \"<b>cannot / is unable to</b>.\" It's a moral judgment and has nothing to do with ability.<br>・The 「許す」 in 「許すまじき」 is about whether <b>others</b> forgive, so 「政治家として許すまじき発言」 can easily be misread as \"a statement we can't forgive as politicians.\" To say \"that politician shouldn't have said that,\" <span class='gd-jp'>政治家としてあるまじき発言</span> is the clearest.<br>・It's very stiff and sounds odd in everyday conversation. In casual speech, <span class='gd-jp'>先生なのに、ありえない。</span> (They're a teacher, and yet… unbelievable.) is enough.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same case of scolding a teacher for corporal punishment:<br><b>べきではない</b> = the general \"shouldn't.\" It works in any setting and can end a sentence: <span class='gd-jp'>教師は体罰をするべきではない。</span> (Teachers shouldn't use corporal punishment.)<br><b>まじき</b> = also \"shouldn't,\" but heavier and more written, and it can only go before a noun: <span class='gd-jp'>体罰は教師にあるまじき行為だ。</span> (Corporal punishment is conduct unbecoming of a teacher.)<br><b>べからざる</b> = the focus is \"must not / indispensable.\" It often talks about rules or necessity and doesn't have to involve a position: <span class='gd-jp'>水は生活に欠くべからざるものだ。</span> (Water is indispensable to life.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if there's 「position + に／として」 in front and you're calling someone unfit, it's まじき. If you're talking about rules or something \"indispensable,\" it's べからざる.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たる」 = \"being the kind of person who is a ~.\" It often appears together with まじき in meaning: <span class='gd-jp'>教師たる者、生徒の手本でなければならない。</span> (One who is a teacher must be a model for their students.)</div>","n1-53":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜べからず」is what you see on a sign: <b>\"Do not ~\"</b>, <b>\"~ prohibited\"</b>, <b>\"Please do not ~\"</b>. It's classical Japanese, and it feels like English signs such as \"No trespassing\" or \"Keep out\": stiff and formal. You'll almost never hear it in everyday speech. You'll mostly see it on <b>notices, slogans and proverbs</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Signs</span>A prohibition written for everyone to read, placed at the end of the sentence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここに入るべからず。</span>(No entry here.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここにごみを捨てるべからず。</span>(No dumping trash here.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Proverbs and slogans</span>Many old sayings and famous quotes use this form. Just memorize the whole sentence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>働かざる者食うべからず。</span>(Those who don't work shall not eat.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>初心忘るべからず。</span>(Never forget your original resolve.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>べからざる + noun</span>When a noun follows, switch to <b>べからざる</b>. It means \"a ~ that must not be done\" or \"a ~ that cannot be ~ed\", and it shows up in essays and news.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>水は生活に欠くべからざるものだ。</span>(Water is something indispensable to life.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>それは許すべからざる行為だ。</span>(That is an unforgivable act.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs use the <b>dictionary form</b> (the base form you find in a dictionary). Just add it directly:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>入る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>入るべからず</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>捨てる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>捨てるべからず</span></span><br><b>する</b> usually becomes <b>す</b> before it: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>駐車する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>駐車すべからず</span></span> (You'll also see するべからず, but すべからず sounds more authentic.)<br>To modify a noun, change ず to ざる: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>欠くべからず</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>欠くべからざるもの</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use <b>べからず</b> at the end of a sentence and <b>べからざる</b> before a noun. Don't write「べからずもの」.<br>・It belongs on signs and in writing, so <b>you can't use it to tell someone face to face not to do something</b>. Saying「吸うべからず」to a friend would sound very odd. In conversation, use <span class='gd-jp'>吸わないで</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>吸っちゃだめ</span>.<br>・Don't attach it to the ない form. 「行かないべからず」is wrong, because it already means \"must not\" by itself.<br>・The 「忘る」 in <span class='gd-jp'>初心忘るべからず</span> is the classical base form of <span class='gd-jp'>忘れる</span>. It's a fixed phrase, so memorize it as is and don't change it.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>すべからく</span> looks similar but is a different word. It means \"by all means; ought to\", and it is not negative.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same situation, smoking:<br><b>べからず</b> = a <b>prohibition</b> on a sign: <span class='gd-jp'>ここでたばこを吸うべからず。</span> (No smoking here.)<br><b>べきではない</b> = <b>advice</b> that someone \"shouldn't\". You can use it in ordinary speech or writing: <span class='gd-jp'>ここでたばこを吸うべきではない。</span> (You shouldn't smoke here.)<br><b>まじき</b> = something that shouldn't be done by someone <b>in that position</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>患者の前でたばこを吸うなんて、医者にあるまじき行為だ。</span> (Smoking in front of patients is conduct unbecoming of a doctor.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, <b>べく</b> only looks similar. It means something completely different, \"in order to ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>合格すべく、毎日勉強している。</span> (I study every day in order to pass.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for a \"prohibition\" on a notice or in a proverb, use べからず. For advice that someone \"shouldn't\", use べきではない. When it's about \"as a ~\" (someone's role), use まじき. And \"in order to ~\" is べく.</div>","n1-55":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たる」means <b>\"as a ~\"</b> or <b>\"being a ~\"</b>, with the same tone as \"as a teacher\" or \"those who are parents\". You first bring up a weighty role, then say what someone in that role <b>ought to</b> do.<br><span class='gd-jp'>たる</span> is a very old form meaning the same as <span class='gd-jp'>である</span>. It goes only before a noun, so it sounds formal and lofty, and you mostly see it in writing, speeches and lectures.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + たる + noun. The most common pattern is followed by <span class='gd-jp'>者</span> (often written <span class='gd-jp'>もの</span>, which also means \"person\"):<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>医者</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>医者たる者</span></span> (someone who is a doctor)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>親</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>親たるもの</span></span> (someone who is a parent)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Saying what one should do</span>This is the biggest use. It's often followed by <span class='gd-jp'>べきだ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>なければならない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>てはならない</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>医者たる者、患者の話をよく聞くべきだ。</span>(As a doctor, you should listen carefully to your patients.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>プロたる者、言い訳はしない。</span>(As a professional, you don't make excuses.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Criticizing a failure</span>You first state the role, then point out that the person did something unworthy of it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>教師たる者が生徒にうそをつくなんて、許されることではない。</span>(For a teacher to lie to students is unforgivable.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>With other nouns</span>What follows doesn't have to be 者. It can also be a noun like「資格」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼に代表たる資格はない。</span>(He isn't qualified to be a representative.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Only a <b>noun</b> goes before it, usually a role, position or standing that carries responsibilities. You can't attach it to a verb or adjective.<br>・It is not the past-tense 「た」 plus 「る」. <span class='gd-jp'>たる</span> is a single word, so don't split it up.<br>・It's very stiff, and using it in casual chat sounds like you're lecturing. For \"as a ~, you should ~\" in conversation, <span class='gd-jp'>〜なんだから</span> is enough: <span class='gd-jp'>先生なんだから、ちゃんとしてよ。</span> (You're a teacher, so act like one!)<br>・Don't mix it up with the homophone <span class='gd-jp'>足る</span> (to be worthy of, to be enough). They're compared below.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same topic, teachers:<br><b>たる者</b> = state the role first, then say what one should do (or criticize a failure to do it): <span class='gd-jp'>教師たる者、生徒の手本であるべきだ。</span> (As a teacher, you should be a role model for your students.)<br><b>にあるまじき</b> = directly judge a specific <b>act or remark</b> as \"unbecoming of that role\". It's followed by nouns like <span class='gd-jp'>行為</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>発言</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>教師にあるまじき発言だ。</span> (That's a remark unbecoming of a teacher.)<br><b>ともなれば</b> = once you reach that level, <b>naturally</b> ~. It describes an expected result, not an obligation: <span class='gd-jp'>ベテラン教師ともなれば、生徒の顔を見ただけで悩みに気づく。</span> (A veteran teacher can tell a student is worried just by looking at their face.)<br><b>に足る</b> = <b>worthy of</b> ~. It follows words like <span class='gd-jp'>信頼</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>尊敬</span>, and note the extra <span class='gd-jp'>に</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は信頼に足る教師だ。</span> (He is a teacher worthy of trust.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to say \"as a ~, one should…\" or \"as a ~, how could you…\", state the role first with たる(者). To judge a specific act as \"unbecoming of a ~\", use「～にあるまじき + 行為／発言」. For \"at that level, naturally ~\", use ともなれば. For \"worthy of ~\", use に足る.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Two more related forms are worth learning together: <span class='gd-jp'>〜まじき</span> (unbecoming of ~), which also judges someone's role, and <span class='gd-jp'>〜ともなれば</span>, which talks about \"once you reach that level\".</div>","n1-36":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に堪えない」 has two meanings that point in opposite directions, and English has a match for each:<br>①<b>“Unbearable to see / hear”</b>: something so bad or so tragic that you can't stand to look at it or listen to it.<br>②<b>“Deeply grateful, deeply moved”</b>: feelings so full they can't be contained, which means “extremely ～”.<br>Both are very written and formal. You'll almost never hear them in everyday chat.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>①<b>Verb dictionary form + に堪えない</b>: almost only the three verbs “see, hear, read” go in front.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見るに堪えない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞くに堪えない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読むに堪えない</span></span><br>②<b>Emotion noun + に堪えない</b>: the most common words are <span class='gd-jp'>感謝</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>感激</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>感慨</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>遺憾</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>痛恨</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>感謝</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>感謝に堪えません</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遺憾</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遺憾に堪えない</span></span><br>「堪えない」 is read <b>たえない</b>, and it's often written directly in hiragana as 「たえない」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Usage ①: so bad you can't stand it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Criticism, disgust</span>This says the content is too poor, too ugly or too tragic to be worth seeing, hearing or reading. It often connects directly to a noun, as in 「聞くに堪えない悪口」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>聞くに堪えない悪口だった。</span>（Those insults were unbearable to listen to）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この翻訳はひどくて、読むに堪えない。</span>（This translation is so awful it's unreadable）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>事故の現場は見るに堪えない状態だった。</span>（The accident scene was too horrific to look at）</div><div class='gd-h'>Usage ②: feelings too full to hold</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Formal speeches</span>Common in speeches, thank-you letters, apology statements and eulogies, and it expresses <b>your own</b> strong feelings. When you say it to someone's face, it usually becomes 「〜に堪えません」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皆様の温かいご支援に、感謝の念に堪えません。</span>（We are deeply grateful for your warm support）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このような事態となり、誠に遺憾に堪えません。</span>（It has come to this, and we deeply regret it）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>二十年ぶりに母校を訪れ、感慨に堪えない。</span>（Visiting my old school for the first time in twenty years, I'm overcome with emotion）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・②<b>is not negative</b>. 「<span class='gd-jp'>感謝に堪えない</span>」 doesn't mean “so thankful it's unbearable / annoying”. It means “so grateful my heart can't hold it”, which is the same as “extremely grateful”.<br>・Don't use it for physical endurance. For “I can't stand this heat”, say <span class='gd-jp'>暑くてたまらない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>暑さに耐えられない</span>, not 暑さに堪えない.<br>・①criticizes the thing itself as too awful or too tragic. It doesn't mean “I don't have time to watch” or “I don't want to watch”.<br>・②is very formal. Saying 「感謝に堪えない」 to a friend sounds like you're giving a speech. In daily life, <span class='gd-jp'>本当にありがとう</span> is enough.<br>・②needs a kanji emotion noun like 「感謝」 or 「遺憾」 in front. It can't attach directly to an adjective, so you can't say 悲しいに堪えない.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same “accident”. The three patterns feel different:<br><b>に堪えない</b> = expressing your own <b>very deep feeling</b> in a formal setting, like an official statement:<br><span class='gd-jp'>このような事故が起きたことは、誠に遺憾に堪えません。</span>（That such an accident happened is deeply regrettable）<br><b>を禁じ得ない</b> = something makes an emotion <b>well up involuntarily</b>, with the focus on “can't hold it back”:<br><span class='gd-jp'>遺族の話を聞いて、涙を禁じ得なかった。</span>（Hearing the bereaved family's story, I couldn't hold back my tears）<br><b>てたまらない</b> = the colloquial “so ～ I can't stand it”, for personal feelings or physical sensations:<br><span class='gd-jp'>あの映像を思い出すと、つらくてたまらない。</span>（When I remember that footage, it hurts so much I can't bear it）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: for “deeply ～” in a statement or speech, use に堪えない. For “can't help ～ing”, use を禁じ得ない. For “so ～ I could die” with a friend, use てたまらない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>In the affirmative form 「〜に堪える」, the meaning becomes “withstand ～, be worth ～”: <span class='gd-jp'>大人の鑑賞に堪える作品だ。</span>（This is a work worthy of adult appreciation）. 「〜に足る」 is close in meaning, so it's worth studying together.</div>","n1-39":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てやまない」 is like English <b>“sincerely hope ～” or “never cease to ～”</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>やむ</span> means “stop”, as in <span class='gd-jp'>雨がやむ</span> (the rain stops). So <span class='gd-jp'>願ってやまない</span> literally means “the wish won't stop”, that is, a deep, intense feeling you keep in your heart all the time. It's a formal, written expression, common in speeches, congratulatory messages and essays.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb て-form + やまない. It's usually written in hiragana as <span class='gd-jp'>やまない</span>, and rarely in kanji.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>願う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>願って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>願ってやまない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>期待する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>期待して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>期待してやまない</span></span><br>In formal settings, when speaking to someone, use the polite form <span class='gd-jp'>やみません</span>. For past feelings, use <span class='gd-jp'>やまなかった</span>.<br>Almost always it follows a verb of feeling: <span class='gd-jp'>願う</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>祈る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>望む</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>期待する</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>愛する</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>尊敬する</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>信じる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>憧れる</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll most often see it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Speeches, congratulations</span>Wishing someone well. This is the most typical use.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皆様のご健康を祈ってやみません。</span>（I sincerely wish you all good health）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Your own conviction</span>Used in writing to express a long-held belief.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は彼の才能を信じてやまない。</span>（I have always believed in his talent）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Captivating</span>The subject is a thing, and it means the thing keeps drawing people in deeply.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この町の景色は、今も多くの人を魅了してやまない。</span>（The scenery of this town still captivates so many people）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Modifying a noun</span>When talking about someone else's feelings, it often goes before a noun as a modifier.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>祖父が愛してやまなかった海</span>（The sea my grandfather loved all his life）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It may be glossed as “nonstop”, but it is <b>not</b> for an action that keeps going. <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ってやまない</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>走ってやまない</span> are both wrong. For rain that keeps falling, just say <span class='gd-jp'>雨がやまない</span>. It's only for <b>feelings and wishes</b>.<br>・It attaches only to verbs, not adjectives. “So happy I can't stand it” can't be 嬉しくてやまない. Say <span class='gd-jp'>嬉しくてたまらない</span>.<br>・It's very formal. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>君を愛してやまない</span> to a friend in casual chat would sound like you're reading from a script.<br>・In formal settings, when speaking to someone, use <span class='gd-jp'>願ってやみません</span>. Don't say 願ってやまないです.<br>・At the end of a sentence, the subject is usually “I”. Other people's feelings mostly appear in writing, or before a noun as in the example above.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same topic, “hometown”. The three feelings differ:<br><b>てやまない</b> = a deep feeling held in the heart for a long time, written style: <span class='gd-jp'>故郷を愛してやまない。</span>（I love my hometown dearly）<br><b>てたまらない</b> (N2) = a feeling right now that's so strong you can't stand it, and it works in speech too: <span class='gd-jp'>故郷に帰りたくてたまらない。</span>（I'm dying to go back to my hometown）<br><b>を禁じ得ない</b> = a feeling that wells up involuntarily when you see something: <span class='gd-jp'>故郷の変わりように驚きを禁じ得ない。</span>（Seeing how my hometown has changed, I can't help but be surprised）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: for long-held wishes, love and beliefs, use <span class='gd-jp'>てやまない</span>. For a feeling that is intense right now, such as distress or a strong craving, use <span class='gd-jp'>てたまらない</span>. For a reaction you can't help when something moves you, use <span class='gd-jp'>を禁じ得ない</span>.</div>","n1-56":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜なくして（は）」means <b>\"without ~, there's no way…\"</b>. You put something very important in front, then say \"without it, this simply can't happen\" to stress that <b>this thing is absolutely indispensable</b>. The tone is very formal, and you'll mostly see it in essays, speeches and award acceptance remarks.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Attach なくして directly to a noun, <b>with no 「が」 or 「を」 in between</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>努力</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>努力なくして</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>協力</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>協力なくしては</span></span><br>What follows is always an \"impossible / can't be done\" expression. The most common are <span class='gd-jp'>あり得ない</span> (can't exist), <span class='gd-jp'>できない</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>語れない</span> (can't be talked about).<br>To put an action in front, use \"dictionary form + ことなくして\": <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>苦労する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>苦労することなくして</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Making an argument</span>Explaining the conditions you can't do without behind a success or result.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日々の努力なくして成功はあり得ない。</span>(Without daily effort, success is impossible.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>信頼関係なくして、いいチームは作れない。</span>(Without mutual trust, you can't build a good team.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>苦労することなくして、本当の喜びは味わえない。</span>(Without hardship, you can't taste true joy.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Thank-you speeches</span>Looking back and saying \"it wouldn't have been possible without you.\" The ending is often in the past tense.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皆様のご協力なくしては、このプロジェクトは成功しませんでした。</span>(Without everyone's cooperation, this project would not have succeeded.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Evaluation</span>Saying that a person or thing is so important you can't leave them out.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の存在なくして、日本のサッカーの歴史は語れない。</span>(You can't tell the history of Japanese soccer without mentioning him.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What follows must be \"impossible / can't be done.\" You <b>can't</b> say 「努力なくして成功した」, because that contradicts itself.<br>・Don't add 「を」: <span class='gd-jp'>財布をなくして困った</span> means \"I lost my wallet and was in trouble.\" That's the verb <span class='gd-jp'>なくす</span> (to lose), which has nothing to do with this grammar.<br>・Put something <b>truly important</b> in front, like effort, love, cooperation or trust. With something trivial like an umbrella it sounds exaggerated; in daily life just say <span class='gd-jp'>傘がないと出かけられない</span>.<br>・It's stiff, so you won't use it when chatting with friends. In casual speech, 「〜がなかったら、〜できない」 is enough.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three can thank people for their help:<br><b>なくしては</b> = without ~, it can't be done (most formal): <span class='gd-jp'>皆様の協力なくしては、成功できませんでした。</span><br><b>なしには</b> = almost the same meaning, a little less stiff: <span class='gd-jp'>皆様の協力なしには、成功できませんでした。</span><br><b>あっての</b> = the reverse angle, \"B exists only because of A\": <span class='gd-jp'>皆様の協力あっての成功でした。</span> (This success was thanks to everyone's help.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Note that 「なしに」 without は can also simply mean \"did ~ without doing ~ first\": <span class='gd-jp'>連絡なしに休んだ。</span> (I took the day off without telling anyone.)<br>なくして has no such use; it's only for \"it's impossible without it.\"</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what follows is \"impossible / can't be done,\" both なくして and なしには work, and なくして is more formal. If it's \"just did it without ~,\" only なしに works. If you want to talk from the \"having it\" side, use あっての.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜あっての」 (B exists thanks to A) is the flip side of なくして, so it's best to learn the two together: <span class='gd-jp'>お客様あっての商売だ。</span> (Business exists because of customers.)</div>","n1-57":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「にして」 is a very stiff <b>written-style</b> expression, a bit like literary English: \"only at fifty did he…\", \"in a single night\", \"at once a doctor and a writer.\" It has three common meanings: <b>\"only when/at ~\"</b> (including \"only ~ can\"), <b>\"in an instant\"</b>, and <b>\"both ~ and ~\"</b>. The first two often carry a sense of \"that's quite something / I never expected that.\" \"Both ~ and ~\" simply lines up two roles or qualities; it's formal and dignified and doesn't necessarily imply surprise.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Basically it follows a noun: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>50歳</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>50歳にして</span></span><br>The \"both ~ and ~\" use also often follows na-adjectives (adjectives that take な, like <span class='gd-jp'>簡潔な</span>). Drop the な and attach it: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>簡潔な</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>簡潔にして</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Only when ~</span>Put a \"stage\" in front, such as an age, an attempt number, or a year. It's often followed by <span class='gd-jp'>初めて</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>ようやく</span>, meaning \"only after getting this far did it finally…\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>三度目の挑戦にして、ようやく合格した。</span>(It took until my third attempt to finally pass.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>50歳にして初めて海外に行った。</span>(He went abroad for the first time at 50.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Followed by <span class='gd-jp'>なお</span>, it becomes \"even at this stage, still…\":</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は90歳にしてなお現役の医者だ。</span>(Even at 90, he is still an active doctor.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Only ~ can</span>Put a person in front to mean \"only this kind of person could do it.\" It's often written 「〜にして初めて」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは彼にして初めてできる仕事だ。</span>(This is work that only he could do.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この親にしてこの子あり。</span>(Like father, like son.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In an instant</span>It follows very short spans of time like <span class='gd-jp'>一瞬</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>一夜</span>, meaning \"in the blink of an eye…\". In speech, people just say <span class='gd-jp'>一瞬で</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>町は一瞬にして炎に包まれた。</span>(The whole town was engulfed in flames in an instant.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は一夜にしてスターになった。</span>(She became a star overnight.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Both ~ and ~</span>「AにしてB」 = having both A and B at once. It means the same as 「で」: <span class='gd-jp'>医者で作家</span> is the everyday way to say it, while <span class='gd-jp'>医者にして作家</span> is the formal, written way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は医者にして作家でもある。</span>(He is both a doctor and a writer.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>説明は簡潔にして明快だった。</span>(The explanation was concise and clear.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>幸いにして</span> (fortunately) and <span class='gd-jp'>不幸にして</span> (unfortunately) are fixed phrases; just memorize them as single words:<br><span class='gd-jp'>幸いにして、けが人はいなかった。</span>(Fortunately, no one was injured.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・For \"only when ~,\" put a <b>life stage or a count</b> in front (an age, which attempt, which year). You can't just drop in a clock time; 「3時にして」 is wrong.<br>・This is the language of writing, speeches and news. Using it with friends sounds affected; in speech, 「で」 or 「〜歳で初めて」 is enough.<br>・With na-adjectives, drop the な: it's <span class='gd-jp'>簡潔にして</span>, not 「簡潔なにして」.<br>・Don't confuse it with the te-form of 「〜にする」: <span class='gd-jp'>ジュースにして飲む</span> means \"make it into juice and drink it,\" which has nothing to do with this grammar. Judge by context.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にして</b> = something happens <b>only</b> at this stage:<br><span class='gd-jp'>50歳にして初めてパソコンを買った。</span>(I bought a computer for the first time at 50.)<br><b>にしては</b> = \"for ~, it's rather…\", a comment that differs from the usual expectation:<br><span class='gd-jp'>50歳にしては体力がある。</span>(For a 50-year-old, he has good stamina.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if 「初めて／ようやく／なお」 follows and says something finally happened or is still true, it's にして. If what follows evaluates \"how it compares with the norm,\" it's にしては. One extra 「は」 changes the meaning a lot.</div>","n1-59":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜には当たらない」means <b>\"there's no need to ～\" or \"no reason to ～\"</b>. You use it to comment calmly: this isn't that big a deal, and <b>that kind of reaction is overblown</b>. For example, no need to be surprised, no need to be sad, no need to scold anyone. It's close to saying \"What's the big deal?\" in English, but the tone is more formal, so you'll see it mostly in essays, commentary, and formal speech.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb dictionary form (the base form you look up in a dictionary) + には当たらない<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>驚く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>驚くには当たらない</span></span> (no need to be surprised)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>悲観する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>悲観するには当たらない</span></span> (no need to be pessimistic)<br>For a politer tone, say <span class='gd-jp'>〜には当たりません</span>. The 「は」 is sometimes dropped, as in <span class='gd-jp'>驚くに当たらない</span>, with the same meaning.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Commenting on an overblown reaction</span>Usually the reason comes first, like \"because that's just how it is\" or \"it's only this much\", and then the conclusion follows: \"so there's no need to ～\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼はずっと努力してきたのだから、合格したのも驚くには当たらない。</span> (He's been working hard all along, so it's no surprise that he passed.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一度失敗したくらいで、悲観するには当たらない。</span> (You've only failed once, so there's no need to be pessimistic.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもが言ったことだから、そんなに責めるには当たらない。</span> (It's just something a child said, so there's no need to blame them that much.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most often</div><div class='gd-sec'>The verb in front is often a \"reaction\" verb: <span class='gd-jp'>驚く</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>嘆く</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>悲観する</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>責める</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>非難する</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>褒める</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>感心する</span>.<br>The reason part often pairs with <span class='gd-jp'>〜くらいで</span> (just because of something this small), <span class='gd-jp'>〜からといって</span> (even if it's because of ～), and <span class='gd-jp'>〜のだから</span> (since ～).</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The <span class='gd-jp'>当たる</span> here has nothing to do with \"winning a prize\" or \"hitting a target\". Learn the whole phrase as one fixed expression.<br>・What comes before it is a <b>reaction to something, or a judgment of it</b>. For \"no need to make a special trip\", meaning \"you don't have to go out of your way to do something\", Japanese usually uses <span class='gd-jp'>〜には及ばない</span>, not this one (compared below).<br>・With a noun, <span class='gd-jp'>に当たらない</span> often means something else: \"doesn't count as ～, doesn't amount to ～\". For example, <span class='gd-jp'>これは違反には当たらない。</span> (This doesn't count as a violation.) As a beginner, just remember <b>verb dictionary form + には当たらない = no need to ～</b>.<br>・Translating it as \"not worth ～\" can mislead you into thinking \"this thing has no value\". What it really means is \"that reaction is unnecessary and unreasonable\", so \"no need to ～\" is more accurate.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar-looking phrases</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>には及ばない</b> = no need to go to the trouble of doing (often caring, said to the other person)<br>Situation: a subordinate made a small mistake.<br>The boss says to the subordinate: <span class='gd-jp'>わざわざ謝りに来るには及ばないよ。</span> (No need to come all the way to apologize.)<br>A colleague comments on the matter: <span class='gd-jp'>一度のミスで、そこまで彼を責めるには当たらない。</span> (It was just one mistake, so there's no need to blame him that much.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に足りない</b> = the target itself isn't significant enough to ～, not worth ～<br>Situation: there's a match tomorrow.<br><span class='gd-jp'>あんな相手は恐れるに足りない。</span> (An opponent like that is nothing to fear: the point is that the opponent is weak.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>初めての試合だからといって、恐れるには当たらない。</span> (Even though it's your first match, there's no need to be afraid: the point is that the fear is unnecessary.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: to say \"that reaction isn't called for\", use には当たらない. To say \"no need to go to the trouble of doing it\", use には及ばない. To say \"the target itself has little weight\", use に足りない.</div>","n1-62":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜がてら」means <b>\"while ～\" or \"on the side of ～\"</b>. You head out once and take care of two things in one go: buying bread on a walk, or chatting while seeing a friend off at the station. The key is that <b>one action serves two purposes</b>, like killing two birds with one stone.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun + がてら</span>This is the most common pattern. The noun in front is an \"action\" noun, such as <span class='gd-jp'>散歩</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>買い物</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>見物</span> (sightseeing), <span class='gd-jp'>運動</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>ドライブ</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>散歩</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>散歩がてら</span></span> (while taking a walk)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>散歩がてら、パンを買ってきた。</span> (I went for a walk and bought some bread on the way back.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>花見がてら、久しぶりに実家に帰った。</span> (I went back to my family home for the first time in a while, and saw the cherry blossoms while I was at it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ます-stem + がてら</span>With verbs, drop 「ます」 and add がてら directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>送ります</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>送りがてら</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅まで送りがてら、ちょっと話そうか。</span> (I'll walk you to the station, so let's chat a bit on the way.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half usually involves <b>moving or going out</b>, such as <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>来る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>寄る</span> (to drop by), <span class='gd-jp'>歩く</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>出かける</span>. That's because \"on the side\" really means \"since I'm going out anyway, I'll do this too\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>運動がてら、一駅歩いて帰った。</span> (For the exercise, I walked one extra station on the way home.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What comes before it must be an <b>action</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>散歩がてら</span> works, but words that aren't actions, like 「雨がてら」 or 「休みがてら」, can't be used.<br>・The two things must be done by the <b>same person</b>.<br>・It isn't strictly written language. You'll hear it often in everyday conversation, and it's only slightly more mature-sounding than <span class='gd-jp'>ついでに</span>.<br>・It describes doing something on the side <b>this one time</b>, not doing two things in parallel over the long term. For long-term, use <span class='gd-jp'>かたわら</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar-looking phrases</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take \"going shopping and stopping by the post office\":<br><b>ついでに</b> = the most casual and handy one. The main trip is shopping, and the post office is incidental: <span class='gd-jp'>買い物のついでに、郵便局に寄った。</span><br><b>がてら</b> = one trip out with two purposes: <span class='gd-jp'>買い物がてら、郵便局に寄った。</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>かたがた</b> = very formal, mostly used for visits and greetings, often in business settings or letters: <span class='gd-jp'>お礼かたがた、ご挨拶に伺いました。</span> (I came to thank you, and to pay my respects as well.)<br><b>かたわら</b> = long-term, doing one thing as your main job while also doing another: <span class='gd-jp'>会社に勤めるかたわら、小説を書いている。</span> (I write novels while working at a company.)<br><b>ながら</b> = doing two actions at the same time: <span class='gd-jp'>歩きながら電話する。</span> (I talk on the phone while walking.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: for everyday \"while I'm at it\", use ついでに or がてら. For visits and greetings to clients or elders, use かたがた. For doing both over the long term, use かたわら. For two simultaneous physical actions, use ながら.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜かたがた</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜かたわら</span>. Memorize all three together, and multiple-choice questions on the exam will be easy to sort out.</div>","n1-64":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ずくめ」is like English <b>\"all in black,\" \"nothing but ~,\" \"~ from start to finish\"</b>. Looking at the whole picture, it's all one kind of thing: dressed head to toe in black, a whole year of one good thing after another, or rules everywhere you turn.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Just add ずくめ to a noun. It's that simple.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>黒</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>黒ずくめ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いいこと</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>いいことずくめ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>規則</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>規則ずくめ</span></span><br>Once attached, it behaves like an ordinary noun:<br>Before a noun, add「の」：<span class='gd-jp'>黒ずくめの男</span> (a man dressed all in black)<br>At the end of a sentence, add「だ」：<span class='gd-jp'>いいことずくめだ</span><br>When the sentence continues, use「で」：<span class='gd-jp'>規則ずくめで、疲れる</span></div><div class='gd-h'>The patterns you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'>ずくめ <b>doesn't attach to just any noun</b>. Japanese speakers mostly use a handful of set combinations, so the most practical thing is to memorize the ones below first.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Colors, clothing</span>The whole outfit is one color.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>黒ずくめの男が店に入ってきた。</span> (A man dressed all in black walked into the shop.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Good things</span>One good thing after another.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今年は結婚に昇進に、いいことずくめだった。</span> (This year I got married and promoted. Nothing but good news.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>旅行中はごちそうずくめで、3キロも太った。</span> (Every meal on the trip was a feast, and I gained a full 3 kg.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Rules, restrictions</span>Being controlled at every turn, usually with a complaint about feeling tied down.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの会社は規則ずくめで、息が詰まる。</span> (My company is full of rules. It's suffocating.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Common in the news</span>Describing an event with many things that differ from the usual.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今回の大会は異例ずくめだった。</span> (This tournament was unusual in every respect.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>As you can see, ずくめ <b>is neither good nor bad in itself</b>. The point is simply \"uniformly the same throughout.\" <span class='gd-jp'>黒ずくめ</span> is neutral, <span class='gd-jp'>いいことずくめ</span> is positive, and <span class='gd-jp'>規則ずくめ</span> leans negative.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches only to <b>nouns</b>. To say \"all delicious things,\" say <span class='gd-jp'>美味しいものずくめ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>ごちそうずくめ</span>. You can't attach it straight to an adjective, as in 美味しいずくめ.<br>・<b>Dirt stuck on you</b> doesn't take ずくめ: \"covered in mud\" is <span class='gd-jp'>泥まみれ</span>, not 泥ずくめ; \"drenched in sweat\" is <span class='gd-jp'>汗まみれ</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>結構ずくめ</span> (conditions so good you can't find fault) often carries a hint of suspicion: <span class='gd-jp'>結構ずくめの話には気をつけたほうがいい。</span> (You should be careful about deals that sound too good to be true.)<br>・You'll occasionally see it written「づくめ」. It's the same thing, but the usual spelling today is ずくめ.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same piece of clothing and see how the three expressions differ:<br><b>ずくめ</b> = uniformly one thing throughout: <span class='gd-jp'>黒ずくめの服</span> (an outfit that's black from head to toe)<br><b>まみれ</b> = the surface is coated with liquid or powdery stuff, almost always negative: <span class='gd-jp'>泥まみれの服</span> (clothes covered in mud)<br><b>だらけ</b> = so much of something that it's everywhere, mostly something undesirable: <span class='gd-jp'>しわだらけの服</span> (clothes full of wrinkles)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: uniformly one thing (outfits, good things, rules, and other set combinations) → ずくめ; stuck on the surface, coated all over → まみれ; so many that they're everywhere → だらけ.</div>","n1-63":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜まみれ」is like English <b>\"covered in mud,\" \"drenched in sweat,\" \"soaked in blood\"</b>. The key is that things like mud, sweat, blood, or dust <b>stick to the surface and coat it all over</b>, and they're usually dirty or unpleasant.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Body or object covered in something dirty</span>The most basic use. Words that often go with it: <span class='gd-jp'>泥</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>汗</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>血</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>ほこり</span> (dust), <span class='gd-jp'>油</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>泥まみれになって、子供と遊んだ。</span> (I played with the kids and got covered in mud.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>汗まみれで家に帰ってきた。</span> (I came home drenched in sweat.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>倉庫からほこりまみれのアルバムが出てきた。</span> (A dust-covered photo album turned up from the storeroom.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Figurative: tangled up in something bad</span>You can also use it for abstract things, as if you're wrapped up in debt or lies and can't shake them off.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は借金まみれだ。</span> (He's up to his neck in debt.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの人の話は嘘まみれだ。</span> (Everything that person says is a pack of lies.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Simple: <b>add まみれ directly to a noun</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泥</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泥まみれ</span></span><br>After that, treat it as a noun. The most common forms are:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>〜まみれになる</span>: to end up covered in ~<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>〜まみれだ</span>: (right now) covered in ~<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>〜まみれで</span>: while covered in ~ (doing something)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>〜まみれの＋名詞</span>: (something) covered in ~　Example: <span class='gd-jp'>血まみれのシャツ</span> (a blood-soaked shirt)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・まみれ gives the feeling of <b>something stuck on, clinging to the surface</b>, so don't use it for things that are merely \"many.\" For a crowd, say <span class='gd-jp'>人でいっぱい</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>人だらけ</span>, not 人まみれ.<br>・For things that aren't \"stuck on,\" like wounds or typos, use <span class='gd-jp'>だらけ</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>傷だらけ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>間違いだらけ</span>.<br>・It's negative. Good or pretty things don't normally take まみれ, so for positive ideas like \"covered in flowers\" or \"full of happiness,\" use another expression.<br>・Add「の」before a noun: it's <span class='gd-jp'>血まみれのシャツ</span>, not 血まみれシャツ.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>だらけ</b> = <b>lots of, all over the place</b> (the focus is on large quantity, scattered everywhere): <span class='gd-jp'>この作文は間違いだらけだ。</span> (This essay is riddled with mistakes.)<br><b>まみれ</b> = <b>coated, stuck all over the surface</b> (the focus is on being covered by liquid or powdery stuff): <span class='gd-jp'>顔が汗まみれだ。</span> (His face is drenched in sweat.)<br>For things like mud, sweat, and blood, both work: <span class='gd-jp'>泥だらけ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>泥まみれ</span> are both fine, but まみれ paints a stronger picture of being thickly coated.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ずくめ</b> = <b>uniformly one thing from start to finish</b> (not necessarily negative; it can be good things too): <span class='gd-jp'>黒ずくめの服</span> (an all-black outfit), <span class='gd-jp'>いいことずくめ</span> (nothing but good things)<br><b>まみれ</b>, on the other hand, means being coated in something dirty: <span class='gd-jp'>泥まみれの服</span> (clothes covered in mud)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: stuck on you, coated all over → まみれ; large quantity, everywhere → だらけ; uniformly one thing, all of it → ずくめ.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The pattern most often compared with this one is <span class='gd-jp'>〜ずくめ</span> (uniformly ~, nothing but ~). Take a look at it next. Memorizing the two together is the best way to avoid mixing them up.</div>","n1-69":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜を皮切りに」means <b>\"starting with ~\"</b> or <b>\"kicking off with ~\"</b>. Think of a concert: \"We're starting with the Taipei show, then touring the whole country.\" The Taipei show is the 「皮切り」, the first leg. What matters is that <b>a whole series of things follows</b>.</div><div class='gd-sec'>「皮切り」originally meant the first moxibustion burn, the hottest and most painful one. It later came to mean \"the first in a series of events.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Events, tours</span>The first stop starts things off, and the rest follow one by one.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>東京公演を皮切りに、全国ツアーが始まった。</span>(Starting with the Tokyo show, the nationwide tour got under way.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Business expansion</span>It starts with the first store or the first product, then grows from there.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は渋谷店を皮切りに、各地に店舗を増やしている。</span>(Starting with its Shibuya store, this chain has been opening branches around the country.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>One person leads, others follow</span>Someone speaks up first, and then everyone else chimes in.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の発言を皮切りに、次々と意見が出た。</span>(Once he spoke up, opinions came out one after another.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half of the sentence often includes words like <span class='gd-jp'>次々と</span> (one after another), <span class='gd-jp'>相次いで</span> (in succession) or <span class='gd-jp'>各地で</span> (in various places), to show that \"a lot more is coming.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It attaches directly to a noun: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>東京公演</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>東京公演を皮切りに</span></span><br>If you mean an action, you usually use its noun form: <span class='gd-jp'>発売</span> (release), <span class='gd-jp'>開店</span> (store opening), <span class='gd-jp'>発言</span> (remark).<br>Adding 「して」 means the same thing, so both forms work. 「を皮切りに」 without 「して」 is more concise and more common in writing: <span class='gd-jp'>東京公演を皮切りにして</span>.<br>You will also see <span class='gd-jp'>を皮切りとして</span>, which means the same.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>There must be a \"series\" afterward</b>. If only one thing happens, you can't use it. <span class='gd-jp'>入学を皮切りに、一人暮らしを始めた</span> is wrong because only one thing follows. Change it to <span class='gd-jp'>入学をきっかけに</span>.<br>・It isn't simply \"starting from ~\". For a starting point in time, 「から」 is enough: <span class='gd-jp'>月曜日から仕事が始まる</span>. Don't say 月曜日を皮切りに.<br>・<b>It's rarely used for chains of bad events</b>. For earthquakes, accidents and the like happening one after another, Japanese speakers generally don't use 皮切り. It's mostly used for events, business, competitions and other things that \"unfold step by step.\"<br>・It sounds fairly formal and is common in news, reports and introductions. You'll rarely hear it in chats with friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same concert: with different grammar, the meaning changes a lot.<br><b>を皮切りに</b> = this show is the <b>first stop</b>, with many more to come:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そのライブを皮切りに、彼らは全国を回った。</span>(Starting with that concert, they toured the whole country.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>をきっかけに</b> = because of this event, <b>some change happened</b> (not necessarily a series):</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そのライブをきっかけに、彼らは有名になった。</span>(Thanks to that concert, they became famous.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>を限りに</b> = the exact opposite: it's the <b>last time</b>, the end of the line:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日のライブを限りに、活動を休止する。</span>(Today's concert is the last one; after it, they'll go on hiatus.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use を皮切りに for \"first stop, with a string of more to come\"; use をきっかけに for \"changed because of that event\"; use を限りに for \"the last time, ending here.\"</div>","n1-75":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜きらいがある」means <b>\"has a tendency to ~\"</b> or <b>\"is prone to ~\"</b>, and it is always a <b>negative</b> tendency. The tone is like a teacher writing in a report card, \"This student has a tendency to be hasty.\" It points out a flaw, but politely and indirectly. You'll mostly see it in writing, in commentary, or in fairly formal speech.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb dictionary form + きらいがある　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>抱え込む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>抱え込むきらいがある</span></span><br>Verb ない-form + きらいがある　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞かないきらいがある</span></span><br>Noun + の + きらいがある　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>独断</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>独断のきらいがある</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は何でも一人で抱え込むきらいがある。</span>(He tends to take everything on by himself.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は人の話を最後まで聞かないきらいがある。</span>(She tends not to listen to people until the end.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼のやり方は独断のきらいがある。</span>(His way of doing things is a bit high-handed.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most often</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜すぎるきらいがある</span>Especially common when criticizing \"going too far.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの部長は細かいことを気にしすぎるきらいがある。</span>(Our department head tends to worry too much about small details.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Talking about a group</span>Used to comment on the overall style of a group, an organization or society.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最近のニュースは話題性を重視しすぎるきらいがある。</span>(Recent news tends to put too much emphasis on being talked about.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It's written with the kanji 「嫌い」, but here it <b>has nothing to do with \"dislike\"</b>. It means \"tendency, flaw.\" Today it's mostly written in hiragana as <span class='gd-jp'>きらい</span>.<br>・It only works for <b>negative</b> tendencies. You can't use it to say someone is considerate. Say <span class='gd-jp'>優しいところがある</span> instead.<br>・It describes a person's (or group's) <b>usual style or personality</b>, not something that happened once. You can't use きらいがある for \"He was late yesterday.\"<br>・With a noun you must add 「の」: it's <span class='gd-jp'>独断のきらいがある</span>, not 「独断きらいがある」.<br>・It sounds stiff. When chatting with friends and saying \"He tends to overthink,\" <span class='gd-jp'>考えすぎるところがある</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>考えすぎがちだ</span> sounds more natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of these describe a person who easily leans toward the negative:<br><b>がち</b> = \"often ~, tends to ~\". The focus is on <b>frequency</b>, and you can use it in everyday conversation: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は物事を悲観的に考えがちだ。</span> (He often looks at things pessimistically.)<br><b>きらいがある</b> = \"has a tendency to ~\". It carries a flavor of <b>gently commenting</b> on someone's character, and it's more of a written style: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は物事を悲観的に考えるきらいがある。</span> (He has a tendency to look at things pessimistically.)<br><b>気味</b> = \"a slight feeling of ~\". It describes a <b>mild state right now</b>, not a personality trait: <span class='gd-jp'>最近、ちょっと疲れ気味だ。</span> (I've been feeling a bit tired lately.)<br>Also, がち can describe weather and natural phenomena (<span class='gd-jp'>冬は空気が乾燥しがちだ</span>, the air tends to be dry in winter), while きらいがある is basically only used to comment on the style of people or groups.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: in conversation, use がち for \"often ~\" and 気味 for \"a bit ~\". Use きらいがある when you're writing a commentary or politely pointing out a person's or group's \"bad habit.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>It often appears together with 「ともすると・ともすれば」 (before you know it, ~): <span class='gd-jp'>人はともすると、楽な方を選ぶきらいがある。</span> (People tend to pick the easier path before they know it.)</div>","n1-80":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にとどまらず」is the news-style <b>“not limited to ~, but extending to ~.”</b> <span class='gd-jp'>とどまる</span> means “to stay where you are,” so <span class='gd-jp'>とどまらず</span> means “it doesn't stop there.” You first name a smaller scope, then follow with a bigger one, like ripples spreading outward on water.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Widening scope</span>It's most often used for things that “spread”: influence, damage, popularity, events. You'll see it a lot in news, reports and speeches. It's written and formal.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女の人気は日本にとどまらず、アジア全体に広がっている。</span>(Her popularity isn't limited to Japan. It has spread across all of Asia.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この問題は一つの会社にとどまらず、業界全体に関わる。</span>(This problem doesn't involve just one company. It affects the whole industry.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Going a step further with an action</span>You can also put an action before it, meaning “not only did ~, but went even further and ~.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ボランティアたちは物資を届けるにとどまらず、被災者の話にも耳を傾けた。</span>(The volunteers didn't just deliver supplies. They also listened to the survivors' stories.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun ＋ にとどまらず: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>国内</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>国内にとどまらず</span></span><br>Verb dictionary form (the base form you'd look up in a dictionary) ＋ にとどまらず: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>届ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>届けるにとどまらず</span></span><br>You'll also often see <span class='gd-jp'>国内だけにとどまらず</span> with 「だけ」 added. The meaning is the same, just a bit more emphatic.</div><div class='gd-sec'>At the end of a sentence or when modifying a noun, it becomes <b>にとどまらない</b>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の才能は音楽にとどまらない。</span>(His talent isn't limited to music.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It's <span class='gd-jp'>とどまる</span>, not <span class='gd-jp'>とまる</span>. <span class='gd-jp'>涙が止まらない</span> means “the tears won't stop,” which has nothing to do with this grammar. Don't drop the 「とど」.<br>・You must follow it with a <b>bigger scope</b>, often with 「も」「まで」「へと」: <span class='gd-jp'>海外にも</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>全国へと</span>. If you say only the first half, the sentence feels unfinished.<br>・It's very formal, so don't use it for small everyday things. For “I didn't just eat ramen, I had sushi too,” just say <span class='gd-jp'>ラーメンだけじゃなくて、寿司も食べた</span>. Using にとどまらず there would sound like you're reading a press release.<br>・The affirmative <span class='gd-jp'>にとどまる</span> (without the negative) is also common, with the opposite meaning: “stay limited to ~.” <span class='gd-jp'>今回の値上げは一部の商品にとどまる。</span>(This price increase is limited to some products.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Let's say an anime is popular:<br><b>だけでなく</b> = the most ordinary “not only ~ but also ~,” usable in any situation: <span class='gd-jp'>このアニメは子どもだけでなく、大人にも人気だ。</span>(This anime is popular not only with kids but with adults too.)<br><b>に限らず</b> = not limited to one category, <b>and the same goes for others</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>このアニメは子どもに限らず、大人にも人気だ。</span>(It's not just kids. Adults like it too, so basically everyone does.)<br><b>にとどまらず</b> = the scope <b>spreads outward</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>このアニメの人気は日本にとどまらず、世界中に広がっている。</span>(Its popularity isn't limited to Japan. It has spread all over the world.)<br><b>はおろか</b> = the first item goes without saying, and <b>even the most extreme case</b> is true too: <span class='gd-jp'>このアニメは、子どもはおろか、お年寄りまで知っている。</span>(Never mind kids, even elderly people know this anime.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use にとどまらず when influence or scope keeps growing; に限らず for “not limited to any one type”; はおろか for “even the most extreme case”; and when in doubt, だけでなく is the safest.</div>","n1-81":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜すら」means <b>“even ~…”</b>. You pick the most basic example, the one least likely to be a problem, to show how extreme the situation is: <span class='gd-jp'>名前すら知らない</span> means you don't even know the name, so you certainly don't know anything else. The meaning is close to 「さえ」, but <b>the tone is harder and more literary</b>, and it often appears in articles, news and commentary.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun ＋ すら</span>The most basic pattern. The original 「が」「を」 is simply replaced by すら.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>時間がない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>時間すらない</span></span> (there isn't even time)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しくて、食事する時間すらない。</span>(I'm so busy I don't even have time to eat.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun ＋ ですら</span>Very common when the noun is a person who is performing the action. The 「で」 here doesn't mean “at/in,” so just memorize 「ですら」 as a set.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>専門家ですら答えられない問題だ。</span>(This is a question even experts can't answer.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもですら知っていることだ。</span>(This is something even children know.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun ＋ に・と etc. ＋ すら</span>Particles like 「に」「と」 stay, and すら goes after them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このことは親にすら話していない。</span>(I haven't even told my parents about this.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>て-form ＋ すらいない</span>This emphasizes <b>the action itself</b>: they haven't even gotten as far as doing it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼はメールを読んですらいない。</span>(He hasn't even read the email.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・すら is <b>not only for negatives</b>. Most of the time it's followed by 「ない」, but an affirmative sentence like <span class='gd-jp'>子どもですら知っている</span> is perfectly natural.<br>・You can't drop 「に」「と」: say <span class='gd-jp'>親にすら</span>, not 親すら話していない.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>メールすら読んでいない</span> means “hasn't even read the email” (the focus is the email), while <span class='gd-jp'>メールを読んですらいない</span> means “hasn't even read it” (the focus is the act of reading). Where you put すら changes what you emphasize.<br>・すら sounds stiff. In casual conversation with friends, <span class='gd-jp'>名前も知らない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>名前さえ知らない</span> is more natural.<br>・すら often carries a feeling of surprise, disbelief or criticism, so it's rarely used to praise someone.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>すら</b> and <b>さえ</b> are almost interchangeable in the “even ~” sense:<br><span class='gd-jp'>名前すら知らない。</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>名前さえ知らない。</span> (I don't even know the name.)<br>The difference is that すら is more literary, and さえ also has a use that すら <b>doesn't</b> have: 「〜さえ〜ば」 = “as long as ~.”<br><span class='gd-jp'>時間さえあれば行ける。</span> (As long as I have time, I can go.)<br>You <b>can't</b> replace さえ with すら in this sentence.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for “even ~,” either works, and すら gives more punch in writing. For “as long as ~,” only さえ works.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>すら is often paired with 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜はおろか</span>」 (let alone ~), with the hard thing first and the easy thing after:<br><span class='gd-jp'>漢字はおろか、ひらがなすら書けない。</span> (Let alone kanji, I can't even write hiragana.)</div>","n1-1":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に至っては」 means <b>“as for ~, it's even worse…” or “and ~ is the extreme case, where…”</b>. First you say how things are for everyone or overall, then you pick out <b>the single most extreme case</b> and talk about that.<br>It's like complaining: “Everyone in my family is terrible at getting up in the morning, and <b>my dad is the worst</b>. He doesn't get out of bed until noon.”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + に至っては.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>父</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>父に至っては</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>数学</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>数学に至っては</span></span><br>The whole sentence usually goes: <b>general situation → 〇〇に至っては → the most extreme thing</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの家族はみんな朝が弱い。父に至っては、昼まで起きてこない。</span>(Everyone in my family struggles in the morning. My dad is the worst: he doesn't get up until noon.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>成績が悪く、数学に至っては赤点だ。</span>(My grades are bad, and in math I actually failed.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会議では誰も意見を言わなかった。部長に至っては、最初から最後まで寝ていた。</span>(Nobody gave an opinion in the meeting. The department head was the worst: he slept from start to finish.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half often includes emphatic expressions like 「<span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>まで</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>一度も〜ない</span>」 to make the sense of extremity even stronger.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You must first have a <b>whole group</b> (a group of people, several subjects, a whole situation) before you can “pick out the most extreme one.” If you suddenly say 「父に至っては…」 with nothing before it, a Japanese listener won't know what you're comparing against.<br>・It's almost always used for <b>bad or jaw-dropping things</b>. Using it to praise someone, like “he's even better, he got a perfect score,” sounds odd.<br>・What follows has to be the <b>truly extreme</b> case. If it's only an ordinary level, this pattern sounds like making a big fuss over nothing.<br>・This is a written, formal expression. When chatting with friends, people more often say 「<span class='gd-jp'>父なんか、昼まで寝てるよ</span>」.<br>・It's read <b>いたっては</b>; 「至」 is read いた.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に至っては</b> = <b>picks out the most extreme one</b> from the whole (mostly negative):<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>部屋はどこも汚かった。台所に至っては、足の踏み場もなかった。</span>(Every room was dirty. The kitchen was the worst: there was nowhere to even put your feet.)<br><b>に至るまで</b> = from A to B, <b>everything included</b>; it stresses a wide range and works for good or bad:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>リビングから台所に至るまで、全部きれいに掃除した。</span>(From the living room to the kitchen, I cleaned everything until it was spotless.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ときたら</b> = simply singles out one person to <b>complain about</b>. You don't need to mention a group first, and the tone is more casual and carries dissatisfaction:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの父ときたら、毎晩飲んで帰ってくる。</span>(Speaking of my dad, he comes home drunk every night.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族はみんなお酒好きだが、父に至っては毎晩飲んで帰ってくる。</span>(Everyone in my family loves drinking, but my dad is the extreme case: he comes home drunk every night.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: first “everyone is ~,” then “and so-and-so is even worse” → に至っては; “everything from A to B” → に至るまで; just “honestly, I can't stand so-and-so” → ときたら.</div>","n1-4":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たりとも…ない」 means <b>“not even one ~”</b>, in a more emphatic version. Think of someone slamming the table and saying “Not a single cent!” or “Not one minute is to be wasted!” That <b>firm, no-compromise</b> tone is what たりとも gives in Japanese.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The formula is fixed: <b>「一」 + counter + たりとも + negative</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>一日</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>一日たりとも</span></span> (not even one day)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>一円</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>一円たりとも</span></span> (not even one yen)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>一人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>一人たりとも</span></span> (not even one person)<br>What follows <b>must be negative</b>: things like <span class='gd-jp'>〜ない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜ません</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜できない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>許されない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜するな</span>.<br>The most common combinations: <span class='gd-jp'>一秒</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>一瞬</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>一日</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>一円</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>一人</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>一歩</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>一滴</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>一度</span>. These are all “smallest units,” stressing that not even that tiny amount is acceptable.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Strong resolve</span>You absolutely won't or don't want to do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>残り時間、一秒たりとも無駄にしたくない。</span>(I don't want to waste even one second of the time left.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Rules and orders</span>“Not allowed,” “not permitted.” Very common with bosses, coaches and public notices.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試合が終わるまで、一瞬たりとも気を抜くな。</span>(Until the match is over, don't let your guard down for even a moment.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>医療ミスは一件たりとも許されない。</span>(Not a single medical error can be tolerated.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Strong emotion</span>Often paired with 「〜たことはない」 to express “never once.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>君のことを一日たりとも忘れたことはない。</span>(There hasn't been a single day when I forgot you.)</div><div class='gd-h'>One special fixed expression</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>何人たりとも</span> = “no one, whoever they are, is allowed to ~.” Here 何人 is read <b>なんぴと</b>. It's very stiff, written language, often seen in notices, novels and anime lines.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何人たりとも、ここに立ち入ることは許さない。</span>(No one, whoever they are, is allowed to set foot here.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Only <b>「一」</b> can come before it: <span class='gd-jp'>二日たりとも</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>三人たりとも</span> are both wrong. The whole point is the “smallest amount.”<br>・What follows is <b>always negative</b>. You can't say something positive like 「一日たりとも休む」.<br>・Attach たりとも directly after the counter. Don't add 「も」 or 「の」: 「一日もたりとも」 is wrong.<br>・The tone is heavy and bookish. In everyday chat, <span class='gd-jp'>一円も払わない</span> is enough; switching to たりとも would sound like you're swearing an oath or making a declaration.<br>・To simply describe a fact (for example, “there wasn't a single student in the classroom”), <span class='gd-jp'>一人もいなかった</span> is more natural. たりとも suits resolve, rules and strong emotion.<br>・The 何人 in 何人たりとも is read なんぴと, not なんにん.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Let's compare three ways of talking about money:<br><b>一〜も…ない</b> = simply “there isn't even one,” neutral and everyday: <span class='gd-jp'>財布には一円もない。</span> (There isn't a single yen in my wallet.)<br><b>一〜たりとも…ない</b> = a strong stance, no compromise: <span class='gd-jp'>税金は一円たりとも無駄にできない。</span> (Not even one yen of tax money can be wasted.)<br><b>すら</b> = names one “most basic thing” for emphasis, and doesn't need “一 + counter” before it: <span class='gd-jp'>今月は電車代すらない。</span> (This month I don't even have money for the train.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: just stating “there isn't even one” → も; “一 + counter” with a tone of resolve, prohibition or absolute refusal → たりとも; pointing to “even something this basic ~” → すら.</div>","n1-5":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜であれ」 means <b>\"no matter who/what it is, ...\"</b> or <b>\"even if it's ~, ...\"</b>. For example, \"Everyone has to wait in line, no matter who they are\" or \"Even the company president has to follow the rules.\" Whatever you put in front, the conclusion that follows doesn't change. It's very formal and mostly shows up in news, speeches and writing.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a <b>noun</b> in front and add であれ directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>社長</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>社長であれ</span></span><br>For na-adjectives, drop 「な」 and add であれ: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>有名な</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>有名であれ</span></span><br>You can also use question words like 「誰、何、どんな～」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>誰</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>誰であれ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>どう</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>どうであれ</span></span><br>「であれ」 is actually the imperative form of 「である」 (the written-style version of 「だ」, common in prose). But here it is <b>not telling anyone to do something</b>. It's a concessive use meaning \"it doesn't matter what it is\", i.e. \"even if it's ~\".</div><div class='gd-h'>The three most common patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>たとえ＋noun＋であれ</span>Even if it's some special status or situation, the result is the same. 「たとえ」 (even if) is often added to strengthen it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>たとえ子供であれ、自分のしたことには責任がある。</span>(Even a child is responsible for what they've done.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>誰／何／どんな～＋であれ</span>No matter who or what it is, it's all the same.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰であれ、許可なく入ることはできない。</span>(No matter who you are, you can't enter without permission.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>理由はどうであれ、約束を破ったのは事実だ。</span>(Whatever the reason, the fact is that you broke the promise.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>AであれBであれ</span>Whether it's A or B, the result is the same.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>男であれ女であれ、能力があれば評価される。</span>(Whether you're a man or a woman, you'll be recognized if you have ability.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>晴れであれ雨であれ、試合は予定どおり行う。</span>(Rain or shine, the match will go ahead as planned.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half is usually a <b>conclusion that won't change because of this</b>. Common endings are 「〜できない」, 「許されない」, 「〜べきだ」 and 「変わりはない」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What comes before must be a noun or the stem of a na-adjective. A verb can't connect directly: <b>you can't say</b> 「行くであれ」. If you want a verb there, use 「〜ようが」 or 「〜にせよ」 instead.<br>・It's stiff, and sounds odd in everyday conversation. In speech, use 「でも」 instead, for example: <span class='gd-jp'>誰でも許可が必要だよ。</span> (Everyone needs permission, you know.)<br>・To say \"nobody can get in\", use <span class='gd-jp'>誰も入れない</span>, not <span class='gd-jp'>誰でも入れない</span> (which sounds very unnatural). To say \"not just anyone can get in\", say <span class='gd-jp'>誰でも入れるわけではない</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>であれ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>であれば</span> differ by only one syllable, but the meanings are completely different. であれば means \"if it's ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>社長であれば決められる。</span> (If it's the president, he can decide.) であれ means \"even if it's ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>社長であれ、勝手には決められない。</span> (Even the president can't decide on his own.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to say \"Whatever the reason, being late is not OK\":<br><b>であれ</b>: a noun or na-adjective stem comes before it; the most formal.<br><span class='gd-jp'>どんな理由であれ、遅刻は許されない。</span><br><b>にせよ</b>: can follow a noun or a verb; a little less stiff.<br><span class='gd-jp'>どんな理由にせよ、遅刻はよくない。</span><br><span class='gd-jp'>行くにせよ行かないにせよ、連絡してください。</span> (Whether you go or not, please let me know.)<br><b>ようが／ようと</b>: attaches to the volitional form of a verb (the 〜う／〜よう form), with the feel of \"no matter how much ~, it makes no difference\".<br><span class='gd-jp'>どんな理由があろうと、遅刻は許されない。</span></div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if a noun comes before it and the situation is very formal, use であれ. If you want a verb there, use にせよ or ようが.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Other \"whether A or B\" patterns include 「〜にせよ…にせよ」 and the set phrases with 「〜かれ〜かれ」: <span class='gd-jp'>多かれ少なかれ</span> (more or less) and <span class='gd-jp'>遅かれ早かれ</span> (sooner or later).</div>","n1-6":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とあれば」 means <b>\"if it's ~, then (I'll) ...\"</b>. What comes before it is a <b>special, weighty reason</b>, such as \"for the kids,\" \"the boss's order,\" or \"a close friend's request.\" What follows is \"then I'm willing to do it\" or \"then I have no choice but to do it.\" It has the feel of \"If it's for my children, no hardship is too much.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Nouns attach directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子供のため</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子供のためとあれば</span></span><br>Na-adjectives also attach directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>必要</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>必要とあれば</span></span><br>Verbs use the plain form (dictionary form, ない form or た form): <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>社長が来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>社長が来るとあれば</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Willing to sacrifice</span>For an important person or cause, you'll do anything. It most often appears together with 「のため」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供のためとあれば、親はどんな苦労も厭わない。</span>(If it's for their children, parents will gladly endure any hardship.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族のためとあれば、どんな仕事でもする。</span>(If it's for my family, I'll do any kind of work.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Having to accept</span>The person or situation is special enough that refusing wouldn't be right.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親友の頼みとあれば、断るわけにはいかない。</span>(If it's a close friend asking, I can't very well refuse.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長の命令とあれば、従うしかない。</span>(If it's the president's order, we have no choice but to obey.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'>You'll hear these two set phrases a lot in business settings:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>必要とあれば、いつでもご連絡ください。</span>(If necessary, please contact me at any time.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お客様のご希望とあれば、できる限り対応いたします。</span>(If it's the customer's wish, we'll accommodate it as far as we can.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What comes before must be a <b>weighty reason</b>. Don't use it for ordinary everyday conditions: for \"If it rains, I'll take an umbrella\", say <span class='gd-jp'>雨なら傘を持っていく</span>. 「雨とあれば」 sounds strange.<br>・What follows is usually a <b>resolve or judgment</b>, such as <span class='gd-jp'>何でもする</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>喜んで</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>しかない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>わけにはいかない</span>, not a plain description of something that happened.<br>・After a noun, attach とあれば directly; you don't add だ.<br>・The tone is formal and bookish. With friends, just say <span class='gd-jp'>子供のためなら</span>, which means about the same and is more relaxed.<br>・The \"for\" meaning comes from the <span class='gd-jp'>のため</span> before it. とあれば itself only means \"if it's ~\", so not every sentence has a \"for\" sense.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>「とあれば」 and 「とあって」 both start with 「と＋あ…」 and differ only slightly at the end. 「ともなれば」 also starts with と and looks similar. Let's compare them using the same \"president\" situation:<br><b>とあれば</b> = <b>if it is</b> ~, then... (hypothetical; followed by resolve): <span class='gd-jp'>社長の命令とあれば、従うしかない。</span> (If it's the president's order, we have no choice but to obey.)<br><b>とあって</b> = <b>because it is</b> ~, so... (a fact that has already happened; followed by a description of the situation): <span class='gd-jp'>社長の命令とあって、みんな必死に働いている。</span> (Because it's the president's order, everyone is working frantically.)<br><b>ともなれば</b> = <b>once you reach</b> the status or level of ~, naturally...: <span class='gd-jp'>社長ともなれば、責任も重くなる。</span> (Once you become president, the responsibility naturally gets heavier.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for \"if it's ~, I'm willing / I have no choice\", use とあれば. For \"because it's ~, things are like this now\", use とあって. For \"once you reach the level of ~, naturally...\", use ともなれば.</div>","n1-17":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜そばから」means <b>\"no sooner have you done X than Y undoes it again\"</b>. You're working on something here, and over there it's immediately undone, and <b>it happens every time</b>, so the effort is wasted. Picture yourself mopping the floor, and your kid walks straight over it in slippers. You mop, they stomp, over and over. That helpless feeling is そばから.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add そばから directly after the dictionary form or the た form of a verb.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>片付ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>片付けるそばから</span></span> (just as I tidy up)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>覚えた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>覚えたそばから</span></span> (just after memorizing)<br>The two forms mean about the same. If you really want to split hairs, the dictionary form feels more like \"as I'm doing it, it's being ruined,\" and the た form feels more like \"the moment I finish, it's ruined.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Effort cancelled out</span>You do something, and it's immediately undone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>覚えたそばから忘れてしまう。</span> (I forget things the moment I memorize them.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雪かきしたそばから、また積もっていく。</span> (The moment I finish shoveling snow, it piles up again.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In one end, out the other</span>Something arrives and is gone right away, so you can't hold on to it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>稼ぐそばから使ってしまうので、全然貯金できない。</span> (I spend money as fast as I earn it, so I can't save anything.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>掃除するそばから、犬が毛を落としていく。</span> (As soon as I clean, the dog sheds hair all over again.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half often pairs with <span class='gd-jp'>〜てしまう</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>〜ていく</span>, or uses the passive <span class='gd-jp'>〜される</span> (e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>子供に散らかされる</span>). This conveys the feeling that \"things are out of my control and I can't do anything about it.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・そばから is about things that <b>happen repeatedly</b>. You can't use it for a one-time event. For example, you can't say 「家に着いたそばから雨が降り出した」 for \"It started raining the moment I got home.\" For that, use <span class='gd-jp'>とたん</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>や否や</span>.<br>・It's almost always used in situations of <b>complaint, resignation, or wasted effort</b>. When good things keep coming, you normally wouldn't use そばから.<br>・The second half describes \"how things just are,\" so it can't take expressions of will or command like 「I'll ～」 or 「Please ～」.<br>・The そば here isn't 「蕎麦」 (buckwheat noodles). It means 「旁邊」, \"beside.\" Think of it as \"I've just done it, and right beside me it's already ～.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>〜が早いか</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜や否や</span> also mean \"as soon as ～, ～,\" but they describe <b>one particular occasion</b>, where two things happen back to back, with no sense of wasted effort.<br>Take the same topic, getting paid:<br><b>そばから</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>給料が入るそばから使ってしまう。</span> (My pay gets spent the moment it comes in, every month.)<br><b>や否や</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>給料が入るや否や、新しいスマホを買った。</span> (The moment my pay came in that one time, I bought a new phone.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: use そばから for \"it happens every time, and what I did is wasted.\" Use が早いか or や否や for \"on that one occasion, the next thing followed right after the first.\"</div>","n1-21":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜んがため（に）」means <b>\"in order to ～, at all costs\"</b>. It means \"for the purpose of\" just like 「ために」, but the tone is much stronger, with a drive of \"I have to achieve this.\" It's very old-fashioned. You almost never hear it in everyday conversation, and you're more likely to see it in novels, speeches, and opinion pieces.</div><div class='gd-h'>What is this 「ん」?</div><div class='gd-sec'>The 「ん」 here is <b>not a negative</b>. It's a classical-Japanese form meaning \"want to ～\" or \"intend to ～,\" close to modern 「〜う／〜よう」. 「が」 works like 「の」. So <span class='gd-jp'>勝たんがため</span> is roughly \"the purpose of wanting to win,\" in other words \"in order to win.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in its ない form, <b>drop ない</b>, then add 「んがため（に）」.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勝つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勝たない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勝たんがため</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>守る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>守らんがため</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>売る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>売らんがため</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言わんがため</span></span> (verbs ending in う change to わ, same as in the ない form)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>生きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>生きない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>生きんがため</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>逃れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>逃れんがため</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>する</span> becomes <b>せ</b>, not し.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>せんがため</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>優勝する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>優勝せんがため</span></span><br>「来る」 almost never appears in this pattern, so don't worry about memorizing it.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll most often see it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>んがために</span>Goes in the first half of the sentence, followed by what was done to achieve it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>勝たんがために、彼は必死に練習した。</span> (To win, he practiced desperately.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>生きんがために、どんな仕事でもした。</span> (To stay alive, I took any job.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>んがための＋noun</span>Modifies the noun that follows. <span class='gd-jp'>売らんがため</span> often has a negative nuance, meaning \"thinking only of making a sale.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>売らんがための広告には、もううんざりだ。</span> (I'm fed up with ads that only care about selling.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>んがためだ</span>Goes at the end of the sentence, explaining \"the reason for doing that was ～.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼が嘘をついたのは、自分を守らんがためだった。</span> (He lied in order to protect himself.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The form for する is <b>せんがため</b>. 「しんがため」 is wrong.<br>・「ん」 is not a negative: <span class='gd-jp'>勝たんがため</span> means \"in order to win,\" not \"in order not to win.\"<br>・The second half must be <b>something a person does deliberately</b>, usually a goal the same person wants to achieve. You can't use it for the weather or natural phenomena.<br>・The second half can't be a request or command like 「please ～」 or 「go and ～」: <span class='gd-jp'>合格せんがため、勉強しなさい</span> sounds unnatural. Say <span class='gd-jp'>合格するために、勉強しなさい</span> instead.<br>・Used for small things, it sounds exaggerated and funny. If you're going to the convenience store for bread, <span class='gd-jp'>パンを買うために</span> is plenty.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take one situation: \"To pass, I studied late every night.\"<br><b>ために</b> = the ordinary \"in order to,\" usable anywhere: <span class='gd-jp'>合格するために、毎晩遅くまで勉強した。</span><br><b>べく</b> = the written-style \"in order to.\" It attaches directly to the dictionary form, and する can become <span class='gd-jp'>すべく</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>合格すべく、毎晩遅くまで勉強した。</span><br><b>んがため</b> = even more old-fashioned, with a \"even at the risk of my life\" feel: <span class='gd-jp'>合格せんがため、毎晩遅くまで勉強した。</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Another one to watch out for is <b>んばかりに</b>. The form before it is exactly the same, but the meaning is completely different: \"looking as if ～ were about to happen.\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>泣かんばかりに頼んだ。</span> (I begged so hard I was almost in tears.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: 「ん」 followed by <b>がため</b> means \"in order to\"; followed by <b>ばかり</b> it means \"almost ～.\" To say \"in order to,\" use ために in everyday speech and べく in writing. Save んがため for when you want to convey \"at all costs.\"</div>","n1-7":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜をおいて」 means <b>\"no one but ~\" or \"who else but ~?\"</b>. The speaker runs through all the possible people or things in their head and concludes that <b>only this one is truly the right fit</b>. It's not just saying \"only.\" It also carries a very high opinion of that one choice.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + <span class='gd-jp'>をおいて</span> + <span class='gd-jp'>ほかに</span>〜<span class='gd-jp'>ない</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>いない</span><br>Use <span class='gd-jp'>いない</span> when you're talking about people, and <span class='gd-jp'>ない</span> for things, times and places.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>彼</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>彼をおいてほかにいない</span></span> (no one but him)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>今</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>今をおいてほかにない</span></span> (no other time but now)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>People</span>Most common when recommending someone, or praising them as the one and only choice.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>次のリーダーにふさわしいのは、山田さんをおいてほかにいない。</span> (For our next leader, nobody fits but Yamada.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Timing</span><span class='gd-jp'>今をおいてほかにない</span> is a set phrase meaning \"it's now or never.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>留学するなら、今をおいてほかにない。</span> (If you're going to study abroad, now is the time.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Rhetorical question</span>The ending doesn't have to be 「ない」. You can also use a \"who else?\" rhetorical question, which means the same thing: \"only him.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この役を演じられるのは、彼をおいて誰がいるだろうか。</span> (Who else but him could play this role?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people go wrong</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>You can't use it for plain facts.</b> \"There was no one in the room but him\" is just an objective situation. Say <span class='gd-jp'>部屋には彼以外誰もいなかった</span>, not をおいて.<br>・It's normally used for <b>positive evaluations</b>, like \"the best fit,\" \"the greatest,\" or \"only he can do it.\" Using it for bad things sounds odd.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>をおいて</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>において</span> differ by only one character but mean completely different things. <span class='gd-jp'>会議室において</span> means \"in the meeting room,\" marking a place or occasion.<br>・This is fairly formal, written-style language, common in articles, speeches and the news. When chatting with friends, just say <span class='gd-jp'>彼しかいないよ</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>何をおいても</span> is a different idiom meaning \"above all else, whatever happens\": <span class='gd-jp'>何をおいても駆けつける</span> (I'll rush over no matter what). Don't mix it up with the \"nothing other than ~\" meaning.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar-looking patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Say you're talking about one really excellent old shop:<br><b>をおいて</b> = <b>picks out the single one</b> from a field of candidates: <span class='gd-jp'>この味を出せるのは、あの店をおいてほかにない。</span> (No place but that shop can produce this flavor.)<br><b>ならでは</b> = a characteristic that <b>only it has</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>これはあの店ならではの味だ。</span> (This is a flavor unique to that shop.)<br><b>以外</b> = simply says <b>\"other than ~\"</b>, with no evaluation: <span class='gd-jp'>あの店以外で食べたことがない。</span> (I've never eaten anywhere other than that shop.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you're saying \"in choosing who or which, this is the only answer,\" use をおいて. If you're saying \"this trait belongs exclusively to it,\" use ならでは. If you're just stating \"other than ~\" objectively, use 以外.</div>","n1-11":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ならまだしも」 means <b>\"~ would be one thing, but…\"</b>. You first take a step back and say, \"if it were only this, I could accept it,\" then point out, \"but at the level it's actually at, it's not okay.\" The weight of the sentence is in the second half: <b>you're complaining and saying you can't accept it</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一度ならまだしも、三度も遅刻するなんて。</span> (Once would be one thing, but being late three times!)</div><div class='gd-h'>Breaking it down</div><div class='gd-sec'>You can think of <span class='gd-jp'>まだしも</span> as \"<span class='gd-jp'>まだ</span> (still) + <span class='gd-jp'>しも</span> (adds emphasis),\" meaning \"still passable.\" So the sentence structure is always:<br><b>a milder, tolerable situation</b> + ならまだしも, <b>a more serious, unacceptable situation</b>.<br>The mild situation in the first half is often <em>not</em> what actually happened and is only there for contrast. With <span class='gd-jp'>冗談ならまだしも</span>, the implication is \"you're clearly not joking.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>冗談</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>冗談ならまだしも</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: attach directly, without だ. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>下手</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>下手ならまだしも</span></span><br><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: use the plain form (the casual form, without ます or です). <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知らないならまだしも</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Dissatisfaction with something that already happened</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>知らないならまだしも、知っていて黙っていたのは許せない。</span> (Not knowing would be one thing, but I can't forgive that you knew and kept quiet.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Judging a situation that hasn't happened yet</span>The second half doesn't have to be about the past. It can also be a judgment about a hypothetical or the future.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夏はまだしも、冬はこの部屋じゃ無理だ。</span> (Summer might be fine, but there's no way I could live in this room in winter.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking someone to stop</span>The second half can also be a request or a prohibition.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一日ならまだしも、毎日来るのはやめてほしい。</span> (One day would be fine, but I really wish you'd stop coming every day.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'>After a noun you'll also often hear <span class='gd-jp'>はまだしも</span> used directly, like <span class='gd-jp'>夏はまだしも</span> above, with the same meaning.<br>Another pattern with a very close meaning is <span class='gd-jp'>ならともかく</span>, and in many cases the two are interchangeable.</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people go wrong</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>The order can't be reversed</b>: put the mild case first and the serious one second. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>三度ならまだしも、一度遅刻するなんて</span> makes no logical sense.<br>・Don't add だ after nouns and な-adjectives: it's <span class='gd-jp'>冗談ならまだしも</span>, not 冗談だならまだしも.<br>・The second half must be a negative evaluation (things like <span class='gd-jp'>ありえない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>許せない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>無理だ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>やめてほしい</span>). You can't use it to praise or talk about good things.<br>・The tone is complaining or scolding. Saying it directly to a superior or a customer can sound accusatory, so be careful about the setting.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar-looking patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Say you see a grown adult crying over something trivial:<br><b>ならまだしも</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもならまだしも、大人がこんなことで泣くなんて。</span> (A child would be one thing, but a grown adult crying over something like this!)<br><b>ではあるまいし</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもではあるまいし、こんなことで泣かないで。</span> (You're not a child, so don't cry over something like this.)<br>The point of <b>ならまだしも</b> is the <b>gap</b> between \"the first part I could accept\" and \"this level is not okay,\" and it's often used to complain or say you can't accept something. The point of <b>ではあるまいし</b> is \"it's not like you're ~,\" which <b>denies the premise</b> before telling the other person what they should do or giving a judgment.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you want to say \"A would be fine, but B is too much,\" use ならまだしも. If you want to say \"it's not like you're A, so you should…,\" use ではあるまいし.</div>","n1-24":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とあって」is a favorite in news and reports. It means <b>\"because it's such a special occasion, naturally…\"</b>. Think of English news lines like \"With the holiday weekend just starting, the airport was packed with travelers.\" That \"with…\" feeling is what it captures. First you name a special day or occasion, then you describe the scene or reaction that followed.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Special days and occasions</span>Long weekends, big sales, fireworks festivals: things that don't happen every day, so there are more people and more excitement than usual.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>連休初日とあって、空港は大混雑だった。</span>(It was the first day of the long weekend, so the airport was incredibly crowded.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年に一度のセールとあって、店の前には朝から長い列ができていた。</span>(It was the once-a-year sale, so a long line had formed in front of the store since early morning.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>People's reactions</span>Because the situation is rare, the people involved are nervous, excited, or extra serious.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>初めての海外旅行とあって、娘はずいぶん張り切っている。</span>(It's her first trip abroad, so my daughter is really fired up.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人気歌手が来るとあって、会場には若い人が大勢集まった。</span>(A popular singer was coming, so tons of young people gathered at the venue.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly, usually without 「だ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>連休</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>連休とあって</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>花火大会</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>花火大会とあって</span></span><br><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: use the plain form (no ます or です; past tense is fine too).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来るとあって</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>優勝した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>優勝したとあって</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安いとあって</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The second half can only describe <b>an actual result or situation you can observe</b>. You can't put your own intentions, invitations, or requests there.<br>×<span class='gd-jp'>連休とあって、旅行に行こう。</span><br>○<span class='gd-jp'>連休だから、旅行に行こう。</span> (It's a long weekend, so let's go on a trip.)<br>・The first half needs to be something <b>special or rare</b>. An everyday reason sounds odd. For something like \"I was hungry, so I bought bread,\" just use 「から」or「ので」.<br>・Nouns usually don't take だ: say <span class='gd-jp'>連休とあって</span>, not 連休だとあって.<br>・This is a written, news-style tone. When chatting with friends, 「〜から」or「〜ので」sounds more natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here's the same \"company president\" situation with three patterns:<br><b>とあって</b> = because it's ~, <b>this is the result we see</b> (something that has already happened)<br><span class='gd-jp'>社長が来るとあって、社員はみんな緊張している。</span> (The president is coming, so all the employees are nervous.)<br><b>とあれば</b> = <b>if it's ~</b>, then (I'm willing to)… (hypothetical, conditional)<br><span class='gd-jp'>社長の頼みとあれば、断るわけにはいかない。</span> (If it's a request from the president, I can't very well refuse.)<br><b>にあって</b> = <b>being in the position or situation of ~</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>社長という立場にあって、そんな発言は許されない。</span> (In the position of president, a remark like that is not acceptable.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: \"Because it's ~, (everyone) naturally…\" is とあって; \"if it's ~\" is とあれば; \"being in the midst of ~\" is にあって.</div>","n1-25":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ばこそ」means <b>\"precisely because ~, …\"</b>. It's close to「からこそ」but more formal, the kind of phrasing you'd see in essays or speeches. It's often used when something that looks bad actually has a good reason behind it, like \"I scold you precisely because I love you.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Stating the real reason</span>Others may misunderstand your behavior, and you want to stress \"I'm doing this because ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あなたを思えばこそ、厳しく言うのです。</span>(It's precisely because I care about you that I speak harshly.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>信頼していればこそ、この仕事を任せたのです。</span>(It's precisely because I trust you that I entrusted this job to you.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Only possible with this condition</span>Emphasizes that a certain good condition is the key.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>若ければこそ、失敗を恐れずに挑戦できる。</span>(Only because you're young can you take on challenges without fearing failure.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>健康であればこそ、好きなことに挑戦できる。</span>(Only because you're healthy can you take on what you love.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First put the preceding word into its 「〜ば」form, then add <b>こそ</b>.<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the え-row sound of the same row, then add ば.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>思う</span> → う becomes え → <span class='gd-jp'>思えばこそ</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop る and add れば.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>信じる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>信じればこそ</span></span><br><b>Group 3 (する・来る)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>すればこそ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ればこそ</span> (くればこそ)</span><br>For 「〜ている」, change いる to いれば: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>案じている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>案じていればこそ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: change い to ければ. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>若い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>若ければこそ</span></span><br><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: add であれば. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>親</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>親であればこそ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>健康</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>健康であればこそ</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親であればこそ、子どもの幸せを願うのだ。</span>(It's precisely because we're parents that we wish for our children's happiness.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The 「ば」here is <b>not \"if\"</b>; it means \"because.\" <span class='gd-jp'>思えばこそ</span> doesn't mean \"if you think of (them)\" but \"precisely because I think of (you).\" This is a holdover from classical Japanese, which is why it looks identical to the conditional form.<br>・Nouns and な-adjectives can't attach directly: say <span class='gd-jp'>健康であればこそ</span>, not 「健康ばこそ」.<br>・The tone is very formal and rarely used in conversation. With friends, just use <span class='gd-jp'>からこそ</span>; ばこそ would sound like you're reading a script.<br>・The reason in the first half is usually positive and sincere (love, trust, health, effort). It's not really used for bad luck or negative causes.<br>・The second half often ends with <span class='gd-jp'>のだ／のです</span>, giving a \"this is actually the reason\" feel. Watch for that in the examples.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>からこそ</b> = precisely because ~, …; works in both speech and writing: <span class='gd-jp'>好きだからこそ、厳しく言うんだよ。</span> (It's precisely because I like you that I'm being tough.)<br><b>ばこそ</b> = same meaning, but more formal, mostly in essays and speeches: <span class='gd-jp'>愛すればこそ、厳しく言うのである。</span> (It is precisely because I love them that I speak sternly.)<br><b>あっての</b> = \"~ exists only thanks to…\"; attaches to a noun and stresses \"without it, it doesn't hold up\": <span class='gd-jp'>健康あっての人生だ。</span> (Life exists only with health.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use からこそ in everyday speech; use ばこそ when writing or giving a speech and you want a solemn tone; use あっての when you mean \"without it, there's nothing.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Among other N1 ways to express reasons, take a look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜ゆえ（に）</span> (because of ~; even more formal, written style) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜あっての</span>.</div>","n1-12":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AこそあれB（ない）」means <b>“there is A, but there's no B”</b>, or more plainly, <b>“only A, no B.”</b> Think of “I feel only gratitude toward him, no resentment.” It's stiff written language. In everyday conversation, just say 「<span class='gd-jp'>Aはあっても、Bはない</span>」.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Only A, no B</span>Put a pair of <b>opposite or contrasting</b> things on either side, such as gratitude and resentment, or criticism and praise. First you admit that A really exists, then the point is to stress that B is “simply not there.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼には感謝こそあれ、恨みは少しもない。</span>(I feel only gratitude toward him, not the slightest resentment.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の行動には批判こそあれ、賞賛の声はほとんど聞かれない。</span>(His actions draw only criticism; you hardly hear any words of praise.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Set phrases</span><span class='gd-jp'>程度の差こそあれ</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>多少の違いこそあれ</span>＝“there may be a difference in degree, but…” Here the second half is <b>not necessarily negative</b>. It's often used to say “everyone is actually the same.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>程度の差こそあれ、誰にでも悩みはある。</span>(The degree may differ, but everyone has worries.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + こそあれ directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>感謝</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>感謝こそあれ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>批判</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>批判こそあれ</span></span><br>The second half usually ends with 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜はない</span>」, 「<span class='gd-jp'>ほとんど〜ない</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ことはない</span>」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What comes before it must be a <b>noun</b>. You can't attach a dictionary-form verb directly; 「批判するこそあれ」 is wrong. To use a verb, switch to「こそすれ」 below, and attach it to the <b>ます-stem (the ます form with ます removed)</b>.<br>・It does <b>not</b> mean “precisely because there is ~.” That's a common misunderstanding from older textbooks; “precisely because” is a different grammar point.<br>・The two sides must be a contrasting pair within the same topic. If they're unrelated, for example “the festival has a simple feel, but shows no signs of decline,” a Japanese person will find it odd.<br>・The tone is very formal and it mostly shows up in essays, commentary and speeches. Say it to a friend and you'll sound like you're reading an official notice.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>こそすれ</b> also means “only A, no B,” but what comes before it is an <b>action</b>.<br>Formation: <b>verb ます-stem (drop ます) + こそすれ</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>上がります</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>上がりこそすれ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>褒められます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>褒められこそすれ</span></span><br>For する verbs you can use either 「noun + こそすれ」 or 「し + こそすれ」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>感謝します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>感謝こそすれ</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>感謝しこそすれ</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>物価は上がりこそすれ、下がることはないだろう。</span>(Prices will only go up, never down, I think.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>褒められこそすれ、叱られる理由はない。</span>(He should be praised; there's no reason to scold him.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Compare them in the same situation:<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼女には感謝こそあれ、恨みはない。</span>(What I <b>have</b> in my heart is gratitude: this talks about “existence.”)<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼女には感謝こそすれ、恨むことはない。</span>(What I <b>do</b> is thank her: this talks about an “action.”)<br>The two sentences mean almost the same thing in English.</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, “precisely because ~” is <span class='gd-jp'>〜ばこそ</span>, and “only by ~ can you ~” is <span class='gd-jp'>〜てこそ</span>, as in <span class='gd-jp'>努力してこそ成功できる</span> (only through effort can you succeed). Don't mix them up with こそあれ just because they all contain こそ.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if a <b>noun</b> comes before it (感謝, 批判, 差), use こそあれ. If a <b>verb</b> comes before it, use こそすれ, and first turn the verb into the ます form and drop ます, as in 「上がり（こそすれ）」「褒められ（こそすれ）」「感謝し（こそすれ）」. Never write 「褒められるこそすれ」.</div>","n1-13":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜まで（のこと）だ」is like English <b>“worst case, I'll just ~”</b> and <b>“I only ~, that's all.”</b> <span class='gd-jp'>まで</span> originally means “up to,” and here you can think of it as <b>“things only go as far as this point, no big deal.”</b> The meaning changes depending on whether a dictionary form or a た form comes before it, so let's take them separately.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Dictionary form: worst case, I'll just ~</span>When a plan doesn't work out, you've already thought of a fallback, and your attitude is “fine, I'll just do this.” It's very often preceded by 「〜なら」「〜ば」「〜ても」, first stating “if things go badly.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>だめなら、また一からやり直すまでだ。</span>(If it doesn't work, I'll just start over from scratch.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰も手伝ってくれないなら、一人でやるまでだ。</span>(If nobody will help, I'll just do it by myself.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車が動かなければ、歩いて帰るまでのことだ。</span>(If the trains aren't running, I'll just walk home.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>た form: I only ~, that's all</span>You use it to explain something you did, meaning “I had no other intention” or “it's nothing special.” It's very common when someone thanks you, and it sounds modest.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>当然のことをしたまでです。</span>(I only did what anyone should do.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>念のため、確認したまでです。</span>(I just checked to be on the safe side, that's all.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>思ったことを言ったまでだ。</span>(I merely said what I was thinking.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb dictionary form + までだ／までのことだ: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>やり直す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>やり直すまでだ</span></span><br>Verb た form + までだ／までのことだ: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>したまでです</span></span><br>With or without 「<span class='gd-jp'>のこと</span>」 the meaning is the same, but adding it gives more of a “that's all there is to it” feel. To be polite, just replace <span class='gd-jp'>だ</span> with <span class='gd-jp'>です</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The <span class='gd-jp'>まで</span> here is not “until.” <span class='gd-jp'>帰るまで</span> on its own means “until I go home,” so check whether it's closed off with <span class='gd-jp'>だ</span>.<br>・Don't mix up the dictionary form and the た form: <span class='gd-jp'>やり直すまでだ</span> means “I'll just redo it” (not done yet), while <span class='gd-jp'>やり直したまでだ</span> means “I only redid it” (already done).<br>・The dictionary-form usage carries a “do or die” tone and normally expresses <b>your own</b> resolve; you don't use it to tell someone else what to do.<br>・What comes before it is usually a backup plan that wasn't your first choice but is fine anyway, not something happy and good.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜までだ</b> = I'll just do it if I have to: <span class='gd-jp'>聞かれたら、説明するまでだ。</span>(If I'm asked, I'll just explain.)<br><b>〜までもない</b> = there's simply no need to do it: <span class='gd-jp'>簡単だから、説明するまでもない。</span>(It's simple, so there's no need to explain.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜までだ</b> = there's still a way out, optimistic: <span class='gd-jp'>失敗したら、またやるまでだ。</span>(If I fail, I'll just try again.)<br><b>〜ばそれまでだ</b> = once that happens, it's all over, pessimistic: <span class='gd-jp'>一度失敗すれば、それまでだ。</span>(Fail once and it's over.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: “verb + までだ” means there's still a path ahead; 「それまでだ」means the path is cut off; 「までもない」means there's no need to take that path at all.</div>","n1-26":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜手前」 is like English <b>\"I said I'd do it, so I can't back out now…\"</b> or <b>\"With everyone watching, I can't lose face…\"</b>. Because of something you already said or did, or because people are watching, you have to protect <b>your own face and standing</b>. So you end up doing something, or not being able to do something.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>You already said it</span>A verb comes before it, usually something you already said or did. Since you said it, you can't now make yourself look bad by going back on it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>引き受けた手前、途中でやめるわけにはいかない。</span>(Since I took the job on, I can't just quit halfway.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「絶対合格する」と言った手前、遊んでいられない。</span>(After boasting \"I'll definitely pass,\" I can't be out having fun.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>People are watching</span>A person comes before it, meaning \"in front of ～\" or \"considering how ～ will see me.\" What you're protecting is <b>your own</b> image in those people's eyes.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>みんなの手前、そんなことは言えない。</span>(In front of everyone, I can't say something like that.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもの手前、夫婦げんかはしないようにしている。</span>(For the kids' sake, we try not to argue in front of them.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Your own position</span>Use 「〜である手前」 to talk about your own role. It means \"as a ～, I can't possibly…\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>教師である手前、生徒の前でたばこは吸えない。</span>(As a teacher, I can't smoke in front of my students.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs: attach directly to the plain form (the form without ます). The た-form is the most common; the dictionary form and the ている-form also work.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>約束した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>約束した手前</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言っている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言っている手前</span></span><br>Nouns: add 「の」 first.<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>部下</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>部下の手前</span></span><br>The second half usually expresses \"have to\" or \"can't,\" for example <span class='gd-jp'>〜わけにはいかない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜しかない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜られない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜ないといけない</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It's about the face and standing of <b>yourself (or the subject of the sentence)</b>, not about protecting the other person's face. <span class='gd-jp'>部長は部下の手前、ミスを認めにくかった。</span> means the department head couldn't bring himself to admit a mistake in front of his subordinates. What he's protecting is his own image, not his subordinates' pride.<br>・The second half almost always has a forced feeling, like \"have no choice but to ～\" or \"can't ～.\" It doesn't go with happy or exciting things. Something like 「約束した手前、楽しみだ」 sounds odd.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>手前</span> is read <b>てまえ</b>. Originally it means \"this side, just before something,\" as in <span class='gd-jp'>駅の手前で降りる</span> (get off just before the station). When you see 「noun + の手前」, check the context: if a person comes before it and what follows is something you can't do or have to do, it's this grammar point, not a location.<br>・It's fairly formal and bookish. In everyday conversation you're more likely to hear something like 「言っちゃったし…」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>〜からには</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜以上</span> also translate as \"now that ～,\" but the focus is different:<br><b>からには／以上</b> = now that ～ has happened, you should and must see it through. The focus is on <b>logic and resolve</b>.<br><b>手前</b> = now that ～ has happened, not doing this <b>would be embarrassing</b>. The focus is on <b>how others see me</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>約束したからには、必ず行く。</span>(Since I promised, I'll definitely go: resolve.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>約束した手前、行かないわけにはいかない。</span>(I promised them, so I can't very well not go: saving face.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>After からには and 以上 you can put something you want to do, but not after 手前:<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本に来たからには、富士山に登りたい。</span>(Now that I'm in Japan, I want to climb Mt. Fuji.)<br>Swapping in 手前 here doesn't work, because climbing Mt. Fuji has nothing to do with saving face.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can add \"or else it'd be embarrassing / I couldn't justify it\" after the clause, use 手前. If it's just \"now that ～, I should ～,\" use からには or 以上.</div>","n1-32":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ところを」 is most commonly like English <b>\"Thank you for taking the time out of your busy schedule\" or \"Sorry to disturb you while you're resting.\"</b> You first name the state the other person is in (busy, resting, tired), then follow with <b>thanks or an apology</b>. It's a very formal, polite set phrase. It can also mean <b>\"you would normally ～, but…\"</b></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Politeness: disturbing someone</span>Put the other person's situation before it, then \"thank you,\" \"sorry,\" or what they did for you. It shows up constantly in business calls, speeches, and emails.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お忙しいところをありがとうございます。</span>(Thank you, even though you're so busy.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お休みのところ、申し訳ありません。</span>(I'm sorry to disturb you while you're off.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お疲れのところ、すみません。少しだけいいですか。</span>(Sorry to bother you when you're tired. Do you have a moment?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Should have been ～, but</span>The first part says what would normally happen, and the second says that something else actually happened, usually something in your favor.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本来ならこちらから伺うべきところを、わざわざお越しいただきました。</span>(We should have been the ones to visit you, but you kindly made the trip yourself.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>普通なら罰金のところを、今回は注意だけで済んだ。</span>(Normally it would have been a fine, but this time I got off with just a warning.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休んでいる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休んでいるところを</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>伺うべき</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>伺うべきところを</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly; in polite speech お is often added in front.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忙しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お忙しいところを</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add な first.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大変</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大変なところを</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add の first.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>お休み</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お休みのところを</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ご多忙</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ご多忙のところを</span></span><br>In speech 「を」 is often dropped, so <span class='gd-jp'>お忙しいところ</span> is perfectly fine.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't add の after an い-adjective: it's <span class='gd-jp'>お忙しいところ</span>, not お忙しいのところ. Only nouns take の.<br>・This is very formal. Saying 「お忙しいところを」 to a friend sounds stiff and distant. Between friends, <span class='gd-jp'>忙しいのにごめんね</span> is enough.<br>・In the polite usage, what comes before it is the <b>other person's</b> state, not your own. If you're busy yourself but still helping, you wouldn't say 「私の忙しいところを」.<br>・What follows usually <b>affects the other person</b>: disturbing them, troubling them, or something they did for you. You can't follow it with something unrelated to them.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ところを＋見る／見つかる</b> = <b>the scene in which someone got caught</b>. This is the N3 ところ plus the object marker を, not a polite phrase: <span class='gd-jp'>タバコを吸っているところを先生に見られた。</span> (I got seen by my teacher while I was smoking.)<br><b>ところを (should have been ～, but)</b> = it would normally go one way, but it actually had a good result: <span class='gd-jp'>普通なら怒られるところを、許してもらった。</span> (I would normally have been scolded, but I was forgiven.)<br><b>ものを</b> = \"if only you had ～,\" with a tone of complaint or regret, often at the end of a sentence: <span class='gd-jp'>一言言ってくれればよかったものを。</span> (If you'd just said a word, it would have been fine.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what follows is 「見られた、見つかった」 → a caught-in-the-act scene. If it's thanks, an apology, or a benefit → the polite use or \"should have been ～, but.\" If the whole sentence is a complaint like \"I wish you had ～\" → ものを.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>A very similar pattern, also common in polite speech, uses 「中」: <span class='gd-jp'>お足元の悪い中、お越しいただきありがとうございます。</span> (Thank you all for coming despite the bad weather.)</div>","n1-42":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「Aと言わずBと言わず」means <b>\"whether it's A or B, all of it is ～\"</b>. You name two representative parts so the listener feels that <b>the whole thing is like this</b>. It's like saying in English \"mud on his hands, mud on his feet\" when you really mean he's filthy from head to toe.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>手と言わず足と言わず、泥だらけになった。</span>(Hands and feet alike, everything was covered in mud.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + と言わず + noun + と言わず. <b>Each of the two nouns takes its own 「と言わず」</b>.<br>「言わず」is a more literary form of「言わない」. Literally it means \"not saying A, not saying B\", i.e. \"making no distinction between A and B\". It's also often written in hiragana: <span class='gd-jp'>といわず</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Body</span>From head to toe, the whole body.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>顔と言わず腕と言わず、蚊に刺されてしまった。</span>(Face, arms, everywhere, I got bitten by mosquitoes.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Place</span>The whole space, the whole area.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>壁と言わず床と言わず、部屋中に落書きがしてあった。</span>(Walls and floor alike, the whole room was covered in graffiti.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Time</span>No matter the time of day, it's constant.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昼と言わず夜と言わず、工事の音が響いている。</span>(Day and night, the sound of construction never stops.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>平日と言わず週末と言わず、この店はいつも行列ができている。</span>(Weekdays or weekends, this shop always has a line.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>You'll notice the second half often contains \"all of it\" words like <span class='gd-jp'>部屋中</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>町中</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>いつも</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>だらけ</span>. They echo the \"A and B alike\" in the first half.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You need 「と言わず」 twice. <span class='gd-jp'>手と足と言わず</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>手と言わず足と言わず</span>.<br>・A and B should be <b>the same kind of thing</b>, and parts of one whole (hands and feet = the whole body, day and night = all day). Pairing two unrelated things sounds odd.<br>・It <b>describes what you actually see</b>, and usually something surprising or not so good (covered in mud, wounds everywhere, nonstop noise).<br>・It's written, fairly stiff language, more common in novels and news reports. In everyday conversation Japanese people usually say <span class='gd-jp'>手も足も</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>どこもかしこも</span>.<br>・Don't confuse it with a standalone 「と言わず」: <span class='gd-jp'>明日と言わず、今すぐ行こう。</span> (Not tomorrow, let's go right now.) That means \"no need to wait for A\", which is a different pattern.<br>・In <span class='gd-jp'>何も言わずに帰った</span> (left without saying anything), 「言わず」 really means \"not speaking\". It has nothing to do with this pattern.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both mean \"whether A or B\", but <span class='gd-jp'>Aであれ Bであれ</span> feels different:<br><b>と言わず</b> = <b>describes the actual situation in front of you</b>, it's like this everywhere:<br><span class='gd-jp'>駅前と言わず住宅街と言わず、どこも観光客であふれている。</span> (Whether in front of the station or in residential areas, tourists are everywhere.)<br><b>であれ</b> = <b>the same rule or judgment applies no matter which one</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>駅前であれ住宅街であれ、ごみを捨ててはいけない。</span> (Whether in front of the station or in a residential area, you must not litter.)<br>The second sentence states a rule, so switching to と言わず would sound unnatural.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you're describing \"everywhere you look, it's like this\", use と言わず. If you're stating a rule or judgment that holds \"whichever one it is\", use であれ.</div>","n1-44":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「名詞＋ったら」is like the English <b>\"Honestly, my brother…!\" or \"Oh, that mother of mine…\"</b>. You first single out someone close to you, then talk about something they did that's both annoying and funny. The point is <b>exasperation and complaint, but with affection</b>. You're not really scolding.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Just add 「ったら」 right after the noun. No conjugation needed.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>弟</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>弟ったら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>お母さん</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お母さんったら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>あなた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>あなたったら</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Complaining to someone else</span>When chatting with a friend about family, coworkers or pets, it introduces the topic of \"there they go again\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの猫ったら、また私の椅子で寝てる。</span>(Honestly, my cat is sleeping on my chair again.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父ったら、また眼鏡をなくしたの。</span>(Honestly, my dad lost his glasses again.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Scolding someone to their face</span>You address the person directly to mean \"Oh, you!\" It's often paired with「もう」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう、お母さんったら！その話はしないでって言ったでしょ。</span>(Oh, Mom! I told you not to talk about that.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんったら、冗談ばっかり。</span>(Honestly, Tanaka, you're always joking around.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The rest of the sentence often trails off, as in <span class='gd-jp'>弟ったら、また忘れ物して…。</span> (My brother, honestly, he forgot something again…). The unspoken part is that little sigh.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It is <b>not \"if\"</b>. The たら in <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったら</span> means \"if ～\", while noun + ったら means \"speaking of ～, honestly…\". They're completely different.<br>・The tone is casual and familiar, used for family, friends and pets. It's not suitable to say to a boss or customer's face, or in formal settings.<br>・What follows is usually something that <b>leaves you speechless, exasperated or a little embarrassed</b>. To simply praise or thank someone, a plain「は」is enough. Even with something good, the feeling is a shy or exasperated \"oh, they're too polite, honestly\".<br>・The small っ before たら takes a one-beat pause: say「ったら」, not「たら」.<br>・You'll also hear <span class='gd-jp'>お母さんってば</span>, which has a very similar tone to ったら and is also colloquial.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ったら</b> = exasperation mixed with affection, and you can also use it to address the person directly:<br><span class='gd-jp'>うちの子ったら、またゲームばっかり。</span> (Honestly, my kid is playing video games again.)<br><b>ときたら</b> = a more serious telling-off with a stronger negative feeling. It's mostly said to someone else, not used to address the person to their face:<br><span class='gd-jp'>うちの子ときたら、毎日ゲームばっかりで全然勉強しない。</span> (As for my kid, he does nothing but play video games every day and never studies.)<br><b>ったらない</b> = usually follows an adjective and means \"extremely ～\". It has nothing to do with complaining about a person:<br><span class='gd-jp'>あの時は恥ずかしいったらなかった。</span> (I was so embarrassed back then, you have no idea.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you say \"honestly!\" with a smile, it's ったら. If you say it with a frown while listing grievances, it's ときたら. If ったら is followed by「ない」and comes after an adjective, it means \"extremely ～\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, study <span class='gd-jp'>～ときたら</span> to tell apart the two tones: affectionate complaint and serious scolding.</div>","n1-35":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜の至り」 means <b>“extremely ~” or “~ beyond words”</b>, as in “I’m deeply honored” or “I’m utterly grateful.” <span class='gd-jp'>光栄の至りです</span> means “I am honored to the very highest degree.” It’s very formal. You’ll hear it in ceremony speeches, award acceptance remarks, and business letters, but you wouldn’t say it when chatting with friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>What is 「至り」?</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>至り</span> is a noun made from the verb <span class='gd-jp'>至る</span> (to reach), and it means “reaching the very peak.” So <span class='gd-jp'>光栄の至り</span> = honor at its peak = honored beyond measure.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + の至り, and the sentence usually ends with です or でございます.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>光栄</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>光栄の至りです</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>感激</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>感激の至りでございます</span></span><br>Note that the nouns that go in front are <b>almost always the same few</b>. You can’t plug in just any noun.</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you’ll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Gratitude and honor</span>The most common are <span class='gd-jp'>光栄の至り</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>感激の至り</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このような賞をいただき、光栄の至りです。</span>(To receive an award like this is an honor beyond words.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皆様から温かいお言葉をいただき、感激の至りです。</span>(I’m deeply moved to receive such warm words from all of you.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Apology and embarrassment</span>Common ones are <span class='gd-jp'>恐縮の至り</span> (I feel terribly sorry / deeply obliged) and <span class='gd-jp'>赤面の至り</span> (so embarrassed my face turns red).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お忙しいところお越しいただき、恐縮の至りでございます。</span>(I’m deeply grateful, and sorry, that you took the trouble to come when you’re so busy.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こんな初歩的なミスをしてしまい、赤面の至りです。</span>(Making such a basic mistake is utterly embarrassing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>The odd one out</span><span class='gd-jp'>若気の至り</span> works a little differently. It refers to “mistakes made out of youthful naivety.” Here 至り is closer to “the result of.” People often use it to look back on foolish things they did in the past, with a touch of self-mockery, so it isn’t necessarily formal.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>学生時代は若気の至りで、ずいぶん無茶をした。</span>(In my student days I did a lot of reckless things out of youthful folly.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where learners slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What goes in front is <b>noun + の</b>. You can’t attach it directly to an adjective. <span class='gd-jp'>嬉しいの至り</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ありがたいの至り</span> are both wrong. To express happiness or thanks, use the fixed pairings <span class='gd-jp'>光栄</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>感激</span>.<br>・Don’t swap in nouns on your own. Memorizing 光栄・感激・恐縮・赤面・若気 is the safest approach.<br>・This phrase is mainly about <b>your own</b> feelings, and it carries a humble tone. Using it for someone else’s feelings, such as saying “the department head is extremely honored,” sounds very odd.<br>・The tone is extremely formal. If you say <span class='gd-jp'>ケーキありがとう、感激の至り！</span> to a friend, it sounds like you’re joking and putting on airs.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>若気</span> is read <span class='gd-jp'>わかげ</span>, not わかき.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>の至り</b> = your own gratitude or embarrassment reaching its peak, in a humble tone: <span class='gd-jp'>お招きいただき、光栄の至りです。</span> (Being invited is an honor beyond words.)<br><b>極まりない</b> = something or someone’s attitude is “extremely ~”, mostly used to criticize or complain: <span class='gd-jp'>連絡もなく欠席するなんて、失礼極まりない。</span> (Being absent without even a word is extremely rude.)<br><b>に至る</b> = a verb meaning that things “develop to a certain result or point”: <span class='gd-jp'>何度も話し合った結果、ようやく合意に至った。</span> (After talking it over many times, we finally reached an agreement.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: use の至り for your own “extremely ~” feelings, 極まりない to scold or complain that something is “extremely ~”, and に至る for things that “end up reaching ~”.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>〜に堪えない</span> is another way to express strong feelings on formal occasions: <span class='gd-jp'>皆様のご支援には感謝に堪えません。</span> (I can’t thank you enough for everyone’s support.)</div>","n1-41":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜といったらない」 means <b>“incredibly ~,” “so ~ you can’t describe it,” “~ beyond words”</b>. Literally it’s “if you talk about how ~ it is, there are simply no words to describe it.” You use it to emphasize that a feeling is intense to the extreme. It works for both good and bad things: incredibly happy, freezing cold, mortifyingly embarrassed, and so on.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Noun</b> + といったらない (most often an adjective turned into a 「～さ」 noun)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → change い to さ → <span class='gd-jp'>寒さといったらない</span></span><br><b>い-adjective</b>: attach directly<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>うれしい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>うれしいといったらない</span></span><br><b>な-adjective</b>: drop な and attach directly<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>退屈</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>退屈といったらない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>When talking about something in the <b>past</b>, change it to 「といったら<b>なかった</b>」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>初めて富士山を見た時の感動といったらなかった。</span>(The emotion I felt when I first saw Mt. Fuji was beyond words.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日同じ作業ばかりで、退屈といったらない。</span>(It’s the same task every day, so boring I can’t stand it.)</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you’ll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ったらない</span>A shortened, spoken version of 「といったらない」. You hear it a lot when chatting with friends or venting, and it’s mostly attached directly to an い-adjective.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>隣の工事、うるさいったらないよ。</span>(The construction next door is unbelievably loud.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ったらありゃしない</span>An even more colloquial and emotional version, mostly used for complaints, embarrassment, and anger.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>みんなの前で転ぶなんて、恥ずかしいったらありゃしない。</span>(Falling in front of everyone was so embarrassing I wanted to die.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where learners slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The 「ない」 here is <b>not a negation</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>うれしいといったらない</span> means “I’m overjoyed,” not “I’m not happy.”<br>・Don’t add な to な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>退屈といったらない</span>, not 退屈なといったらない.<br>・If it’s already over, use 「といったらなかった」. Otherwise it sounds like it’s still happening.<br>・What goes in front is usually an adjective or a noun, especially a 「～さ」 noun. Verbs are rarely attached directly.<br>・「ったらない」 and 「ったらありゃしない」 are very colloquial. Don’t use them in formal situations or with your boss.<br>・Don’t confuse it with 「といったら (speaking of ~)」: <span class='gd-jp'>日本といったら、やっぱり寿司だ。</span> (Speaking of Japan, it’s sushi, of course.) There’s no ない afterward, and the meaning is completely different.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜ったら（n1-44）</b>: attached to a <b>person</b>, it means “honestly, that ~,” with a tone of exasperation or complaint, followed by a full sentence.<br><span class='gd-jp'>うちの母ったら、また夜中に電話してきた。</span> (Honestly, my mom called in the middle of the night again.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>うちの母、心配性ったらない。</span> (My mom is unbelievably anxious.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if 「ったら」 is followed right away by 「ない」 and what comes before is an adjective or a 「～さ」 noun, it’s about degree. If what comes before is a person and a full sentence follows, you’re complaining about that person.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜極まりない（n1-34）</b>: also means “extremely ~,” but it’s stiffer and more written, and mostly used for negative judgments, as in news reports or complaint letters. 「といったらない」 is more about your own firsthand feelings and works for both positive and negative things.<br><span class='gd-jp'>あの店員の態度は失礼極まりない。</span> (That clerk’s attitude is extremely rude: like a formal complaint.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>あの店員の態度の悪さといったらなかった。</span> (That clerk’s attitude was unbelievably bad: like telling a friend about what happened.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: use 「極まりない」 for formal writing and harsh criticism, and 「といったらない」 for your own feelings with a bit of emotional exaggeration.</div>","n1-45":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ときたら」 works like an English complaint such as <b>\"Honestly, my son…\" or \"Don't even get me started on…\"</b>. You name a person or thing that drives you up the wall, then rattle off what's wrong with it. The core feeling is <b>dissatisfaction, exasperation, and \"what can you do?\"</b></div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add ときたら directly to a noun, usually a person or a thing.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>うちの息子</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>うちの息子ときたら</span></span>（talk about my son…）<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>このパソコン</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>このパソコンときたら</span></span>（this computer, honestly…）</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Complaining about people around you</span>Most often used for family, coworkers, and bosses: people close to you who give you headaches.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの息子ときたら、ゲームばかりしている。</span>（My son, honestly, does nothing but play video games all day）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの部長ときたら、会議のたびに話が長い。</span>（Our department head, honestly, talks forever at every meeting）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Complaining about things</span>It works for things that don't work well, too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このパソコンときたら、すぐ固まるんだから。</span>（This computer, honestly, freezes all the time）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Scolding yourself</span>It's perfectly natural to aim it at yourself, too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私ときたら、また傘を電車に忘れてきた。</span>（Honestly, me… I left my umbrella on the train again）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Leaving it unfinished</span>In speech, people often skip the actual complaint and stop at ときたら, letting their tone show the annoyance.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>まったく、うちの主人ときたら……</span>（Honestly, my husband…）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It's for complaining <b>to a third party</b> about someone or something. You can't use it to address the person directly. Saying 「お母さんときたら！」 to your mom's face is wrong.<br>・What follows is a description or a complaint. Don't follow it with a request or decision like \"please do ~\" or \"let's ~\". Something like <span class='gd-jp'>うちの部長ときたら、早く帰ってください</span>, with a request attached, is wrong.<br>・It's basically negative, so don't use it to praise someone. <span class='gd-jp'>うちの息子ときたら、頭がいい</span> would sound odd.<br>・It's casual and chatty, and rarely used in formal writing or reports.<br>・In speech you'll sometimes hear <span class='gd-jp'>夏ときたら、やっぱりビールでしょ。</span>（Speaking of summer, it's got to be beer, right?）This \"talking about A, you think of B\" use is a different usage, not a complaint, so don't mix them up when you hear it.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ったら</b>: It can also complain, but the tone is lighter, often with an affectionate \"what am I going to do with you?\" feel, and you <b>can say it to the person's face</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>もう、お母さんったら！</span>（Oh, Mom, honestly!）: said to your mom, which is fine<br><b>ときたら</b>: The criticism is harsher, and you can only use it when talking to others about that person.<br><span class='gd-jp'>うちの母ときたら、人の話を全然聞かない。</span>（My mom, honestly, never listens to anything anyone says）: complaining to a friend</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に至っては</b>: You first mention a group of people or a bunch of things, then pick out <b>the most extreme one</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>みんな遅刻したが、田中さんに至っては来なかった。</span>（Everyone was late, but Tanaka was even worse: he didn't show up at all）<br><b>ときたら</b> doesn't need a comparison. You just single someone out and complain about them.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to say \"honestly, you!\" to someone's face, use ったら; to complain to others about a specific person, use ときたら; to pick the worst case out of a group, use に至っては.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>It often appears together with 「<span class='gd-jp'>まったく</span>」 and 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜んだから</span>」, which make the complaint even stronger: <span class='gd-jp'>うちの夫ときたら、まったく何もしないんだから。</span>（My husband, honestly, does absolutely nothing）</div>","n1-46":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にあって」 is the formal, written-style <b>\"in the midst of ~\" or \"as ~\"</b>. You put a special era, situation, or status in front, and then describe the state, behavior, or what should be done in that environment. It's common in news, speeches, and essays, and almost never used in everyday chat.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It attaches only to nouns: noun + にあって<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>非常時</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>非常時にあって</span></span>（in this kind of emergency）<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>現代</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>現代にあって</span></span>（in the modern age）</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Special eras and situations</span>Emergencies, recessions, the age of information overload, and the like.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この非常時にあって、リーダーは冷静であるべきだ。</span>（In this kind of emergency, a leader should stay calm）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>情報があふれる現代にあって、正しい情報を選ぶ力が必要だ。</span>（In a modern age overflowing with information, we need the ability to pick out accurate information）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Status and position</span>Often written as 「〜という立場にあって」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長という立場にあって、軽率な発言は許されない。</span>（As company president, you can't afford to say careless things）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Being in a certain place</span>Usually an unusual place such as a foreign country or a battlefield, with a literary flavor.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>異国にあって、家族からの手紙が何よりの支えだった。</span>（Living in a foreign land, letters from my family were my greatest support）</div><div class='gd-h'>Two common variations</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>にあっても</span>Adding 「も」 gives <b>\"even in ~, still…\"</b>, followed by things like \"still didn't give up\" or \"still stayed strong\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どんな苦境にあっても、彼女は笑顔を忘れなかった。</span>（No matter how difficult her situation was, she never forgot to smile）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>にあっては</span>Adding 「は」 emphasizes <b>\"in this situation, one has no choice but to…\"</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この不況下にあっては、どの会社も節約せざるを得ない。</span>（Under this recession, every company has no choice but to cut costs）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches only to nouns. If you want to use a verb, first turn it into a noun, or say 「〜という状況にあって」 instead.<br>・What comes before it should be a <b>special environment or status worth mentioning</b>. Ordinary times like 「月曜日にあって」 or 「普通の日にあって」 sound strange.<br>・It's very stiff written language. When chatting with friends, just say 「忙しい中」 or 「〜なのに」; using にあって would sound like reading a press release.<br>・Don't confuse it with the plain \"is located at\": <span class='gd-jp'>その店は駅前にあって便利だ。</span>（That shop is in front of the station, so it's convenient）Here あって just means \"being located\", and it isn't this grammar point.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にあたって</b> (just one letter different, and the one most often misread) = <b>right before or at the moment of doing something</b>, and it can attach to verbs.<br><span class='gd-jp'>社長に就任するにあたって、一言ご挨拶申し上げます。</span>（On the occasion of taking up the post of president, I'd like to say a few words）<br><span class='gd-jp'>社長という立場にあって、軽率な発言は許されない。</span>（As company president, you can't afford to say careless things）<br>The first sentence focuses on the point in time of taking office; the second is about continuously being in the president's position.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>とあって</b> = <b>because it's ~, so…</b> (giving a reason).<br><span class='gd-jp'>不況とあって、就職が厳しい。</span>（Because of the recession, finding a job is hard）<br><span class='gd-jp'>不況にあって、この会社は売り上げを伸ばしている。</span>（Even amid the recession, this company is increasing its sales）<br>とあって means \"because of this, that happens\"; にあって means \"being in this environment, how someone behaves\".</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use にあって for \"in the midst of ~ / as ~\", にあたって for \"on the occasion of doing ~\", and とあって for \"because it's ~\".</div>","n1-48":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「～ともなれば」means <b>\"once you're at the level of ~, of course…\"</b>. You put a status, age, or period that is <b>a step above the ordinary</b> in front, and the second half says \"at that level, this naturally follows.\" It's the tone of English like: \"Well, he's company president now, so of course he takes work calls on his days off.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Moving up in status or age</span>When someone reaches a certain position or age, their responsibilities and other people's expectations change with it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>プロともなれば、これくらいは当然できる。</span> (When you're a professional, of course you can do this much.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長ともなれば、休みの日も仕事の電話が来る。</span> (When you're the company president, work calls come even on your days off.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>When the time comes</span>When a special season or day arrives, the surroundings naturally change.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>12月ともなれば、街はクリスマス一色になる。</span> (Once December comes, the streets are all about Christmas.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>When it's a really big undertaking</span>After a verb, it means \"if you're really going to go that far,\" and the sense of \"if\" is stronger here.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>海外に引っ越すともなれば、準備することが山ほどある。</span> (If you're really moving overseas, there's a mountain of things to prepare.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + ともなれば: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大学生ともなれば</span></span><br>Verb dictionary form + ともなれば: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>結婚する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>結婚するともなれば</span></span><br>The second half often uses expressions like <span class='gd-jp'>当然</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>～なければならない</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>～ものだ</span>, which say \"of course\" or \"naturally should.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The first half has to give the feeling that <b>you've reached a different level</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>小学生ともなれば</span> works because, compared with kindergarten, you've moved up a step. Drop in just any ordinary word and the sentence sounds odd.<br>・The second half says \"this is what naturally happens,\" so <b>don't put your own will or a request there</b>. To say \"if I become president, I want to try harder,\" you can't say <span class='gd-jp'>社長ともなれば、もっと頑張りたい</span>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>社長になったら、もっと頑張りたい。</span><br>・The tone is fairly formal, more common in news, writing, and speeches. In everyday conversation people usually say <span class='gd-jp'>～になると</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>～となると</span>.<br>・Don't mix it up with <span class='gd-jp'>となれば</span>: without the <span class='gd-jp'>も</span>, it's just a plain \"if that's the case,\" with no emphasis on \"now that you're at this level.\" Example: <span class='gd-jp'>明日も雨となれば、試合は中止だ。</span> (If it rains tomorrow too, the game is cancelled.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ともなると</b>: almost the same meaning, and on exams you can usually swap the two.<br><span class='gd-jp'>12月ともなると、街はクリスマス一色になる。</span><br><span class='gd-jp'>12月ともなれば、街はクリスマス一色になる。</span><br>Both are correct. If you have to split hairs, <span class='gd-jp'>ともなれば</span> has the <span class='gd-jp'>ば</span>, so it carries slightly more of a \"if it really gets to that point\" feel.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>とあれば</b>: it looks similar but points the opposite way. It means \"if it's for ~ / if it's a request for ~,\" and what follows is often something <b>you're willing to do or have no choice but to do</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>社長の命令とあれば、従うしかない。</span> (If it's the president's order, we have no choice but to obey.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>社長ともなれば、責任も重くなる。</span> (When you're the president, your responsibility naturally gets heavier too.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if the first half is \"moved up a level\" and the second half says \"this naturally follows,\" use ともなれば／ともなると. If the first half is \"a special reason\" and the second half says \"then I'll do it,\" use とあれば.</div>","n1-49":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>ともなく</span> has two uses, and both are about <b>something being vague</b>.<br>One is an <b>action with no particular purpose</b>, like \"half-watching\" in English: the TV is on and your eyes are on it, but you're not really watching anything.<br>The other is <b>not being able to say the source</b>: \"from somewhere,\" \"nobody knows who started it.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Doing something absent-mindedly</span>You didn't plan to do it, but you find yourself doing it. The biggest giveaway is that <b>the same verb appears before and after</b>, or two verbs with very close meanings.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>見るともなく窓の外を見ていた。</span> (I wasn't looking at anything in particular, just gazing out the window.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>聞くともなく隣の席の話を聞いていた。</span> (I wasn't trying to listen, but the conversation at the next table reached my ears anyway.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>見るともなくテレビを眺めていたら、見覚えのある街が映った。</span> (I was half-watching TV when a familiar street appeared on the screen.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't say who or where</span>Put a question word like 「どこ、誰、いつ」 in front. It's often followed by <span class='gd-jp'>から</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>へ</span>, or <span class='gd-jp'>に</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どこからともなく、いい匂いがしてきた。</span> (A nice smell drifted in from somewhere.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰からともなく拍手が起こった。</span> (Nobody knows who started it, but applause broke out.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>男はどこへともなく歩いていった。</span> (The man walked off to who knows where.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰にともなく「ありがとう」とつぶやいた。</span> (Not addressing anyone in particular, I murmured \"thank you.\")</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: take the dictionary form, add <span class='gd-jp'>ともなく</span>, then repeat the same verb.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見るともなく見る</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞くともなく聞く</span></span><br><b>Question words</b>: take 「どこ、誰、いつ」 plus a particle, then add <span class='gd-jp'>ともなく</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>どこから</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>どこからともなく</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>誰から</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>誰からともなく</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use the dictionary form of the verb: <span class='gd-jp'>見るともなく</span>. You can't drop ます and attach it as 「見ともなく」, and you can't use the past form 「見たともなく」.<br>・Only a limited set of verbs work here, almost all things the eyes, ears, mouth, or mind do naturally: <span class='gd-jp'>見る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>聞く</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>言う</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>考える</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span>. Don't apply it to other verbs at random.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>どこからともなく</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>どことなく</span> look almost the same but mean different things. <span class='gd-jp'>どことなく</span> means \"somehow, in some way, a little ~\" and is about a feeling: <span class='gd-jp'>彼はどことなく父に似ている。</span> (I can't say where exactly, but he somehow resembles my father.) To say \"from somewhere,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>どこからともなく</span>.<br>・\"Absent-mindedly\" doesn't mean \"not doing it.\" With <span class='gd-jp'>見るともなく見る</span> you are still looking, just without any purpose.<br>・This is a written-style expression, common in novels and essays. In everyday conversation people usually say <span class='gd-jp'>なんとなく</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>どこからか</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same-level <b><span class='gd-jp'>ともなると</span></b> (same meaning as <span class='gd-jp'>ともなれば</span>) differs by only a few characters but means something completely different. It means that once you reach a certain level or position, <b>~ naturally follows</b>. What comes before it is mostly a noun, but it can also take the verb dictionary form.<br>Compare them in the same \"living alone\" setting:<br><b><span class='gd-jp'>ともなく</span></b> (absent-minded): <span class='gd-jp'>一人の部屋で、見るともなくテレビを見ていた。</span> (Alone in my room, I was half-watching TV.)<br><b><span class='gd-jp'>ともなく</span></b> (unknown source): <span class='gd-jp'>夜中、どこからともなく猫の鳴き声が聞こえてきた。</span> (In the middle of the night, a cat's meowing came from somewhere.)<br><b><span class='gd-jp'>ともなると</span></b> (noun): <span class='gd-jp'>夜中ともなると、この辺りはすっかり静かになる。</span> (Once it's the middle of the night, this area gets completely quiet.)<br><b><span class='gd-jp'>ともなると</span></b> (verb): <span class='gd-jp'>一人暮らしをするともなると、家事も全部自分でやらなければならない。</span> (Once you're actually living alone, you have to do all the housework yourself.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ともなく</span> usually takes the shape \"same verb + ともなく + same verb\" or \"どこから／誰から + ともなく,\" and means absent-mindedly, or not being able to say who or where.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ともなると</span> is followed by \"then naturally ~ / of course ~,\" meaning that once you reach that level or position, a certain situation naturally arises.<br>Don't just look at whether a noun or a verb comes before it; look at the form and meaning of the whole sentence.</div>","n1-50":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AともBともつかない」 means <b>“you can't tell whether it's A or B”</b>, as in “neither quite A nor quite B.” Think of a smile that isn't quite a smile, or a color that's not really blue and not really green. What's in front of you sits between the two, and you can't say which side it belongs to.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a <b>noun</b> or a <b>plain-form verb</b> (dictionary form, ている form, ない form, anything without ます) in A and B. Add とも after each one, then finish with つかない.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>本気</span>＋<span class='gd-jp'>冗談</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>本気とも冗談ともつかない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>笑っている</span>＋<span class='gd-jp'>泣いている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>笑っているとも泣いているともつかない</span></span><br>Here 「つかない」 means “can't be pinned down, can't settle on one,” and it's usually written in hiragana. Most often a noun follows directly, such as an expression, voice, tone, answer or color.</div><div class='gd-h'>The two patterns you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ともつかない</span>The thing itself is ambiguous. Even when you look at it or listen to it, you can't tell which it is.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本気とも冗談ともつかない言い方だった。</span>(The way he said it, you couldn't tell if he was serious or joking)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ため息ともあくびともつかない声が聞こえた。</span>(I heard a sound that could have been a sigh or a yawn)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は笑っているとも泣いているともつかない顔をしていた。</span>(She had an expression that was neither quite a smile nor quite crying)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>とも言わない</span>A and B are usually a positive/negative pair. It means the other person <b>won't commit</b>, so they stay noncommittal.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は行くとも行かないとも言わない。</span>(He doesn't say he'll go, and he doesn't say he won't)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長はいいとも悪いとも言わなかった。</span>(The manager said neither that it was good nor that it was bad)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・「つかない」 means <b>the thing itself</b> can't be told apart, so use it for things right in front of you, like expressions, voices and answers. Don't use it for a plain “I don't know the answer.” To say “I don't know whether he'll come,” say <span class='gd-jp'>彼が来るか来ないか分からない</span>.<br>・Both A and B <b>need</b> とも. Only one, or 「AかBともつかない」, is wrong.<br>・A verb connects straight to とも with no extra 「の」. Just write <span class='gd-jp'>笑っているとも</span>, not 笑っているのとも.<br>・This is a written, novel-style pattern. In everyday conversation, <span class='gd-jp'>冗談か本気か分からない</span> is enough.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>AともBとも言わない</b>: <b>neither</b> is said, so the attitude is unclear.<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼は賛成とも反対とも言わなかった。</span>(He said neither that he agreed nor that he objected)<br><b>Aと言わずBと言わず</b>: whether it's A or B, <b>all of it</b> is like that.<br><span class='gd-jp'>手と言わず足と言わず泥だらけになった。</span>(Hands, feet, everything was covered in mud)<br>Both have “A… B…” plus 「言わ」, so they look alike, but the meanings are completely different. One means “neither side is stated,” the other means “it's the same everywhere.”</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜ともなく</b> also has とも, but only <b>one slot</b>, often after a question word, and it means “from somewhere, someone, you can't say where or who.”<br><span class='gd-jp'>どこからともなく音楽が聞こえてきた。</span>(Music came drifting in from somewhere)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: two options and you can't tell which one, use ともつかない. Neither side is stated and the person won't commit, use とも言わない. Both are included and everything is the same, use と言わず. Just one question word, “from somewhere, someone,” use ともなく.</div>","n1-51":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てまで」 means <b>“go as far as ~ to do…”</b> or <b>“even ~ in order to…”</b>. To reach some goal, you do something you normally wouldn't, something that costs you a lot. When Japanese speakers say it, it often carries a tone of “is it really necessary to go that far?”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in the て form, add 「まで」, then follow with the goal you really want.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>借金する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>借金して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>借金してまで</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>無理をする</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>無理をしてまで</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>嘘をつく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>嘘をついてまで</span></span><br>The skeleton of the sentence is <b>「Aてまで、B」 = to get B, go as far as doing A</b>. A is the price you pay, and B is what you want.</div><div class='gd-h'>The patterns you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Not worth it</span>It's followed by 「〜必要はない」 or 「〜たくない」, meaning “going that far for this isn't worth it.” This is the most common use.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>借金してまで、ブランド品を買う必要はない。</span>(There's no need to go into debt to buy designer goods)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>体を壊してまで働きたくない。</span>(I don't want to work if it means ruining my health)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Disbelief, doubt</span>When you think the price someone is paying is over the top, you express it as a question.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そこまでして行きたいの？</span>(You want to go that badly, even after all that?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達を裏切ってまで、出世したかったのか。</span>(You wanted a promotion so badly that you'd betray your friends?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Just describing the effort</span>It isn't always criticism. It can simply describe how much someone gave up, sometimes with a bit of admiration.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>徹夜してまで並んで、限定スイーツを手に入れた。</span>(He stayed up all night in line and finally got the limited-edition sweets)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長は自分の給料を減らしてまで、社員を守った。</span>(The president even cut his own salary to protect his employees)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't flip the order.</b> Before てまで comes the “price,” and the “goal” comes after. <span class='gd-jp'>借金してまで買う</span> means “borrow money in order to buy.” If you wrote 「買ってまで借金する」, it would mean “buy in order to borrow money,” which makes no sense.<br>・Put something <b>unusual and costly</b> before it. If you put in something easy that you'd do anyway, the sentence loses the “go as far as” feel and sounds odd.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>そこまでして</span> is a very common set phrase in speech meaning “going that far,” and it has the same feel as てまで. Note that it's about <b>the price being too high</b>, not “given that things are already like this.”<br>・A noun must first become a verb with 「する」 or 「をする」 before it can take てまで: <span class='gd-jp'>借金してまで</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>無理をしてまで</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>家族を犠牲にしてまで</span>. You can't write 「借金まで」 directly. That would mean “even borrowing money…,” which is a different meaning.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>In all of these, 「まで」 follows a verb, and the meaning changes completely depending on the verb form:<br><b>Dictionary form + まで</b> = until ~<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼が来るまで待った。</span>(I waited until he came)<br><b>て form + まで</b> = go as far as ~ to do<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>3時間も待ってまで、彼に会いたかった。</span>(I wanted to see him so badly that I even waited three hours)<br><b>Dictionary form + までだ (at the end of a sentence)</b> = at worst, I'll just ~<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼が来ないなら、一人で行くまでだ。</span>(If he doesn't come, I'll just go by myself)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if まで follows the <b>て form</b>, it's “go as far as.” If it follows the <b>dictionary form</b> and the sentence isn't over, it's “until.” If <b>までだ</b> sits at the end of the sentence, it's “at worst I'll just / that's all.”</div>","n1-60":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「Aかたがた」 means <b>\"and also, as a way of thanking/greeting you\"</b>, the kind of phrasing you see in formal letters. You visit or write to someone and use the occasion for a second purpose too, such as thanking them, updating them, or saying hello. It's very formal and shows up when visiting seniors or clients, or in business letters.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a <b>noun</b> before it, usually a noun for an action, such as <span class='gd-jp'>お礼</span> (thanks), <span class='gd-jp'>ご挨拶</span> (greeting), <span class='gd-jp'>ご報告</span> (report), <span class='gd-jp'>お見舞い</span> (visiting someone who is ill), <span class='gd-jp'>散歩</span> (a walk).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>お礼</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お礼かたがた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ご報告</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ご報告かたがた</span></span><br>What follows is usually an action like visiting, or writing or speaking. The most common ones are <span class='gd-jp'>伺う</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>お邪魔する</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>申し上げる</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Visiting in person</span>You explain why you've come today.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先日のお礼かたがた、ご挨拶に伺いました。</span>(I've come to say hello, and also to thank you for the other day.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>近くまで来ましたので、ご挨拶かたがたお邪魔しました。</span>(I happened to be nearby, so I dropped in to say hello.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Letters</span>Very common in business and greeting letters, almost a set phrase.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>略儀ながら書中をもちまして、お礼かたがたご挨拶申し上げます。</span>(Please forgive me for doing this by letter; I write to thank you and to send my greetings.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>転勤のご報告かたがた、お手紙を差し上げます。</span>(I'm writing this letter to let you know about my transfer, too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches to a noun, not a dictionary-form verb: say <span class='gd-jp'>お礼かたがた</span>, not <span class='gd-jp'>お礼を言うかたがた</span>.<br>・English \"while I'm at it\" or \"by the way\" can sound like the second thing doesn't matter much. かたがた doesn't mean that. Both the thanks and the greeting are sincere purposes. Using <span class='gd-jp'>お礼かたがた</span> isn't rude; it sounds polished.<br>・It's very formal and often pairs with humble verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>伺う</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>申し上げる</span>. You wouldn't use it to tell a friend \"I'm popping into the convenience store.\"<br>・The 「<span class='gd-jp'>方々</span>（かたがた）」 in <span class='gd-jp'>ご来場の方々</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>関係者の方々</span> means \"everyone, all of you.\" It's only pronounced the same and has nothing to do with this grammar.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>かたがた</b> = formal; when visiting or writing, you <b>combine a second purpose</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>お礼かたがた、先生のお宅に伺いました。</span> (I visited my teacher's home, also to thank them.)<br><b>がてら</b> = everyday speech; <b>you go out to do A and do B on the way</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>散歩がてら、パンを買ってきた。</span> (I went for a walk and picked up some bread while I was at it.)<br><b>かたわら</b> = over a long period, <b>you do your main job and also another thing</b>; it has nothing to do with \"while you're at it\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>会社に勤めるかたわら、小説を書いている。</span> (He works at a company and writes novels on the side.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for a formal visit or letter that adds thanks or a greeting, use かたがた. For doing a small errand on a trip out, use がてら. For long-term double duty, use かたわら.</div>","n1-61":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AかたわらB」 means <b>\"besides doing A (your main job), you also do B\"</b>, a bit like having a side hustle. <span class='gd-jp'>かたわら</span> originally means \"beside\": the main thing sits in the middle, and another activity runs alongside it.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Doing something on the side long-term</span>You describe someone's life or work: what their main job is, and what else they keep doing over time. It often appears in profiles, news articles, and self-introductions.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会社に勤めるかたわら、週末は小説を書いている。</span>(Besides working at a company, he writes novels on weekends.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は大学で教えるかたわら、翻訳の仕事もしている。</span>(Besides teaching at a university, she also does translation work.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>仕事のかたわら、日本語の勉強を続けている。</span>(Alongside work, I keep studying Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb: dictionary form (the base form in the dictionary) + かたわら<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>働く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>働くかたわら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>店を経営する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>店を経営するかたわら</span></span><br>Noun: noun + の + かたわら<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>仕事</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>仕事のかたわら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>育児</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>育児のかたわら</span></span><br>The noun is usually a word like \"work,\" \"research,\" or \"child-rearing\" that stands for a whole ongoing activity.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It does <b>not</b> mean doing two things with your hands at the same moment. <span class='gd-jp'>音楽を聞くかたわら勉強する</span> is wrong; for that, use <span class='gd-jp'>聞きながら</span>.<br>・Both activities <b>continue over a period of time</b>, so 「〜ている」 often follows. It doesn't suit something that happens only once.<br>・Put the <b>main</b> activity first and the side activity second. Don't swap them.<br>・It doesn't attach to the た-form: <span class='gd-jp'>勤めたかたわら</span> is wrong. Always use the dictionary form.<br>・It's written-style language and rarely used in chat with friends. In speech people usually say <span class='gd-jp'>仕事しながら</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>本業のほかに</span>.<br>・When you see 「名詞のかたわらで」 as in <span class='gd-jp'>母のかたわらで</span>, it's mostly the literal \"beside ~,\" not this grammar.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ながら</b> = doing two things with your hands at the same moment:<br><span class='gd-jp'>音楽を聞きながら勉強する。</span> (I study while listening to music.)<br><b>かたわら</b> = two long-term roles or jobs:<br><span class='gd-jp'>会社で働くかたわら、夜間の大学に通っている。</span> (Besides working at a company during the day, I attend a night university.)<br><b>がてら</b> = on this one outing, you also take care of another thing; it's short-term and one-off:<br><span class='gd-jp'>散歩がてら、パンを買ってきた。</span> (I went out for a walk and bought some bread while I was at it.)<br><b>かたがた</b> = close in meaning to がてら but more formal, mostly used for visits, thanks, and greetings. What follows is the actual action, and what comes before it is the extra purpose:<br><span class='gd-jp'>先日のお礼かたがた、ご挨拶に伺いました。</span> (I've come to visit and greet you, and also to thank you for the other day.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for someone juggling two roles long-term, use かたわら. For one trip out that covers two things, use がてら (or かたがた in formal situations). For two things done at the same moment, use ながら.</div>","n1-54":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜かろう」 is the <b>old-fashioned, written version</b> of 「〜いだろう」. It means \"probably ~\" or \"surely ~.\" <span class='gd-jp'>難しいだろう</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>難しかろう</span> with the same meaning. It just sounds like an editorial, a novel, or an old man in a period drama.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>It attaches only to い-adjectives</b>: drop the final 「い」 and add 「かろう」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>難しい</span> → drop い → <span class='gd-jp'>難しかろう</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒かろう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高かろう</span></span><br><b>Two to memorize</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よかろう</span></span> (it comes from <span class='gd-jp'>よい</span>)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なかろう</span></span><br>「〜たい」 conjugates like an い-adjective, so it works too: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰りたい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰りたかろう</span></span> (\"probably wants to go home badly\")</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Written conjecture</span>Used in articles and commentary to guess at a situation. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>さぞ</span> (\"surely\").</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>山の上はさぞ寒かろう。</span> (It must be freezing at the top of the mountain.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最初は苦しかろうが、すぐに慣れる。</span> (It'll probably be hard at first, but you'll get used to it quickly.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ではなかろうか</span>= \"Isn't it that ~?\" Very common in papers and editorials, and a softer way to state an opinion.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この計画には無理があるのではなかろうか。</span> (Isn't this plan a bit of a stretch?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>よかろう</span>An elder or boss saying \"Fine, all right,\" with a top-down, grudgingly-granting feel.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>まあ、よかろう。今回だけは許してやる。</span> (Well, fine. I'll let you off just this once.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Set phrase</span><span class='gd-jp'>安かろう悪かろう</span> = cheap means poor quality (you get what you pay for).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>安かろう悪かろうの時代は終わった。</span> (The era of \"cheap means poor quality\" is over.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・な-adjectives and nouns <b>cannot</b> take かろう: <span class='gd-jp'>静かかろう</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>静かだろう</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>静かであろう</span>. Nouns work the same way: use <span class='gd-jp'>学生だろう</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>学生であろう</span>.<br>・Verbs can't take it either. Say <span class='gd-jp'>行くだろう</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>行くであろう</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> must become <span class='gd-jp'>よかろう</span>. There is no 「いかろう」.<br>・「ろう」 is pronounced with a long vowel, like ろー.<br>・Saying <span class='gd-jp'>暑かろう</span> to a friend sounds like you're acting in a period drama. In everyday talk, just say <span class='gd-jp'>暑いだろう</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>よかろう</span> talks down to the listener, so it's very rude to say to a boss or teacher.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜かろうが／〜かろうと」 has two meanings, depending on the sentence:<br><b>\"Probably ~, but…\"</b> (conjecture, then contrast): <span class='gd-jp'>その試験は難しかろうが、準備すれば突破できる。</span> (That exam is probably hard, but you can pass if you prepare.)<br><b>\"Whether ~ or not…\"</b> (the い-adjective version of <span class='gd-jp'>〜ようが／〜ようと</span>): <span class='gd-jp'>高かろうが安かろうが、必要なものは買う。</span> (Whether it's expensive or cheap, I'll buy what I need.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>どんなに辛かろうと、最後までやり抜く。</span> (No matter how hard it is, I'll see it through to the end.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if 「どんなに」 or 「いくら」 comes before it, or two forms appear as a pair (高かろうが安かろうが) → \"no matter ~.\" If there's just one and it's followed by \"but…\" → \"probably ~, but.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The verb and noun version of \"no matter ~\" is <span class='gd-jp'>〜ようが／〜ようと</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ろうが、試合は行われる。</span> (Even if it rains, the match will go ahead.)</div>","n1-58":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とばかりに」 means <b>\"as if to say '~'\"</b>. The person doesn't say anything out loud, but their actions or expression clearly say something. You put that <b>unspoken line</b> in front, and then the action they take.<br>For example, your section chief keeps glancing at his watch. He says nothing, but everyone can tell he's saying \"Let's call it a day.\" That's when you use this.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing how someone looks or acts</span>You spell out the \"line\" behind the person's action. It's most common in novels, news reports, and scene descriptions.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「早く帰れ」とばかりに、課長は時計をちらちら見た。</span> (The section chief kept glancing at his watch, as if to say \"Hurry up and go home.\")</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「遊ぼう」とばかりに、犬がボールをくわえて走ってきた。</span> (The dog ran over with a ball in its mouth, as if to say \"Let's play!\")</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「待ってました」とばかりに、彼は勢いよく手を挙げた。</span> (He shot his hand up, as if to say \"I've been waiting for this!\")</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>What goes in front is <b>a line of dialogue</b>, so it can take almost any form: a command (<span class='gd-jp'>帰れ</span>), an invitation (<span class='gd-jp'>遊ぼう</span>), the ます form (<span class='gd-jp'>待ってました</span>), or even a single word or exclamation. It's often put in 「」 so you can see at a glance that it's a line.<br>Before a noun, it becomes <b>とばかりの</b>:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>「もう帰れ」とばかりの冷たい態度だった。</span> (He had a cold attitude, as if to say \"Just go home already.\")</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ここぞとばかりに</span><span class='gd-jp'>ここぞ</span> = \"this is the place, this is the moment.\" The whole phrase means \"seizing the chance, going all out,\" and it's very common.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会議で、彼はここぞとばかりに不満を言い始めた。</span> (At the meeting, he seized the chance and started airing all his complaints.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>今だとばかりに</span>\"Now's the time!\" Spotting the moment and acting right away.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>相手が転んだ瞬間、今だとばかりに走り出した。</span> (The moment his opponent fell, he bolted, as if to say \"Now!\")</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・This is a way of <b>watching someone from the outside</b>, so you don't normally use it about yourself. You know exactly what you're thinking, so there's no need for \"looks like I'm saying.\"<br>・The line in front was <b>never actually spoken</b>. If the person really said it, use 「〜と言って」 instead.<br>・The ばかり here doesn't mean \"only.\" Don't translate it as \"just ~.\"<br>・It isn't limited to the plain form. Commands, ます forms, and single words all work, because it's simply quoting a line.<br>・It's mostly used in writing and scene description, and is less common in everyday conversation.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>とばかりに</b> = a <b>line of dialogue</b> in front: \"as if to say '~'\"<br><b>んばかりに</b> = <b>an action about to happen</b> in front: \"so much that one almost ~\"<br>Same situation: a child is overjoyed to get a present.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「やった」とばかりに、子どもは両手を上げた。</span> (The child threw both hands up, as if to say \"Yes!\")</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもは飛び上がらんばかりに喜んだ。</span> (The child was so happy he almost jumped up.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>How to form んばかりに: change the verb to the ない form, drop 「ない」, and add 「ん」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飛び上がる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飛び上がらない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飛び上がらんばかりに</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泣き出す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泣き出さない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泣き出さんばかりに</span></span><br>※<b>する becomes 「せん」</b>, not 「しん」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>せんばかりに</span></span> (e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>今にも爆発せんばかりに</span>)<br>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>「帰れ」と言わんばかりに</span> (\"as if about to say 'Go home'\") means almost the same as <span class='gd-jp'>「帰れ」とばかりに</span>, so the two are interchangeable.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what's in front is a line of dialogue, use とばかりに. If it's an action that someone almost carries out, use んばかりに.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Since this is also about describing how someone looks or acts, move on to <span class='gd-jp'>〜んばかりに</span> (\"almost ~\"). Learn the two together and you're least likely to mix them up.</div>","n1-68":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜をもってしても」 means <b>“even with ～, still…”</b>. You put something impressive up front, something that should work (the latest technology, someone's ability), and then say “it still doesn't work” or “it's still hard.” The point is <b>“even something this strong can't do it,”</b> which shows how difficult the matter is.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It attaches directly to a noun: noun + をもってしても<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>最新の技術</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>最新の技術をもってしても</span></span> (even with the latest technology)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>彼の実力</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>彼の実力をもってしても</span></span> (even with his ability)<br>You can also put a person directly before it, meaning “even someone as capable as him”: <span class='gd-jp'>彼をもってしても</span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Technology, science, methods</span> Even the best method available can't do it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今の科学をもってしても、地震を正確に予知することはできない。</span> (Even with today's science, we can't predict earthquakes accurately.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Human ability</span> Even a very capable person finds it hard.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の実力をもってしても、優勝は難しいだろう。</span> (Even with his ability, winning the championship will probably be difficult.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天才と呼ばれた彼をもってしても、この問題は解けなかった。</span> (Even he, who was called a genius, couldn't solve this problem.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Language, expression</span> A common phrase in speeches and formal remarks, meaning your feelings are too strong to put into words.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どんな言葉をもってしても、この感謝の気持ちは伝えきれません。</span> (No words, whatever they may be, can fully express my gratitude.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What follows is almost always a result like <b>“can't do it, it's hard, it's still not enough”</b>, for example <span class='gd-jp'>できない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>難しい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>無理だ</span>. If you follow it with “and it succeeded,” the whole sentence sounds odd.<br>・What comes before it should be something <b>that was expected to be very useful</b>. If you put in something ordinary, like “with my pencil,” there's no “even this doesn't work” tension.<br>・This is a very formal, written expression, common in news, papers and speeches. In everyday speech, just say <span class='gd-jp'>最新の技術でも</span> (even the latest technology…).<br>・Its focus is “using this power to do something,” not “from the viewpoint of ～.” Don't translate it as “judging by ～.”</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>With the same “technology,” three forms give completely different results:<br><b>をもって</b> = with, by means of (simply stating the means): <span class='gd-jp'>この技術をもって、問題を解決した。</span> (We solved the problem with this technology.)<br><b>をもってすれば</b> = if you have ～, you can do it: <span class='gd-jp'>この技術をもってすれば、問題は解決できる。</span> (With this technology, the problem can be solved.)<br><b>をもってしても</b> = even with ～, you can't do it: <span class='gd-jp'>この技術をもってしても、問題は解決できない。</span> (Even with this technology, the problem can't be solved.)<br>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>をもって</span> has a use meaning “as a cutoff point,” as in <span class='gd-jp'>本日をもって閉店します。</span> (This shop closes as of today.). That has nothing to do with をもってしても, so don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: look at the short part in the middle. 「すれば」 means having it is enough; 「しても」 means even having it isn't enough.</div>","n1-71":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ずにはすまない」 means <b>“it won't do not to ～” or “you can't get away without ～ing.”</b> <span class='gd-jp'>すむ</span> (済む) means “a matter is settled, it's acceptable,” so <span class='gd-jp'>すまない</span> means “it can't be settled.” The whole phrase says: given what's reasonable and the current situation, you can't wrap this up without doing it.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Morally obliged</span> You made a mistake or caused trouble. Not apologizing, compensating or taking responsibility simply wouldn't be acceptable.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人の車に傷をつけたのだから、弁償せずにはすまない。</span> (Since you scratched someone's car, you can't get away without paying for it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Forced by circumstances</span> The matter has blown up, and there's no way to close it without dealing with it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここまで問題が大きくなれば、社長も記者会見をせずにはすまないだろう。</span> (With the problem this big, the president probably can't avoid holding a press conference.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>A reason often comes first, such as <span class='gd-jp'>〜のだから</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜以上</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>〜たら</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>だろう</span> is often added at the end, meaning “as things stand, it looks unavoidable.”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ずにはすまない</b>: take the ない-form, remove 「ない」, then add 「ずにはすまない」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>謝る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>謝らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>謝らずにはすまない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>払う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>払わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>払わずにはすまない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>認める</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>認めない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>認めずにはすまない</span></span><br><b>Remember the irregular verbs</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>せずにはすまない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ずにはすまない</span>（こずに）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ないではすまない</b>: add 「ではすまない」 directly to the ない-form without removing anything. The meaning is the same, but the tone is a little less stiff.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会議を無断で休んだのだから、部長に説明しないではすまない。</span> (You skipped the meeting without notice, so you can't get away without explaining to the department head.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>There is also <span class='gd-jp'>〜ではすまない</span> after a noun or 「〜だけ」, meaning “that alone won't settle it”:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冗談ではすまない。</span> (This can't be brushed off as a joke.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>謝るだけではすまない。</span> (Just apologizing won't settle it.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・する becomes <b>せずに</b>, not 「しずに」. The correct form is <span class='gd-jp'>説明せずにはすまない</span>.<br>・The <span class='gd-jp'>すまない</span> here doesn't mean “sorry.” It means “the matter can't be settled.”<br>・This is a written, formal expression, more common in news, commentary and formal settings. When chatting with friends and saying “you should apologize,” Japanese people usually say <span class='gd-jp'>謝らないとまずいよ</span> or use <span class='gd-jp'>謝らないわけにはいかない</span>.<br>・The point is that “external reason or circumstances push you to do it.” It isn't that you badly want to do it, nor that you can't help it emotionally.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same act of “apologizing.” Three similar-looking forms mean very different things:<br><b>ずにはすまない</b> = it wouldn't be acceptable not to do it (forced by the situation)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ミスをしたのは私だから、謝らずにはすまない。</span> (I'm the one who made the mistake, so I can't get away without apologizing.)<br><b>ずにはいられない</b> = you can't help it emotionally (covered at N2)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の涙を見て、謝らずにはいられなかった。</span> (Seeing his tears, I couldn't help apologizing.)<br><b>ずにはおかない</b> = I'll make it happen, I won't rest until it's done (strong will)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>今度こそ、彼に謝らせずにはおかない。</span> (This time, I will definitely make him apologize.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: “it wouldn't be acceptable not to” is すまない, “can't help it” is いられない, and “won't rest until it's done” is おかない.</div>","n1-66":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜いかんによらず／いかんにかかわらず」 means <b>\"regardless of how ~ is, always ...\"</b>, the kind of phrase you'd see in official documents. You put something that can vary (a reason, a result, the weather) up front, and the conclusion that follows <b>doesn't change because of it</b>. You'll see it most on notices, contracts, and ticket refund rules, and almost never in everyday chat.</div><div class='gd-h'>Breaking it down</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>いかん</span> is written <span class='gd-jp'>如何</span> in kanji and means \"how the content or situation is.\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>によらず</span> = not depending on, not determined by; <span class='gd-jp'>にかかわらず</span> = having no bearing on ~.<br>The two mean the same thing: \"no matter how ~ is.\" There's another common variant too: <span class='gd-jp'>いかんを問わず</span> (regardless of ~).</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + の + いかんによらず／いかんにかかわらず<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>理由</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>理由のいかんによらず</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>天候</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>天候のいかんにかかわらず</span></span><br>The noun before it should be something that <b>has several possibilities</b>, such as <span class='gd-jp'>理由</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>結果</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>事情</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>国籍</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>天候</span>.<br>In formal documents, the の is sometimes dropped, giving <span class='gd-jp'>理由いかんにかかわらず</span>. It's enough to recognize it; when you write your own sentences, stick with the version with の.</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll see most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Rules and notices</span> A shop or company is saying \"no exceptions.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>理由のいかんを問わず、購入後のキャンセルはできません。</span> (Regardless of the reason, purchases cannot be canceled.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天候のいかんにかかわらず、イベントは予定通り開催されます。</span> (Regardless of the weather, the event will be held as scheduled.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Eligibility and conditions</span> Everyone is treated equally.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>国籍のいかんによらず、どなたでも応募できます。</span> (Regardless of nationality, anyone can apply.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Only <b>nouns</b> can come before it, not verbs or adjectives directly: <span class='gd-jp'>行くいかんによらず</span> is wrong.<br>・Put a word that has \"various situations\" in front. <span class='gd-jp'>雨のいかんにかかわらず</span> doesn't work, because \"rain\" has no \"how it is\" to speak of. Say <span class='gd-jp'>天候のいかん</span> instead.<br>・Here <span class='gd-jp'>如何</span> is read <b>いかん</b>. The same kanji is read いかが in <span class='gd-jp'>如何ですか</span>, so don't confuse them.<br>・It's very stiff, and sounds odd with friends. In conversation, just say <span class='gd-jp'>どんな理由でも</span> (for whatever reason).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜いかんで／いかんによっては</b> (N1, the \"depends on\" pattern) means exactly the <b>opposite</b>: the outcome <b>depends on</b> how the preceding thing is.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>面接の結果いかんで、採用が決まります。</span> (Whether you're hired depends on the interview result.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>面接の結果のいかんにかかわらず、交通費は支給します。</span> (Regardless of the interview result, we will pay your travel expenses.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜にかかわらず</b> (N2) is the more general \"regardless.\" It doesn't need いかん, and you can put words like <span class='gd-jp'>天気</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>年齢</span> in front: <span class='gd-jp'>天気にかかわらず、試合は行われます。</span> (Regardless of the weather, the match will be held.)<br>Adding 「のいかん」 makes it more formal, with the tone of an official document.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if いかん is followed by 「によらず／にかかわらず／を問わず」, it means \"regardless.\" If it's followed by 「で／によっては」, it means \"depends on.\"</div>","n1-73":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「ただ～のみ」 means <b>\"only ~\"</b> or <b>\"all that's left is ~\"</b>. It's the formal, literary version of 「だけ」. Its most common use is to express resolve: you've done everything you can, and only one road is left in front of you. For example, <span class='gd-jp'>あとはただ待つのみだ</span> (All that's left now is to wait). The tone is firm, as if you're saying \"I won't think about anything else. I'll just focus on this.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Only this</span>Stresses that the range is just this one thing and nothing else.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>合格したのは、ただ一人のみだった。</span>(Only one person passed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>The only thing left to do</span>At the end of a sentence it becomes 「～のみだ」, meaning you can stop worrying about anything else and just do this one thing. It's common in speeches, statements before a match, and news reports.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>やるべきことはすべてやった。あとはただ結果を待つのみだ。</span>(I've done everything I should. All that's left is to wait for the result.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今はただ無事を祈るのみです。</span>(All we can do now is pray they're safe.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Noun</b>: ただ + noun + のみ　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>一人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ただ一人のみ</span></span><br><b>Verb</b>: ただ + dictionary form + のみ　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>前進する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ただ前進するのみだ</span></span><br>To be more polite, change the final だ to です: <span class='gd-jp'>祈るのみです</span>.<br>You can drop 「ただ」 and use only 「のみ」 with the same meaning. Adding ただ just adds emphasis.</div><div class='gd-h'>The form you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun + あるのみ</span>To say \"the only thing left is to do this,\" 「ある」 is often added after the noun. It's practically a set phrase: <span class='gd-jp'>努力あるのみ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>前進あるのみ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>練習あるのみ</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試合まであと一週間。練習あるのみだ。</span>(One week until the match. All we can do is practice.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Signs</span>On the streets of Japan you'll often see 「のみ」 on its own, meaning \"only ~\" or \"~ only.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この席は会員のみご利用いただけます。</span>(This seat is for members only.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>関係者のみ</span>(Authorized personnel only)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It's very formal and dramatic. Saying 「ただ食べるのみ」 to a friend while chatting about lunch would sound like you're acting in a play. In everyday speech, just use <span class='gd-jp'>だけ</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>あとは待つだけだ</span>.<br>・Verbs take the dictionary form, never the ます form. Say <span class='gd-jp'>前進するのみです</span>, not 「前進しますのみ」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>のみならず</span> looks similar, but it means <b>\"not only ~\"</b>, which is the opposite: <span class='gd-jp'>ただ日本のみならず、世界中で人気がある。</span> (It's popular not only in Japan but all over the world.) When you see ならず after のみ, switch your reading to \"not only.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>～のみだ</b> = <b>this is the only road</b> in front of you, so you focus and push forward: <span class='gd-jp'>ここまで来たら、ただ前進するのみだ。</span> (Now that we've come this far, all that's left is to move forward.)<br><b>～まで（のこと）だ</b> = <b>\"the worst that can happen is ~\"</b>. It's a fallback you've already thought out, and it's often preceded by 「だめなら」 or 「失敗したら」: <span class='gd-jp'>だめなら、もう一度挑戦するまでだ。</span> (If it doesn't work, I'll just try again.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you could say \"all that's left is to ~\" or \"the only thing to do is ~\" in English, use のみ. If it's \"if that fails, I'll just ~\" with an \"if it doesn't work\" before it, use までだ.</div>","n1-74":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ないものでもない」 means <b>\"it's not that I won't ~\"</b> or <b>\"I suppose I could ~\"</b>. Say a friend asks you to help them move, and you answer, \"Well, depends on whether you buy me dinner. I wouldn't say no.\" It means it's possible, but you're not exactly eager, and you're not agreeing outright.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its negative ない form, then add <b>ものでもない</b> directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>引き受ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>引き受けない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>引き受けないものでもない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>許す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>許さない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>許さないものでもない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>間に合う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>間に合わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>間に合わないものでもない</span></span><br>Two negatives (\"not\" + \"not the case that\") cancel out and become a <b>reserved \"yes, it's possible.\"</b></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Agreeing with conditions</span>The most common use. It often comes after 「〜ば」, 「〜なら」, or 「〜次第では」 (depending on ~). You set the condition first, then say \"I wouldn't say no.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>条件次第では、引き受けないものでもない。</span>(Depending on the terms, I might not turn it down.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちゃんと謝るなら、許さないものでもない。</span>(If you apologize properly, I might not refuse to forgive you.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Still possible</span>You judge that something isn't completely hopeless.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あと一週間あれば、間に合わないものでもない。</span>(With one more week, we might not miss the deadline.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Grudging understanding</span>You say you \"kind of get\" someone else's way of thinking.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の気持ちも分からないものでもない。</span>(I can't say I don't understand how he feels.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't drop 「ない」.</b> If you write <span class='gd-jp'>引き受けるものでもない</span>, it means \"it's not something you should take on,\" which is the opposite. Likewise, <span class='gd-jp'>お金で買えるものでもない</span> means \"it's not something money can buy,\" which has nothing to do with this grammar.<br>・It only means \"<b>it's possible</b>.\" It doesn't mean \"I definitely will\" or \"I really want to.\" Translating it as \"I'm happy to\" or \"no problem\" goes too far.<br>・The tone is stiff and written-style, and it often carries a slightly <b>superior \"I'll grudgingly agree\" or \"I'll think about it\"</b> attitude. Saying 「手伝わないものでもないです」 to your boss or a customer would be rude, so avoid it when you need to be polite.<br>・What comes before it is usually your own action or judgment, such as taking something on, considering it, or understanding it. It's rarely used to talk about other people's actions.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Several patterns mean almost the same thing: <b>ないこともない</b>, <b>ないでもない</b>, and <b>なくもない</b>. The difference is the setting:<br><b>ないものでもない</b>: written and stiff. Often found in articles, negotiations, and fairly formal statements.<br><span class='gd-jp'>条件が合えば、協力しないものでもない。</span> (If the terms fit, I wouldn't rule out cooperating.)<br><b>ないこともない／ないでもない</b>: the most common in everyday conversation.<br><span class='gd-jp'>時間があれば、行けないこともないよ。</span> (If I have time, I could go, I guess.)<br><b>なくもない</b>: more casual and offhand.<br><span class='gd-jp'>まあ、分からなくもないけど。</span> (Well, I can't say I don't get it.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: they all mean \"it's not that I can't ~.\" Use 「ないこともない」 when chatting. Save 「ないものでもない」 for writing, negotiating terms, or when you want to sound a bit lofty.</div>","n1-79":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「ともすると／ともすれば」means <b>\"all too easily,\" \"before you know it,\" \"tends to ~\"</b>. It describes an <b>undesirable tendency</b>: it doesn't happen every time, but the moment you let your guard down, things drift in that direction. Think of how, once you have free time, you easily end up scrolling your phone until midnight.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's a <b>standalone word</b>. It doesn't attach to anything. Just put it at the start of the sentence, or right before the bad thing you're talking about.<br>The second half usually has an expression meaning \"easy to ~\", and the two together sound most natural:<br><span class='gd-tag'>Common pairings</span><span class='gd-jp'>〜がちだ</span> (tends to ~), <span class='gd-jp'>〜やすい</span> (easy to ~), <span class='gd-jp'>〜てしまう</span> (end up ~ing)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人はともすると、楽なほうへ流れがちだ。</span> (People tend to drift toward the easier option.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しいと、ともすれば食事を抜いてしまう。</span> (When I'm busy, I often end up skipping meals.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一人暮らしだと、ともすると生活が不規則になりやすい。</span> (When you live alone, your lifestyle easily becomes irregular.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>「ともすると」and「ともすれば」mean the same thing and are interchangeable. Just pick whichever sounds better to you.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It isn't a grammar pattern that attaches to the word before it, so you can't write「忙しいともすると」. Keep it separate: <span class='gd-jp'>忙しいと、ともすると〜</span>.<br>・It describes a <b>bad tendency</b>. You wouldn't use it for good habits, e.g. to say \"I often find myself exercising without noticing,\" ともすると doesn't fit.<br>・It describes a \"recurring tendency,\" not a one-time event. \"I accidentally came late yesterday\" is just a one-off slip, so skip ともすると and simply say <span class='gd-jp'>うっかり遅刻した</span>.<br>・The tone is written and formal, and it's common in essays, speeches, and commentary. In casual chat with friends, people more often say <span class='gd-jp'>つい</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>すぐ</span>, for example <span class='gd-jp'>ついスマホを見ちゃう</span> (I can't help checking my phone).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ともすると／ともすれば</b>: stands alone in the sentence and describes a <b>bad tendency you easily fall into</b>. It's often paired with がち or やすい.<br><b>ともなると／ともなれば</b>: attaches to a noun (mostly a status, position, time, or degree), and can also attach to the dictionary form of a verb. It means <b>\"once you reach a certain status, period, or situation, naturally ~\"</b>.<br>Here's a comparison, both about being so busy you skip meals:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しいと、ともすると食事を抜きがちだ。</span> (When busy, I tend to skip meals: a bad habit.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年末ともなると、食事をする時間もない。</span> (Once it's year-end, there isn't even time to eat: that's just how it is at that time of year.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>More examples of ともなると: <span class='gd-jp'>社長ともなると、なかなか休めない。</span> (Once you're a company president, it's hard to take time off.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>海外に住むともなると、準備が大変だ。</span> (When it comes to actually living abroad, the preparation is a lot of work.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if there's a noun or verb before it and the meaning is \"once you get to that stage, naturally ~\", it's ともなると. If it stands alone and means \"tends to ~ (something bad)\", it's ともすると.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>For another way to talk about a bad tendency, look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜嫌いがある</span>, which goes at the end of the sentence: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は物事を悲観的に考える嫌いがある。</span> (He has a tendency to look at things pessimistically.) The two are often used together: <span class='gd-jp'>人はともすると、自分に甘くなる嫌いがある。</span> (People tend to have a flaw of going too easy on themselves.)</div>","n1-82":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜を限りに」means <b>\"up to ~ and no more after that\"</b>. It's like saying in English \"today is the last day\" or \"this is my last job, then I'm done\": you draw a line, and what's before it exists but what's after it doesn't.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a noun for a point in time directly before it, then add「を限りに」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>今日</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>今日を限りに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>今回</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>今回を限りに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>本日</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>本日を限りに</span></span><br>The most common are words for \"this present point in time\", such as <span class='gd-jp'>今日・本日・今回・今月・今年度</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Resolving to stop</span>You cut ties with your past self, often with a sense of determination.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日を限りに、お酒はやめる。</span> (Today is my last drink. I'm quitting alcohol.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Announcing an ending</span>A shop closing, an athlete retiring, a system being abolished, often with a touch of emotion.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今回の試合を限りに、引退します。</span> (I'll retire after this match.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この路線は今年度を限りに廃止される。</span> (This line will be discontinued at the end of this fiscal year.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Another meaning</span>「<span class='gd-jp'>声を限りに</span>」is a fixed phrase meaning \"with all one's might, at the top of one's voice\". It has nothing to do with time, but you should recognize it when you see it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>声を限りに助けを呼んだ。</span> (I shouted for help at the top of my lungs.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It isn't a \"deadline.\" For a submission deadline, use までに: <span class='gd-jp'>金曜日までに出してください。</span> (Please submit it by Friday.) You can't say「金曜日を限りに出してください」.<br>・What follows is mostly something that <b>ends, stops, or won't be done anymore</b>, such as <span class='gd-jp'>やめる・引退する・閉店する・解散する</span>. If you just want to say \"I'll start trying hard from today,\" Japanese usually uses <span class='gd-jp'>今日から</span>.<br>・This is a formal, written-style expression. In everyday talk with friends, <span class='gd-jp'>今日でやめる</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>今日限りでやめる</span> is enough.<br>・Don't confuse it with \"verb + 限り\": <span class='gd-jp'>生きている限り</span> means \"as long as I'm alive\", which is a completely different meaning.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>をもって</b> can also mean \"ending with ~\". Both can be used in the same store-closing notice, but they feel different:<br><span class='gd-jp'>本日をもって閉店いたします。</span> (This store will close as of today.)<br>→ A formal, businesslike announcement that simply notifies people that things end on this date.<br><span class='gd-jp'>本日を限りに閉店いたします。</span> (Today is our last day of business.)<br>→ The focus is \"up to today and never again\", which feels more emotional, like a curtain falling.<br>Also, <b>をもって</b> can express a <b>start</b> too: <span class='gd-jp'>4月1日をもって新制度を実施します。</span> (The new system will be implemented starting April 1.) <b>を限りに</b> can only be used for the \"last\" point, never for a start.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use を限りに when you want to stress \"the last time, never again after this\"; use をもって for formal notices of a start or end on a given day.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The exact opposite is「<span class='gd-jp'>〜を皮切りに</span>」, meaning \"starting with ~, and then things unfold one after another\": <span class='gd-jp'>東京公演を皮切りに、全国ツアーが始まる。</span> (Starting with the Tokyo show, a nationwide tour begins.)</div>","n1-83":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>めく</span> means <b>\"has a touch of ~\" or \"gives off a hint of ~\"</b>. It hasn't fully become that thing yet, but you can already feel it. Say it suddenly turns warm one day in late February and you'd say \"It's starting to feel like spring.\" In Japanese, that's <span class='gd-jp'>春めいてきた</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Seasons slowly changing</span>This is the most common use, usually paired with <span class='gd-jp'>〜めいてきた</span>, meaning \"it's gradually starting to feel like ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一気に春めいてきた。</span>(It suddenly started to feel like spring.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>風が少し秋めいてきた。</span>(The wind is starting to feel a bit like autumn.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing people or ways of speaking</span>Use <span class='gd-jp'>〜めいた</span> before a noun to mean \"with a touch of ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女はどこか謎めいた人だ。</span>(She's a somewhat mysterious person.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皮肉めいた言い方はやめてよ。</span>(Stop talking in that sarcastic way.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>言い訳めいたことは言いたくない。</span>(I don't want to say anything that sounds like an excuse.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it and conjugate it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add <span class='gd-jp'>めく</span> straight onto a noun and the whole thing becomes a verb, specifically a <b>Group 1 (godan) verb</b> that conjugates like <span class='gd-jp'>書く</span>.<br>Before て and た, く changes to い:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>春めく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>春めいて</span></span> → <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>春めいてきた</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>謎めく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>謎めいた</span></span> → <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>謎めいた事件</span></span> (a mysterious incident)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Not every noun works.</b> <span class='gd-jp'>めく</span> only pairs with a small set of fixed words. Common ones are <span class='gd-jp'>春めく</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>秋めく</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>謎めく</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>皮肉めく</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>冗談めく</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>言い訳めく</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>説教めく</span>. Something like 「<span class='gd-jp'>学生めく</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>先生めく</span>」 sounds unnatural. Say <span class='gd-jp'>学生らしい</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>学生っぽい</span> instead.<br>・Before a noun, you almost always use <b>めいた</b>: say <span class='gd-jp'>謎めいた人</span>, not 謎めく人.<br>・The て-form is <span class='gd-jp'>めいて</span>, not めくて or めきて.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>春めいてきた</span> means \"it's starting to feel like spring,\" which is not the same as <span class='gd-jp'>春になった</span> (it's already spring).<br>・It has a written, literary feel and is very common in weather reports and novels. In casual chat with friends, <span class='gd-jp'>っぽい</span> is more common.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to talk about \"spring\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>だんだん春めいてきた。</span> (It's gradually starting to feel like spring.): the focus is on <b>the first signs appearing</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>今日は春らしい天気だ。</span> (Today is typical spring weather.): the focus is on <b>being a true, textbook example</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>春っぽい服を買った。</span> (I bought some spring-looking clothes.): <b>casual speech</b>, a loose \"kind of like ~.\"</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use めく for \"starting to give off a hint of ~\"; use らしい for \"truly has the qualities ~ should have\"; use っぽい for a casual \"it feels kind of ~\" in conversation.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>めく</span> has a sibling, <span class='gd-jp'>めかす</span>, meaning \"to deliberately make it seem like ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>冗談めかして本音を言った。</span> (He said what he really felt in a joking tone.)</div>","n1-84":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This grammar group means <b>\"a good ~ of it\" or \"as much as ~\"</b>. Put a number in front, and the speaker is stressing \"that's a big number!\", and <b>it may even be more than that</b>. Picture seeing a 30 kg bag and saying \"That's a good 30 kilos, easily!\" That's when you use this in Japanese.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>What comes before must be <b>a number + a unit</b> (100キロ、1万人、50万円), followed directly by からある / からする / からの. Which of the three you pick depends on what you're talking about.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>からある</span>Weight, length, height, distance, size.<br>Japanese naturally says 「重さが<b>ある</b>」 and 「長さが<b>ある</b>」, so for \"how heavy / how long it is\" you use ある.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>30キロからある荷物を一人で運んだ。</span>(I carried a good 30 kg of luggage all by myself.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>2メートルからある魚が釣れた。</span>(I caught a fish a good two meters long.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>片道100キロからある道のりを毎日車で通っている。</span>(He commutes by car every day, a full hundred kilometers each way.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>からする</span>Prices.<br>Japanese naturally uses する for \"how much something costs\": <span class='gd-jp'>この時計は100万円する。</span> (This watch costs 1 million yen.) So prices go with からする.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一泊10万円からするホテルに泊まった。</span>(I stayed at a hotel that costs a good 100,000 yen a night.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あのバッグは50万円からするらしい。</span>(I hear that bag costs as much as 500,000 yen.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>からの</span>Most often used for numbers of people, and it <b>must be followed by a noun</b> (の works like a possessive \"of\"). Amounts like money or debt can also take からの + noun.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会場には1万人からの人が集まった。</span>(As many as 10,000 people gathered at the venue.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼には1000万円からの借金がある。</span>(He has debts of as much as 10 million yen.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Only use it with <b>big numbers</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>1キロからある荷物</span> sounds odd, because one kilo isn't worth stressing.<br>・からする only covers <b>prices</b>. For weight, say <span class='gd-jp'>100キロからある</span>, not 100キロからする.<br>・からの always needs a noun after it: <span class='gd-jp'>1万人からの人</span> is fine, but ending a sentence on 「1万人からの。」 is not.<br>・This pattern is somewhat written and formal, and very common in news, reports and articles. You can say it to friends, but the more casual way is to use 「も」 (covered below).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Ordinary 「から」</b> = <b>starting from ~</b> (the lowest price in a range):<br><span class='gd-jp'>この店の時計は1万円からあります。</span> (Watches at this shop start from 10,000 yen.)<br><b>からする</b> = <b>a full ~ in cost</b> (stressing that it's expensive):<br><span class='gd-jp'>この時計は100万円からする。</span> (This watch costs a good 1 million yen.)<br>The first tells you where the cheapest option begins; the second says this one item is seriously expensive.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Number + も</b> = the casual \"wow, as many as ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>この荷物、30キロもあるよ。</span> (This bag is a whole 30 kilos, you know.)<br><b>からある</b> = more written, with the feel of \"at least this much, maybe even more\": <span class='gd-jp'>30キロからある荷物</span> (luggage of a good 30 kg)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: when a shop writes 「～円から」, it's stating the lowest price. When からある / からする / からの follows a number, the speaker is marveling at how big that number is. Use ある for weight and length, する for prices, and からの when a noun follows.</div>","n1-85":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようにも〜ない」 means <b>\"I'd like to ~, but I can't\"</b> or <b>\"even if I wanted to ~, I couldn't\"</b>, as in \"I'd run away if I could, but I can't\" or \"I'd forget it if I could, but I can't.\" You really want to do something, but the situation blocks you, so you <b>simply can't</b>. There's a note of helplessness in it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>逃げようにも逃げられない状況だった。</span>(It was a situation where I couldn't have escaped even if I'd wanted to.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Blocked by circumstances</span>An injury, no car, no way to contact someone... It's not that you don't want to; the conditions don't allow it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>終電がなくて、帰ろうにも帰れない。</span>(The last train is gone, so I can't get home even if I want to.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>足をけがしていて、歩こうにも歩けない。</span>(I've hurt my leg, so I can't walk even if I want to.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Blocked emotionally</span>Sometimes what holds you back is a feeling or a memory.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忘れようにも忘れられない思い出だ。</span>(It's a memory I can't forget, however much I'd like to.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>状況が深刻すぎて、笑おうにも笑えなかった。</span>(The situation was so serious that I couldn't even laugh if I'd wanted to.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Volitional form of the verb (「〜う／〜よう」, the \"let's ~\" form) + にも + the <b>same verb</b> in its \"can't do\" form (negative potential).<br>A reason is often slipped in between: <span class='gd-jp'>帰ろうにも、終電がなくて帰れない。</span><br>The second half doesn't have to repeat the verb. You can just state what is blocking you:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電話しようにも、番号が分からない。</span>(I'd call, but I don't know the number.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to conjugate: check the verb group first</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: In the first half, change the final sound to the 「お」-row sound of the same row and add 「う」. In the second half, change the final sound to the 「え」-row sound of the same row and add 「ない」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → change く to こ → <span class='gd-jp'>書こう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → change く to け → <span class='gd-jp'>書けない</span></span><br>Put together: <span class='gd-jp'>書こうにも書けない</span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰ろうにも帰れない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話そうにも話せない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会おうにも会えない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: In the first half, drop 「る」 and add 「よう」. In the second half, drop 「る」 and add 「られない」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>逃げる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>逃げようにも逃げられない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れようにも忘れられない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しようにもできない</span></span>, for example: <span class='gd-jp'>予約しようにも予約できない</span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ようにも来られない</span> (こようにもこられない)</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The first half must be in the volitional form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>帰ろうにも</span>, not 帰るようにも. It has nothing to do with the N4 「〜ように」 (so that ~), so don't attach the dictionary form just because you see 「よう」.<br>・The second half must mean <b>\"can't do it\"</b>, not \"won't do it\". Writing 帰らない would mean \"I choose not to go home\", which contradicts the \"want to go home\" in the first half.<br>・It assumes the speaker <b>really wants to do it</b>. If you're just too lazy or not interested, you can't use this pattern.<br>・For Group 2 \"can't do\", write <span class='gd-jp'>逃げられない</span> in formal writing and on exams. The spoken form 「逃げれない」 is common in conversation, but avoid it in writing.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to talk about \"escaping\" and how they differ:<br><b>ようにも</b> = you want to, but <b>simply can't</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>逃げようにも、ドアに鍵がかかっていて逃げられない。</span> (I'd like to escape, but the door is locked, so I can't.)<br><b>たところで</b> = even if you did it, <b>it's pointless</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>今さら逃げたところで、すぐに見つかるだろう。</span> (Even if you run now, you'll be found right away.)<br><b>ようと／ようが</b> = <b>no matter how you do it</b>, the result is the same: <span class='gd-jp'>どこへ逃げようと、必ず見つけ出す。</span> (No matter where you run, I'll find you.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use ようにも for \"want to but can't\", たところで for \"doing it is pointless\", and ようと／ようが for \"no matter how you do it\".</div>","n1-86":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とは言い条」 means <b>\"although it's said to be ~, but...\"</b> or <b>\"~ in name, but actually...\"</b>. You first acknowledge a claim, then immediately say that the real situation isn't that simple. The meaning is close to 「とはいえ」, but it's more old-fashioned and bookish, and you almost never hear it in everyday speech.</div><div class='gd-h'>First, understand the word 「言い条」</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>言い条</span> is read <b>いいじょう</b> and originally meant \"what someone says, a claim\". So 「Aとは言い条」 literally means \"it's said to be A, but that's just one way of putting it\". The implication is: <b>that's what people say, but reality is another matter</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Nouns attach directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>プロ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>プロとは言い条</span></span><br>Verbs and adjectives attach in the plain form (the form without ます or です): <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>分かっている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>分かっているとは言い条</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安いとは言い条</span></span><br>In practice, you'll see <b>nouns attached directly</b> most often.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Name doesn't match reality</span>The first part is a commonly accepted claim or name, and the second part points out that reality isn't like that.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>春とは言い条、朝晩はまだ冷える。</span>(Although it's supposedly spring, mornings and evenings are still cold.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>自由とは言い条、実際には多くの制約がある。</span>(It's called freedom in name, but in reality there are many restrictions.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Understand it, but can't do it</span>You admit the first part is true, but the result is still not what you'd hope.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>頭では分かっているとは言い条、なかなか実行に移せない。</span>(Although I understand it in my head, I can't seem to put it into action.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>プロとは言い条、ミスを犯すこともある。</span>(Even though they're professionals, they sometimes make mistakes.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The reading is <b>いいじょう</b>. 「条」 is read じょう (long vowel), so don't read it as 「いうじょう」 or a short 「じょ」.<br>・It goes in the <b>middle of a sentence</b>, and a \"but...\" clause must follow. You can't just end the sentence with it.<br>・It's very stiff written language, used in commentary, novels, and essays. Saying 「春とは言い条」 to a friend would sound like you're reading an old book; in conversation, <span class='gd-jp'>春だけど</span> is enough.<br>・On exams, it's enough to recognize it as \"although ~, but...\". In your own writing, 「とはいえ」 is less likely to sound odd.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three mean \"although ~, but...\". The difference is <b>how formal and how old-fashioned</b> they are. Let's compare them in the same situation:<br><b>とはいうものの</b>: Used in writing, and also in speech on formal occasions.<br></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>春とはいうものの、まだ寒い。</span>(Although it's spring, it's still cold.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>とはいえ</b>: Mainly written; very common in news and essays, and you'll also hear it in somewhat formal speech.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>春とはいえ、まだ寒い。</span>(Although it's spring, it's still cold.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>とは言い条</b>: The most archaic; today you'll see it almost only in essays and literature.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>春とは言い条、まだ寒い。</span>(Although it's spring, it's still cold.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: they all mean \"although ~, but...\". The more archaic and literary it sounds, the more likely it's 「とは言い条」. For your own writing, 「とはいえ」 is the safest choice.</div>","n1-90":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にかこつけて」means <b>\"using ~ as a pretext\"</b> or <b>\"under the guise of ~\"</b>. On the surface you're doing A, but what you really want to do is B. Say you announce \"I'm going on a business trip,\" then spend most of it shopping and eating out. That's the feeling of this phrase.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + にかこつけて, followed by the thing you really want to do.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>出張</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>出張にかこつけて</span></span> (under the guise of a business trip)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>仕事</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>仕事にかこつけて</span></span> (using work as an excuse)<br>The part before it is the \"respectable reason you can say out loud.\" The part after it is \"what you actually wanted to do.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Calling someone out</span>This is the most common use. You're annoyed that someone is hiding behind a legitimate reason, so there's a hint of criticism.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長は視察にかこつけて、毎年ハワイに行っている。</span> (The department head goes to Hawaii every year under the guise of an inspection.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は仕事にかこつけて、毎晩飲みに行っている。</span> (He uses work as an excuse to go out drinking every night.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Self-mockery</span>You can also use it about yourself. It's like admitting \"okay, I really just wanted to do that,\" with a slightly sheepish, funny tone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもの誕生日にかこつけて、自分が食べたいケーキを買った。</span> (Using my kid's birthday as a pretext, I bought the cake I wanted to eat.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨にかこつけて、ジムをサボった。</span> (I used the rain as an excuse to skip the gym.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The particle is <b>に</b>, not を. The similar everyday expression 「〜を口実に」 is the one that takes を. Don't mix the two up. 「出張をかこつけて」 is wrong.<br>・It always carries the sense that <b>\"the stated reason and the real purpose are different.\"</b> If you really did go on a business trip and just happened to see some sights, using this phrase would make it sound like a fake business trip and a real holiday.<br>・Because it carries criticism or self-mockery, it's not suitable for explaining your own schedule in formal situations. If you tell your boss 「出張にかこつけて…」, you're basically admitting you're slacking off.<br>・It's usually written in hiragana. The kanji form is 「託けて」, which you rarely see. Just be able to recognize it.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: you went sightseeing in Kyoto during a business trip.<br><b>がてら</b> = <b>\"while at it\"</b>. Both things are genuine and above board:<br><span class='gd-jp'>出張がてら、京都を観光した。</span> (I did some sightseeing in Kyoto while I was there on business.)<br><b>かたがた</b> = also \"while at it, and also,\" but more formal, often used for visits and thank-yous:<br><span class='gd-jp'>お礼かたがた、先生のお宅に伺った。</span> (I visited my teacher's home, also to express my thanks.)<br><b>にかこつけて</b> = the reason in front is just a <b>front</b>, and the real purpose is the thing that follows:<br><span class='gd-jp'>出張にかこつけて、京都を観光した。</span> (Under the guise of a business trip, I actually went sightseeing in Kyoto.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you genuinely meant to do both things, use 「がてら」, or 「かたがた」 in formal situations. If the first reason is just an excuse and the second thing is what you really wanted, use 「にかこつけて」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>In everyday conversation you'll hear 「〜を口実に」 more often. The meaning is almost the same, but the tone is more direct: <span class='gd-jp'>風邪を口実に、会社を休んだ。</span> (I used a cold as an excuse to take the day off work.)</div>","n1-92":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AとBが相まって」means <b>\"A and B come together and amplify each other.\"</b> It's like English \"a perfect storm\" or \"good weather on top of a long weekend\": either factor alone might be fine, but stacked together the result really stands out. You can use it for good or bad things, and it shows up a lot in news, commentary and essays.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It follows a <b>noun</b>. There are three common patterns:<br><span class='gd-tag'>AはBと相まって</span>The main subject A combines with another factor B.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の才能は努力と相まって、大きな成果を生んだ。</span> (His talent, combined with his effort, produced great results.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>AとBが相まって</span>Two factors are placed side by side, and together they cause the result that follows.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は料理の味と雰囲気の良さが相まって、いつも予約でいっぱいだ。</span> (The food and the great atmosphere together keep this restaurant fully booked.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Bも相まって</span>The most common form in news. 「も」 signals \"there were already other causes, and <b>on top of that</b> there's B.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>円安も相まって、外国人観光客が急増している。</span> (With the weak yen on top of that, the number of foreign tourists is soaring.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>If what comes before it is a whole clause, add 「こと」 first to turn it into a noun:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>駅から近い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>駅から近いことも相まって</span></span> (on top of being close to the station)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Read it <b>あいまって</b>, not 「そうまって」.<br>・You need <b>two or more factors</b>. You can't use it with a single cause. If rain canceled a game, just say <span class='gd-jp'>雨で試合が中止になった</span>. You can't say 「雨と相まって中止になった」.<br>・It isn't limited to good things. Bad things work too: <span class='gd-jp'>寝不足と疲れが相まって、熱を出してしまった。</span> (Lack of sleep combined with fatigue, and I ended up with a fever.) So don't think of it as \"complementing each other,\" which only suits positive things.<br>・This is a fairly formal, written-style expression. When chatting with friends, <span class='gd-jp'>〜もあって</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>〜も重なって</span> sounds more natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に加えて</b> = simply \"besides A, there's also B.\" It just lists things one by one:<br><span class='gd-jp'>円安に加えて、物価も上がっている。</span> (Besides the weak yen, prices are also rising.)<br><b>と相まって</b> = two factors <b>work together</b> to produce a result:<br><span class='gd-jp'>円安と物価高が相まって、生活が苦しくなっている。</span> (The weak yen and high prices combine to make life harder.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>とあって</b> (it differs by just one character, so it's easy to misread) = \"because it's such a special situation as ~, ...\". There is only one cause:<br><span class='gd-jp'>連休とあって、どこも混んでいる。</span> (Since it's a long holiday weekend, everywhere is crowded.)<br><b>と相まって</b> = two causes stacked together:<br><span class='gd-jp'>連休と好天が相まって、どこも混んでいる。</span> (The long weekend and the good weather together make everywhere crowded.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for just \"there's also ~,\" use に加えて. For \"because it's ~,\" use とあって. Use と相まって only when \"two factors stack up and amplify the effect.\"</div>","n1-87":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜かれ〜かれ」 gives you English phrases like <b>\"sooner or later,\" \"more or less,\" and \"for better or worse.\"</b> It isn't a pattern where you can swap in any words. It's <b>three fixed expressions</b>, so just memorize each set as a whole.</div><div class='gd-h'>Where it comes from</div><div class='gd-sec'>「かれ」 is a classical imperative form, roughly \"be it ~, so be it.\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>多かれ少なかれ</span> originally meant something like \"be it many, be it few,\" and later became \"many or few, it's there either way.\"<br>You don't need to study classical Japanese. Just know this: it puts two adjectives with <b>opposite meanings</b> side by side to say \"it's the same whichever way, and the outcome won't change.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>The three you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>遅かれ早かれ</span>Read it 「おそかれはやかれ」. It means \"sooner or later.\" It usually goes with something that's bound to happen, with a feeling of \"you can't avoid it,\" and it's common in everyday conversation too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>嘘は遅かれ早かればれるよ。</span>(Sooner or later, a lie gets exposed.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>遅かれ早かれ、決めなきゃいけないことだ。</span>(Sooner or later, this is something you have to decide.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>多かれ少なかれ</span>Read it 「おおかれすくなかれ」. It means \"more or less.\" It often goes with 「誰でも」 or 「みんな」, saying that everyone has it, just to different degrees.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>多かれ少なかれ、誰にでも悩みはある。</span>(Everyone has worries, more or less.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>善かれ悪しかれ</span>Read it 「よかれあしかれ」. It means \"for better or worse.\" It's more formal than the other two and is mostly used in writing and commentary.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>善かれ悪しかれ、スマホは私たちの生活を大きく変えた。</span>(For better or worse, smartphones have changed our lives enormously.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>You can't make your own</b>: when you see \"adjective + かれ,\" don't try plugging in <span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>大きい</span>. That doesn't work. It really is just these three.<br>・It's <b>悪しかれ</b>, not 「悪かれ」. This uses the classical 「悪し」, not the modern 「悪い」.<br>・The order is fixed: say <span class='gd-jp'>遅かれ早かれ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>多かれ少なかれ</span>, and don't flip them around.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>よかれと思って</span> on its own is a different expression. It means \"meaning well, thinking it would be good for the other person,\" and has nothing to do with \"for better or worse.\"<br>　<span class='gd-jp'>よかれと思って言ったのに、怒られた。</span>(I said it meaning well, and still got scolded.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of these mean \"whether A or B,\" but with <span class='gd-jp'>〜にせよ〜にせよ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜であれ〜であれ</span> you can swap in your own words, while with <span class='gd-jp'>〜かれ〜かれ</span> you can't.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>行くにせよ行かないにせよ、早めに連絡してね。</span>(Whether you go or not, let me know early.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨であれ雪であれ、試合は行われる。</span>(Rain or snow, the game will be held.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>遅かれ早かれ、ばれるよ。</span>(Sooner or later, it'll come out.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Conversely, you wouldn't say \"sooner or later\" as <span class='gd-jp'>遅いにせよ早いにせよ</span>. It isn't grammatically wrong, but it sounds like you're discussing two cases, \"slow\" and \"fast,\" rather than using the set phrase for \"sooner or later.\" Japanese speakers simply say <span class='gd-jp'>遅かれ早かれ</span>.<br>One more note: <span class='gd-jp'>であれ</span> attaches to nouns or na-adjectives and <b>not to i-adjectives</b>, so 「遅いであれ」 is wrong in itself.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for \"sooner or later / more or less / for better or worse,\" use the three fixed かれ sets. For any other \"whether A or B,\" use にせよ or であれ.</div>","n1-88":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜始末だ」 means <b>\"it got to the point where, of all things, ~\" or \"it ended with ~\"</b>. You describe a string of outrageous things, then drop an even worse ending, with a tone of <b>disbelief, exasperation, and reproach</b>. It's like complaining to a friend: \"He was late, fine, and he forgot his stuff too, and then what? He even got yelled at by his boss.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Criticizing someone</span>This is the classic use. You describe someone who keeps getting more ridiculous until they reach an extreme.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>息子は毎晩ゲームばかりして、ついには学校を休む始末だ。</span>(My son plays video games every night, and it's gotten to the point where he even skips school.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は何度注意しても聞かず、最近は返事もしない始末だ。</span>(No matter how many times I warn him he won't listen, and lately he doesn't even answer.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Self-deprecation</span>You can use it about yourself too, with a \"I'm hopeless\" kind of self-mockery.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寝坊して駅まで走ったのに、財布を家に忘れる始末だ。</span>(I overslept and ran all the way to the station, and then I even forgot my wallet at home.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb dictionary form + 始末だ: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休む始末だ</span></span><br>Verb ない form + 始末だ: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>しない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>返事もしない始末だ</span></span><br>Demonstrative + 始末だ: <span class='gd-jp'>この始末だ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>あの始末だ</span> (\"look at the mess it's ended up in\")</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あれほど言ったのに、この始末だ。</span>(I told you so many times, and look how it turned out.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What it usually looks like</div><div class='gd-sec'>It often comes with \"in the end\" words in front, which build up the feeling of things getting worse and worse: <span class='gd-jp'>ついには</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>とうとう</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>最後には</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>挙句の果てには</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>借金を重ね、挙句の果てには夜逃げする始末だ。</span>(He piled up debt, and in the end he even ended up skipping town in the night.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>When talking about the past, the verb in front stays in dictionary form, and you can end the sentence with <span class='gd-jp'>始末だった</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use the <b>dictionary form or ない form</b> in front, not the た form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>叱られる始末だ</span>, not 叱られた始末だ.<br>・It's <b>for bad outcomes only</b>. You can't use 始末だ for \"I studied hard and finally got in.\"<br>・You need the <b>buildup beforehand</b>. Tacking 始末だ onto a single line saying only that he showed up late sounds odd. First explain how things went downhill step by step.<br>・The tone is <b>reproachful and complaining</b>, so saying this about your boss or a customer to their face is very rude. It shows up more in complaints, writing, and commentary.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>始末</span> on its own also means \"dealing with, cleaning up,\" as in <span class='gd-jp'>後始末</span> (cleaning up afterward) and <span class='gd-jp'>始末書</span> (a written apology). That's a separate matter from this pattern.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>For \"ending up ~,\" there are also 「末に」 and 「羽目になる」 from N2. They differ in <b>whether the result is good, who it's about, and how the speaker feels</b>.<br><b>末に</b> = a conclusion after a process, <b>neutral</b>, and a good result is fine:<br><span class='gd-jp'>何度も話し合った末に、ゲームは一日一時間と決めた。</span>(After talking it over many times, we finally decided on one hour of games a day.)<br><b>羽目になる</b> = <b>you yourself</b> get forced into some troublesome task, with a sense of regret:<br><span class='gd-jp'>息子のゲームの課金を、私が払う羽目になった。</span>(I ended up having to pay for my son's in-game purchases.)<br><b>始末だ</b> = the speaker is <b>exasperated and reproaching</b> someone, as in \"it actually got to this point\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>息子はゲームに夢中で、ついには学校を休む始末だ。</span>(My son is so hooked on games that he's even skipping school now.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for a neutral \"in the end we decided ~,\" use 末に. For \"I got stuck having to ~,\" use 羽目になる. When you want to roll your eyes and say \"it ended with him actually ~,\" use 始末だ.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>It often appears together with 「〜あげく」: <span class='gd-jp'>さんざん迷ったあげく、何も買わずに帰ってくる始末だ。</span>(After dithering forever, he actually came home without buying anything.)</div>","n1-14":"<div class='gd-h'>The short version</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜なり」means <b>\"the moment ~, (they) immediately…\"</b>, with a hint of \"Whoa, that fast?\" surprise. It's like telling a friend: \"He walked in and ran straight into his room without even taking his shoes off.\" You use it to describe <b>someone else</b> who had just finished one action and right away did a second one that was a bit unexpected.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add「なり」directly to the dictionary form of the verb (the base form you'd look up in a dictionary). No conjugation needed.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰るなり</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞くなり</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見るなり</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing someone else's reaction</span>One action has only just finished, and the same person immediately does something else, and that something is a bit sudden.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は家に帰るなり、部屋にこもってしまった。</span>(The moment he got home, he shut himself in his room.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その知らせを聞くなり、彼女は泣き出した。</span>(As soon as she heard the news, she burst into tears.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>息子は私の顔を見るなり、走って逃げた。</span>(The moment my son saw my face, he ran away.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Storytelling tone</span>You'll mostly see it in novels and articles, or when you're \"telling someone about\" something that happened. In everyday chat, the「〜たとたん」you learned at N3 is more common for \"as soon as ~\".</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>The subject must be the same before and after.</b> \"The moment he got home, <b>he</b> shut himself in his room\" works. \"The moment the bell rang, the students rushed out\" changes the subject, so なり sounds off.<br>・<b>It's usually not used for yourself.</b> なり feels like an onlooker describing events. For \"I went to bed as soon as I got home,\" saying <span class='gd-jp'>家に帰ってすぐ寝た。</span> is more natural.<br>・What follows must be <b>something that has already happened</b>. You can't follow it with commands, invitations, or intentions:<br>×<span class='gd-jp'>家に帰るなり、宿題をしなさい。</span> (You can't use it to tell someone to do something.)<br>・The verb before it must be in dictionary form. Switching to the た-form,「<span class='gd-jp'>〜たなり</span>」, gives a different meaning: \"has stayed that way ever since,\" as in <span class='gd-jp'>出かけたなり、帰ってこない。</span> (He went out and hasn't come back since.)<br>・「<span class='gd-jp'>〜なり〜なり</span>」(either ~ or ~ is fine) and「<span class='gd-jp'>私なりに</span>」(in my own way) look the same but are completely different grammar. Don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>N1 has several \"as soon as ~\" patterns. They all attach to the dictionary form and are followed by something that already happened. The differences are in <b>who does it, what's emphasized, and where it's used</b>.<br><br><b>なり</b>: the same person does a slightly unexpected action.<br><span class='gd-jp'>部長は会議室に入るなり、大声で怒鳴り始めた。</span>(The moment the department head walked into the meeting room, he started shouting.)<br><br><b>や否や</b>: very formal and written. <b>The subjects before and after can differ.</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>ベルが鳴るや否や、学生たちは教室を飛び出した。</span>(The instant the bell rang, the students rushed out of the classroom.)<br><br><b>が早いか</b>: the focus is on <b>speed</b>. The second action happens almost at the same moment the first ends.<br><span class='gd-jp'>子どもたちは「いただきます」と言うが早いか、料理に手を伸ばした。</span>(The kids had barely finished saying \"Let's eat\" before they reached for the food.)<br><br><b>たとたん</b> (N3): everyday spoken style. <b>You can use it about yourself too</b>, and what follows is often something you can't control.<br><span class='gd-jp'>立ち上がったとたん、めまいがした。</span>(The moment I stood up, I felt dizzy.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: the same person suddenly does something → なり. Two different events follow each other, and it's written style → や否や. You want to stress lightning speed → が早いか. About yourself, or in everyday conversation → たとたん.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at the others in the same group,「<span class='gd-jp'>〜が早いか</span>」and「<span class='gd-jp'>〜や否や</span>」. Then compare them with「<span class='gd-jp'>〜そばから</span>」(no sooner has ~ than ~ again, for things that keep repeating). Memorizing these together is the best way to avoid mixing them up.</div>","n1-23":"<div class='gd-h'>The short version</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ゆえ（に）」is the formal, written-style <b>\"owing to ~\" / \"because of ~\"</b>. It means \"because,\" just like「から」and「ので」, but it has put on a suit and talks in a stiff, literary way. You'll often see it in news commentary, novels, and speeches, and almost never in casual chat with friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Nouns</span>The most common pattern is attaching it directly. You can also put「の」in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>若さ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>若さゆえ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>貧しさ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>貧しさのゆえに</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verbs and い-adjectives</span>Use the plain form (the dictionary-style form without ます), then add either「がゆえ」or「ゆえ」.「がゆえ」sounds more formal.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>人気がある</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>人気があるがゆえに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>若い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>若いがゆえに</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>な-adjectives and nouns used as predicates</span>Replace「だ」with <b>である</b> before attaching.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>真面目だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>真面目であるがゆえに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生であるがゆえに</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually see it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ゆえに、〜</span>A full clause stating the result follows.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人気があるがゆえに、予約がなかなか取れない。</span>(Because it's so popular, it's hard to get a reservation.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>責任ある立場ゆえに、軽率な発言は許されない。</span>(Because of his responsible position, careless remarks are not allowed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ゆえの＋noun</span>It modifies the following noun directly, meaning \"a ~ that comes from ~\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>経験不足ゆえの失敗だった。</span>(It was a failure caused by lack of experience.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>優しさゆえの嘘もある。</span>(There are also lies told out of kindness.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ゆえに at the start of a sentence</span>On its own at the start of a sentence, it means \"therefore\" or \"thus.\" It's common in mathematical proofs and philosophical writing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>我思う、ゆえに我あり。</span>(I think, therefore I am.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns and な-adjectives <b>can't take「だ」</b>. It's not「学生だがゆえに」, you say <span class='gd-jp'>学生であるがゆえに</span>.<br>・In everyday conversation it sounds like you're acting in a period drama or reciting lines. To a friend, \"because I'm busy\" is just <span class='gd-jp'>忙しいから</span>. Saying「忙しいがゆえに」would make people laugh.<br>・It can be written with the kanji「故」. When you see <span class='gd-jp'>若さ故に</span>, don't think it's some other grammar. It's still read ゆえに.<br>・What follows is often a rather negative result, or a \"on the contrary ~\" outcome, with a sense of \"it's precisely because of this trait that it turns out this way,\" as in <span class='gd-jp'>真面目であるがゆえに、かえって批判されることもある。</span> (Precisely because he's serious, he is sometimes criticized for it.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>から／ので</b> = the ordinary \"because,\" fine in both speech and writing: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもを愛しているから、厳しくする。</span> (I'm strict because I love my child.)<br><b>がゆえに</b> = the same meaning in a written style, stating the reason objectively: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもを愛するがゆえに、厳しくする。</span> (Owing to my love for my child, I am strict with him.)<br><b>ばこそ</b> = strongly emphasizes \"it's <b>precisely</b> because of this and nothing else.\" The speaker is often defending their own approach, and the sentence often ends in のだ: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもを愛すればこそ、厳しくするのだ。</span> (It's precisely because I love my child that I'm strict with him.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if you're just stating \"because\" in a more formal way, use ゆえに. If you want to stress \"this is exactly the reason, and I want you to understand me,\" use ばこそ.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another N1 grammar point for giving reasons is <span class='gd-jp'>〜とあって</span> (because of a special situation like ~). Try memorizing it alongside ゆえに so you can compare them.</div>","n1-76":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ではあるまいし」means <b>“It's not like you're ~, so (why… / you should…)”</b>. Picture your mom seeing you cry and saying, “You're not a little kid, so stop crying.” That's the tone. You first state something that is <b>obviously not true</b>, then use it to scold or advise the other person.</div><div class='gd-h'>Where it comes from</div><div class='gd-sec'>Break it down and it makes sense: <span class='gd-jp'>ではあるまい</span> is a slightly literary “it probably isn't ~”, and <span class='gd-jp'>し</span> means “since ~”. Together: “It's not like it's ~, so…”<br>In speech people almost always say <b><span class='gd-jp'>じゃあるまいし</span></b>. <span class='gd-jp'>ではあるまいし</span> shows up more in writing or in somewhat formal settings.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun</span>Attaches directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子供</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子供じゃあるまいし</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供じゃあるまいし、自分のことは自分でやりなさい。</span>(You're not a kid, so take care of your own things.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>神様じゃあるまいし、明日のことなんて分からないよ。</span>(I'm not a god, so how would I know what happens tomorrow?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verb</span>You must put <b><span class='gd-jp'>の</span> (<span class='gd-jp'>ん</span> in speech) or <span class='gd-jp'>わけ</span></b> in between. You can't attach it directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遊びに行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊びに行くんじゃあるまいし</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会えない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会えないわけじゃあるまいし</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>遊びに行くんじゃあるまいし、もっとちゃんとした服を着ていきなさい。</span>(You're not going out to hang around, so wear something more proper.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一生会えないわけじゃあるまいし、そんなに泣かないで。</span>(It's not like you'll never see each other again, so don't cry so much.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What usually follows</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half is what you really want to say. It's almost always <b>a command, prohibition, advice, criticism, or your own judgment</b>: “don't ~ anymore,” “do it yourself,” “this shouldn't happen.” The first half usually uses something so exaggerated that everyone knows it's not true, like <span class='gd-jp'>子供</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>神様</span>, or <span class='gd-jp'>素人</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>素人じゃあるまいし、こんなミスは許されないよ。</span>(You're not an amateur, so a mistake like this is unacceptable.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Spoken inversion</span>In conversation people often tack it on at the end as an afterthought.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いちいち説明しなくても分かるよ。子供じゃあるまいし。</span>(You don't have to explain every little thing. I get it. I'm not a kid.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・A verb must be preceded by <span class='gd-jp'>の／ん／わけ</span>. It's <span class='gd-jp'>死ぬわけじゃあるまいし</span>, not 「死ぬじゃあるまいし」. Nouns are the opposite: don't add の. You won't hear 「子供のじゃあるまいし」.<br>・The first half must be something <b>everyone knows is “obviously not the case”</b>. If you're just stating a plain reason, like “I'm not a student, so I pay the full fare,” this pattern sounds odd. Use <span class='gd-jp'>〜ではないので</span> instead.<br>・The tone is <b>scolding or snapping back</b>, with a hint of impatience. Saying it directly to a boss or a customer would be very rude.<br>・It is not a “probably not ~” guess. The point is the scolding or advice in the second half.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜じゃないんだから</b>: Almost the same meaning, “it's not like you're ~, you know,” but more everyday and much more common. Even learners below N1 use it.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供じゃないんだから、泣かないで。</span>(You're not a kid, so stop crying.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供じゃあるまいし、泣かないで。</span>(Same as above, but a bit old-fashioned, with more of a scolding flavor.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜ではあるまいか</b>: Only the last syllable differs, but the meaning is completely different. It's a literary “could it be ~? / it's probably ~,” used for conjecture.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供ではあるまいし、一人で行けるだろう。</span>(You're not a kid, so you can surely go by yourself.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は何か隠しているのではあるまいか。</span>(Could it be that he's hiding something?)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if it ends in 「し」 followed by a scolding line, it means “it's not like you're ~.” If it ends in 「か」, it's a guess: “could it be ~?”</div>","n1-27":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たところで」 means <b>\"even if you do X, it won't help\"</b> or <b>\"so what if you do X?\"</b>. It's the tone of English lines like \"What's the use of crying now?\" or \"No matter how you explain it, he won't believe you.\" It sounds a bit resigned and a bit of a cold shower: whatever you do in the first part will be wasted effort.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今さら謝ったところで、関係は戻らないだろう。</span>(Even if you apologize now, the relationship won't go back to how it was.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in the た-form (the past-tense form), then add 「ところで」 directly.<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>急ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>急いだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>急いだところで</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言ったところで</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>慌てる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>慌てた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>慌てたところで</span></span><br><b>Group 3 (する・来る)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>したところで</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来た</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来たところで</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Doing it is pointless</span>This is the most common use. The first part often has 「<span class='gd-jp'>いくら</span>」, 「<span class='gd-jp'>どんなに</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>今さら</span>」, and the ending is a futile result such as 「～ない」, 「無駄だ」 or 「仕方がない」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どんなに急いだところで、最終便には間に合わない。</span>(No matter how much you hurry, you won't make the last service.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いくら説明したところで、わかってもらえないだろう。</span>(No matter how much I explain, he probably won't understand.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At best, only ~</span>With 「<span class='gd-jp'>せいぜい</span>」 in the second part, it means \"even if you do it, the result won't amount to much.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今から急いだところで、せいぜい5分早く着くくらいだ。</span>(Even if we hurry now, we'll arrive 5 minutes earlier at best.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It uses the た-form, but it talks about a <b>hypothetical</b>, not something that already happened. 「謝ったところで」 means \"even if (you) apologize,\" not \"you apologized.\"<br>・The second part must be a useless or underwhelming result. For a good result like \"if you try hard, you'll pass,\" you can't say 頑張ったところで合格できる. Use <span class='gd-jp'>頑張れば合格できる</span>.<br>・The second part is your own judgment or guess, often ending in 「～だろう」 or 「～わけではない」. It doesn't take requests like 「～てください」.<br>・The same 「たところで」 has another meaning: \"at the point when ~, just as ~ wraps up,\" followed by the next action: <span class='gd-jp'>話が一段落したところで、休憩しましょう。</span> (Now that the discussion has reached a stopping point, let's take a break.) Check what follows: if it's \"pointless,\" it's the meaning in this lesson; if it's \"then do something,\" it's the timing meaning.<br>・The tone is cold and resigned. Telling someone directly \"it's no use doing that\" can feel like a cold shower, so be careful with context.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ても</b> = the ordinary \"even if.\" It's neutral, and the result can be good or bad: <span class='gd-jp'>彼に説明しても、わかってもらえないだろう。</span> (Even if I explain it to him, he probably won't understand.)<br><b>たところで</b> = strongly stresses \"it'll be wasted effort,\" and sounds more resigned than ても: <span class='gd-jp'>彼に説明したところで、わかってもらえないだろう。</span> (Even if I explain it to him, it's no use. He won't understand.)<br><b>ようと</b> = \"no matter what ~, I'll still ~.\" The focus is on the second part being unaffected, so it can carry your own determination: <span class='gd-jp'>みんなが反対しようと、私はこの計画を進める。</span> (No matter how much everyone objects, I'm going ahead with this plan.)<br>Say the same thing about opposition with たところで and it becomes: <span class='gd-jp'>みんなが反対したところで、社長の考えは変わらない。</span> (Even if everyone objects, it's no use. The president won't change his mind.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: use たところで for \"it's pointless to do it,\" ようと for \"no matter what, I'll do it,\" and ても for a plain \"even if.\"</div>","n1-65":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜いかん」 means <b>\"depending on ~\"</b> or <b>\"it all comes down to ~\"</b>, in the stiff register of official English like \"contingent upon\" or \"hinges on.\" It's written with the kanji 如何, but today it's mostly written in hiragana as <span class='gd-jp'>いかん</span>. The meaning: how things turn out depends on the state of the first thing. It's very stiff and formal, found in news, notices, contracts and managers' speeches.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It attaches only to a <b>noun</b>. The 「の」 in between is optional, and leaving it out is more common.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>努力</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>努力いかん</span></span> (depends on the level of effort)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>試験の結果</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>試験の結果いかん</span></span> (depends on the exam result)<br>The noun must be something that <b>can be good or bad, high or low</b>, for example: <span class='gd-jp'>結果</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>努力</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>態度</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>対応</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>天候</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>返答</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>内容</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>いかんだ／いかんにかかっている</span>At the end of a sentence: \"it's up to ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>合格できるかどうかは、本人の努力いかんだ。</span>(Whether you pass or not depends on the person's own effort.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今期の業績は、チームの努力いかんにかかっている。</span>(This quarter's results depend on the team's efforts.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>いかんで／いかんによって</span>In the middle of a sentence: \"decided by, or changing according to, ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>参加費は人数いかんで変わります。</span>(The participation fee varies depending on the number of people.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>いかんでは／いかんによっては</span>With the extra 「は」, the meaning becomes \"depending on the situation, <b>it's even possible that</b> ~.\" The second part usually describes a special, often unwelcome outcome.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天候いかんでは、試合が中止になることもあります。</span>(Depending on the weather, the match may even be cancelled.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会社の対応いかんでは、訴訟も考えています。</span>(Depending on how the company responds, we are also considering a lawsuit.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Read it <b>いかん</b>. The same kanji 如何 is read いか in <span class='gd-jp'>如何ですか</span> (read いかが), so don't mix them up.<br>・It can't attach directly to a verb or adjective. To say \"depending on whether you try hard,\" write <span class='gd-jp'>努力いかんで</span>, not 「頑張るいかんで」.<br>・The noun must be a variable one. A word like 「明日」, which has no good or bad, high or low, can't be used.<br>・It's too formal. Saying 「天気いかんで決めよう」 to a friend sounds very odd. In casual speech, use <span class='gd-jp'>天気次第で決めよう</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>天気によって決めよう</span>.<br>・In casual speech, the standalone phrase <span class='gd-jp'>それはいかん</span> means \"that won't do.\" It's a different word and has nothing to do with this grammar.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>次第</b> (N2) = almost the same meaning, but it works in both spoken and written Japanese and feels more relaxed:<br><span class='gd-jp'>結果次第で、計画を変えるかもしれない。</span> (Depending on the result, we may change the plan.)<br><b>いかん</b> = the same meaning in the tone of official notices and reports:<br><span class='gd-jp'>結果いかんでは、計画を変更することもあり得る。</span> (Depending on the result, the plan may also be changed.)<br><b>いかんによらず／いかんにかかわらず</b> (n1-66) = the opposite meaning: \"<b>regardless</b> of ~, it's the same.\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>理由のいかんにかかわらず、返金はいたしません。</span> (Regardless of the reason, we do not offer refunds.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if いかん is followed by 「で・によって・だ・にかかっている」, it means \"<b>it depends on it</b>\"; if it's followed by 「によらず・にかかわらず・を問わず」, it means \"<b>regardless of it</b>.\" To sound more casual, swap いかん for 次第.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, learn <span class='gd-jp'>いかんによらず</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>いかんにかかわらず</span> in n1-66, as well as <span class='gd-jp'>理由のいかんを問わず</span> (regardless of the reason), which you often see in notices.</div>","n1-78":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AあってのB」 means <b>\"no A, no B\"</b> or <b>\"B exists only because of A\"</b>. A is the precondition for B to exist; without A, B can't exist. Shop owners often say \"customers are our lifeblood\"; in Japanese that's: <span class='gd-jp'>お客様あっての商売だ。</span>(No customers, no business.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Noun + あっての + noun</b>. A noun goes before it, and a noun must always follow.<br>「あって」 is the te-form of 「ある」 (to exist), and adding 「の」 gives \"the … that exists thanks to ～\".<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>お客様</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お客様あっての商売</span></span> (a business that exists thanks to customers)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>AあってのBだ</span>States a principle or a value: B is held up by A.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>選手はファンあっての存在だ。</span>(Athletes exist because of their fans.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>命あっての物種だ。無理はするな。</span>(Life comes first; everything else depends on it. Don't push yourself too hard.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜あってのことだ</span>The following noun becomes 「こと」, meaning \"this is all thanks to ～\". It's very common in award speeches and thank-yous.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>合格できたのは、先生のご指導あってのことです。</span>(I passed only thanks to my teacher's guidance.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皆さんの応援あっての優勝です。</span>(This championship exists thanks to everyone's support.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>あってこその</span>Adding 「こそ」 in the middle makes it stronger: <span class='gd-jp'>家族あってこその私です。</span>(It's precisely because of my family that I am who I am.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・A noun (or こと) must always follow. You can't say 「お客様あっての売れる」; say <span class='gd-jp'>お客様あっての商売</span>.<br>・The form is fixed as 「あっての」. Don't write 「あるの」.<br>・It isn't a plain \"because\". For \"it was canceled because of rain\", you can't say 「雨あっての中止」; say <span class='gd-jp'>雨で中止になった</span>. A must be something <b>essential and indispensable</b> to B, usually something positive and important such as customers, health, support, or effort.<br>・The tone is fairly formal and a bit heartfelt, used mostly in speeches, interviews, and writing. With friends, just say <span class='gd-jp'>お客さんがいるから</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same idea can be said positively or negatively:<br><b>あっての</b> = the positive \"B exists because of A\", followed by a noun: <span class='gd-jp'>お客様あっての商売だ。</span>(No customers, no business.)<br><b>なくしては</b> = the negative \"without A, B is impossible\", followed by a negative: <span class='gd-jp'>お客様なくしては商売は成り立たない。</span>(Without customers, the business can't survive.)<br><b>ばこそ</b> = \"precisely because ～\", giving the reason for doing something, attached to the ば form: <span class='gd-jp'>お客様のことを思えばこそ、品質にこだわるのだ。</span>(It's precisely because we care about our customers that we're particular about quality.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: for \"B exists thanks to A\", use あっての with a noun after it; for \"it can't be done without A\", use なくしては with a negative after it; for \"precisely because I think or act this way\", use ばこそ.</div>"});
